window.SAME_FOREST_SPREADS = [{"caption":"the cloud he quit fighting comes apart, and the loose ends turn out to be reins.","date":"2026-09-22","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260922bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260922bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><circle cx='150' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='145' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='155' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 146 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 208 L 150 240'/><path d='M 120 252 C 120 238 180 238 180 252 C 180 260 120 260 120 252'/><path d='M 150 216 L 130 244 M 150 216 L 170 244'/><path d='M 90 140 c -44 0 -44 -48 -4 -44 c 8 -36 66 -36 76 -6 c 40 -12 50 26 20 34 c -10 16 -42 16 -50 8 c -8 12 -34 12 -42 8 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.6' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 70 120 c 14 -14 24 6 36 -6 c 12 -12 20 8 34 -4 c 12 -10 20 8 30 -2 M 64 130 c 16 -12 26 6 40 -4 c 12 -10 22 8 36 -2 c 12 -8 20 6 30 0 M 80 108 c 12 -10 22 4 34 -4 c 10 -8 20 6 30 -2' stroke-width='2' opacity='.85'/><path d='M 176 114 q 10 -4 18 4 M 180 128 q 8 -6 16 2' stroke-width='2' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 190 128 C 260 160 340 170 448 208 M 192 114 C 262 148 350 160 452 200' stroke-width='2' opacity='.8'/><ellipse cx='410' cy='222' rx='34' ry='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 438 212 L 458 184'/><ellipse cx='464' cy='180' rx='13' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='470' cy='178' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 456 174 l -2 -9 M 464 173 l 1 -9'/><path d='M 388 232 L 388 256'/><path d='M 400 232 L 400 256'/><path d='M 426 232 L 426 256'/><path d='M 438 232 L 438 256'/><path d='M 376 218 q -14 10 -10 30'/><path d='M 520 240 L 520 204 L 610 204 L 610 240 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='538' cy='242' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='592' cy='242' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 520 226 L 474 220'/><circle cx='590' cy='176' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='583' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='593' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 584 182 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 590 190 L 590 211.92000000000002'/><path d='M 590 211.92000000000002 L 578 240 M 590 211.92000000000002 L 602 240'/><path d='M 590.0 198 L 574.0 220'/><path d='M 590.0 198 L 620.0 184'/><path d='M 616 170 q 26 20 0 52' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 616 170 L 616 222' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/g><g transform='translate(486,198.3) scale(0.95)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"mingyur rinpoche, the joy of living · read chapter 1, he tells it himself","sourceRef":"rinpoche","text":"sit down.\n[[rinpoche|mingyur rinpoche]] had [[panic|panic attacks]] as a kid. real ones, the kind where you think you're dying. at thirteen he went into a [[retreat|three year retreat]] and for the first year they got worse.\nthen he quit fighting them. he let the panic in and watched it. it fell apart in a few days. it had needed the fight to stay alive."},"mode":"disagree","question":"is this the kind of sitting that dissolves the thing, or the kind that keeps it company?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side, along with the great-uncle who raised him and the teacher who taught him to shoot.\nhe asks krishna to drive out between the armies so he can look. he looks, his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down in the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice except krishna, for about three verses, and then even he moves on to something better. the sitting down is real. so is the getting up. the book needs both.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1 for sitting down, chapter 2 for the rest."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic. eighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts.\nhindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts talking, and the first thing he says is not gentle. read the first ten verses before the famous ones and you'll see the tone the rest of the book is correcting for.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2, verses 1 through 10, then keep going. any translation you can hold."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't.\nbut before any of that, right at the top of chapter two, he's just a friend watching another friend fall apart at the worst possible moment, and he says the thing friends say. get up. this isn't you.\nthe kindness comes after. the first thing out of his mouth is a shove.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita 2:1-10 for the shove. the rest of chapter 2 for what comes after it."},"panic":{"lines":"he describes them starting around age seven or eight. his heart would race, he couldn't breathe, he was sure he was about to die. they came most in crowds and when he had to sit in the front at ceremonies, which as a recognized young teacher was constantly.\nhis father taught him to meditate at nine, partly for this. it helped and it didn't. the panic was still there, waiting.\nwhat finally worked is the part he tells over and over, because it's the whole teaching: he stopped treating the panic as an enemy and started treating it as a guest. he looked straight at it. and it turned out to be made of nothing in particular, once it wasn't being pushed against.","name":"the panic attacks","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1, and the introduction. he tells it plainly."},"retreat":{"lines":"in tibetan buddhism there's a traditional intensive: three years, three months, three days, in a closed retreat with a small group, doing nothing but practice. it's how the tradition trains its teachers.\nhe went in at thirteen, the youngest in his group, at sherab ling monastery in india. he says the first year was the worst of his life. the panic had nowhere to go and neither did he.\nsomewhere in that year he made the choice to stop fighting, and by his account the attacks were gone within days and never came back.\nhe was so young when he came out that they sent him back in as the retreat master for the next group. he was seventeen.","name":"the three year retreat","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1. he keeps the retreat details short; the turn is the point."},"rinpoche":{"lines":"a tibetan buddhist teacher, born in nepal in 1975, the son of one of the great meditation masters of the last century, tulku urgyen rinpoche. rinpoche is a title, it means precious one. he was recognized as a reincarnated teacher as a small boy.\nnone of that stopped the panic attacks. he's open about it, which is rare for someone in his position, and it's why people trust him.\nhe's been wired up in neuroscience labs more than almost any meditator alive, which is where the newspapers got the line about the happiest man in the world. he laughs at that.\nin 2011 he walked out of his monastery with no money and no plan and wandered india as a beggar for four and a half years. he nearly died of food poisoning in the first few weeks. he came back and wrote about it. he runs a network of meditation centers called tergar.","name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","where":"the joy of living, 2007, for the panic and the retreat. in love with the world, 2019, for the wandering years."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:2-3 · read the first ten verses of chapter 2, before he gets kind","sourceRef":"gita","text":"get up.\nthat's what [[krishna]] says to [[arjuna]] the second he opens his mouth. before the philosophy, before any of it. don't be a coward, it doesn't suit you, get up.\nsitting with it is not the same as sitting it out. sometimes the thing you're watching so carefully is just you, not moving."},"theme":"sit down, get up","id":"2026-09-22"},{"caption":"the pieces of the pot are the stones of the path.","date":"2026-09-23","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260923bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260923bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><circle cx='150' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='147' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='157' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 148 180 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 188 L 150 221.44'/><path d='M 150 221.44 L 138 256 M 150 221.44 L 162 256'/><path d='M 150.0 196 L 140.0 218'/><path d='M 150.0 196 L 172.0 210'/><path d='M 194 180 C 188 206 196 222 214 224 C 232 222 240 206 234 180 L 224 180 L 218 190 L 212 180 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><ellipse cx='214' cy='180' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 216 196 l -5 10 l 7 8 l -4 10' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 100 256 l 9 -7 l 8 7 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 126 256 l 9 -7 l 8 7 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 150 256 l 9 -7 l 8 7 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 178 256 l 9 -7 l 8 7 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><ellipse cx='220' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='260' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='380' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='420' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='460' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='500' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='540' cy='258' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='632' cy='232' r='24' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='632' cy='232' r='4'/><circle cx='676' cy='248' r='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 616 206 L 656 206 L 664 232 L 684 232' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 616 206 L 612 170' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='628' cy='150' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='621' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='631' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 622 156 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 628 164 L 622 206 L 652 212 L 652 240'/><path d='M 624 180 L 644 196 M 624 180 L 634 198'/><\/g><g transform='translate(642,189.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"richard rohr, falling upward · read the introduction, it's the whole argument","sourceRef":"falling","text":"you don't grow from doing it right.\n[[rohr]]'s whole book [[falling|falling upward]] is one point. the first half of your life builds the container. the second half only starts when something breaks it.\nnobody signs up for the breaking. you get thrown."},"mode":"agree","question":"what broke that you're still trying to glue back?","refs":{"falling":{"lines":"rohr's argument is that there are two halves to a life, and they are not the same job.\nthe first half is building the container. identity, career, family, beliefs, a sense of being somebody. you're supposed to do this, and you're supposed to take it seriously.\nthe second half is what the container was for. and here's the catch he's built the book around: almost nobody moves into the second half on purpose. you get moved. failure, loss, illness, a marriage ending, something you can't fix. he calls it necessary suffering.\nthe title is the point. the way up is the way down. you fall into the second half of your life. you don't climb.","name":"falling upward","where":"falling upward: a spirituality for the two halves of life. chapter 5, on necessary suffering, if you only read one."},"ramdass":{"lines":"born richard alpert, a harvard psychology professor who got fired in 1963 alongside timothy leary for giving psychedelics to students.\nhe went to india in 1967 looking for somebody who knew what the drugs had shown him, and found an old man in a blanket named neem karoli baba, who read his mind and told him things nobody could have known. he never really came back. the old man named him ram dass, servant of god.\nhe wrote be here now in 1971, a book that looks like a hippie art project and is still in print because it works. he spent the next fifty years talking about love, service, and dying, and he was funny the whole time.\nhe died in maui in december 2019, on purpose, at home, surrounded, with a lot of people watching him do it well.","name":"ram dass","where":"be here now for the beginning. still here for after the stroke. his talks are collected by the love serve remember foundation."},"rohr":{"lines":"a franciscan friar, which means a catholic priest in the order started by francis of assisi, the one who talked to birds and gave everything away.\nrohr has spent fifty years in new mexico running a place called the center for action and contemplation, teaching a version of christianity that's more interested in what happens inside you than in who's in and who's out.\nhe writes a lot. falling upward, the universal christ, everything belongs. he's the one who gave a lot of people permission to stay in the tradition without swallowing all of it.\nhe doesn't get everybody all the way there. but he gets you out of the parking lot.","name":"richard rohr","where":"falling upward, 2011. the introduction and the first two chapters carry the whole idea."},"stroke":{"lines":"february 1997. he was sixty five, lying in bed thinking about how he'd write about aging, and a blood vessel in his brain let go. he was found on the floor. paralyzed on the right side, most of his speech gone.\nhe said later that in the moment he looked for the thing he'd taught for thirty years, the witness, the calm center, and it wasn't there. he was just a scared old man on a floor. that was the first lesson.\nhe spent years learning to talk again. slowly. long pauses. his talks after the stroke are half silence, and people say those were his best ones, because he was no longer performing.\nfierce grace was the phrase he landed on. grace that doesn't ask, doesn't soften, and doesn't leave.","name":"the stroke","where":"still here, 2000, is the book he was writing when it happened, finished afterward. fierce grace, 2001, is the documentary. watch the film."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"ram dass, still here · read the book, then find the film fierce grace and watch him try to talk","sourceRef":"stroke","text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] had a [[stroke|stroke]] in 1997.\nit took his right side and most of his words. he called it fierce grace. not at first. at first he called it what you'd call it.\nbut the stroke did what forty years of meditating hadn't. it took away the guy who was good at talking about god, and left the one who had to sit there."},"theme":"what the break is for","id":"2026-09-23"},{"caption":"the river won't stay in the frame. the bush won't burn up.","date":"2026-09-24","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260924bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260924bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 236 C 60 226 120 246 180 236 S 300 226 400 236' stroke-width='2.4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 0 250 C 70 242 130 258 200 250 S 320 244 400 250' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 400 250 C 460 252 520 250 800 251'/><circle cx='150' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='148' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='158' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 149 180 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 188 L 150 221.44'/><path d='M 150 221.44 L 138 256 M 150 221.44 L 162 256'/><path d='M 150.0 196 L 176.0 192'/><path d='M 150.0 196 L 184.0 208'/><path d='M 184 150 L 250 150 L 250 204 L 184 204 Z' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 190 156 L 244 156 L 244 198 L 190 198 Z' stroke-width='1.4' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 540 250 C 520 250 514 228 530 220 C 526 200 552 192 566 206 C 584 196 600 214 588 228 C 600 240 586 254 568 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 528 214 q -6 -18 8 -22 q 2 -16 18 -12 q 14 -14 22 2 q 16 -2 10 16' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.8'/><path d='M 548 196 q 2 -14 14 -10 q 10 -8 14 6' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='630' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='622' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='632' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='627' cy='180' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 630 188 L 630 221.44'/><path d='M 630 221.44 L 618 256 M 630 221.44 L 642 256'/><path d='M 630.0 196 L 638.0 212'/><path d='M 630.0 196 L 622.0 212'/><ellipse cx='668' cy='254' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='692' cy='254' rx='12' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(552,201.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 1 · read 1 through 4 in a row, any translation","sourceRef":"tao","text":"the second you name it, you missed it.\nfirst line of the [[tao|tao te ching]], about twenty five hundred years old: the way you can talk about isn't the way.\n[[laotzu|lao tzu]] opens his whole book by telling you the book can't do it. then he writes eighty more chapters anyway. that's the honest version of every scripture."},"mode":"agree","question":"what have you named that never told you its name?","refs":{"bush":{"lines":"moses is out with his father in law's sheep on a mountain called horeb, and a bush is on fire and not burning up. he goes over to look, which is the whole thing. he could have kept walking.\nthe voice tells him to take his sandals off, the ground is holy. then it tells him it has seen the misery of his people in egypt and it's sending him.\nwhen he asks for a name, the answer in hebrew is ehyeh asher ehyeh. i am who i am, or i will be what i will be. the verb is unfinished on purpose.\nfrom that answer the jewish tradition gets the four letter name it doesn't say out loud, and christians and muslims inherit the same refusal to pin the thing down. every argument about god's name starts at this bush.","name":"the burning bush","where":"exodus 3, the first fifteen verses, slowly."},"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible. in hebrew it's called shemot, which means names, after its first line.\nit's the story of a people enslaved in egypt, the plagues, the escape through the sea, the mountain, the ten commandments, and a lot of instructions about building a tent.\nchapter 3 is where the whole thing turns. an old shepherd, a bush, a name that isn't one. everything after runs on that conversation.","name":"exodus","where":"exodus, chapter 3 today. chapters 1 through 20 for the story."},"laotzu":{"lines":"the name just means old master. nobody is sure he existed as one person.\nthe story goes that he kept the archives at the royal court, got tired of the whole thing, and rode west on a water buffalo to leave the country. the gatekeeper at the border recognized him and wouldn't let him through until he wrote down what he knew.\nhe wrote five thousand characters, handed them over, and rode off. nobody saw him again. that's the book.\nwhether it happened or not, it's the right story for a book that begins by saying words won't get you there.","name":"lao tzu","where":"sima qian's records of the grand historian, written around 100 bc, has the oldest version of the story. it's already a legend by then."},"moses":{"lines":"a hebrew baby raised in the egyptian palace after his mother floated him down the nile to save him from a killing order. he grew up a prince, killed an egyptian he saw beating a slave, and ran.\nhe spent forty years in the desert as a shepherd, married, raising kids, done with all of it. he was eighty when the bush caught fire.\nhe argued with the voice for two chapters. who am i. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk right. send somebody else. every excuse got answered and he went.\nhe led the slaves out of egypt, spent forty more years in the desert with them, and died on a mountain looking at the land he never got to enter. the five books that open the bible are traditionally his.","name":"moses","where":"exodus, chapters 2 through 4 for the beginning. deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"tao":{"lines":"eighty one short chapters, about five thousand chinese characters in all. you can read the whole thing in an hour and spend the rest of your life on it.\ntao means the way. te means the power or virtue that comes from being lined up with it. ching means classic. so, the classic of the way and its power.\nit's the root text of taoism and it leaks into everything else in china, including zen, which is what you get when buddhism moves in next door to this book.\nit doesn't tell you what the way is. it says it can't. then it spends eighty chapters pointing: water, valleys, empty bowls, the space inside a wheel. the useful part is always the part that isn't there.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4 to start. stephen mitchell's version reads easiest, ursula le guin's is the most honest about being a guess."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 3:14 · read all of chapter 3, and notice what moses tries to get out of","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"[[moses]] asked for a name.\nhe's barefoot in front of a [[bush|bush that's on fire and not burning up]], and he asks, who do i say sent me.\nthe answer is i am who i am. that's not a name. that's a refusal to be one.\nwe've spent three thousand years painting a face on it anyway."},"theme":"the name it never gave","id":"2026-09-24"},{"caption":"the strings of the lute are the lanes he runs in.","date":"2026-09-25","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260925bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260925bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 246 L 400 246' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 0 262 L 400 262' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 0 278 L 400 278' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 0 256 L 800 256' stroke-width='0.1' opacity='0'/><path d='M 40 256 L 40 240 L 66 240 L 72 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='120' cy='250' r='3' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='104' cy='253' r='4' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='90' cy='256' r='5' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='200' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='199' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='209' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='204' cy='180' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 200 188 L 190 221.44'/><path d='M 190 221.44 L 156 256 M 190 221.44 L 224 256'/><path d='M 197.5 196 L 167.5 190'/><path d='M 197.5 196 L 231.5 204'/><path d='M 400 246 C 460 246 520 178 574 178' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 400 262 C 460 262 520 186 574 186' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 400 278 C 460 278 520 194 574 194' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='600' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='593' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='603' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 594 198 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 600 208 L 600 240'/><path d='M 570 252 C 570 238 630 238 630 252 C 630 260 570 260 570 252'/><ellipse cx='580' cy='196' rx='22' ry='28' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 580 168 L 612 128' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 606 134 l 10 -6 M 610 140 l 10 -6' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='580' cy='200' r='5'/><path d='M 600 194 L 578 206 M 600 194 L 616 176'/><\/g><g transform='translate(44,215.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"1 corinthians 9:24-27 · read the whole chapter, he's answering a complaint","sourceRef":"corinthians","text":"run like you mean it.\n[[paul]] told the [[corinthians]] every runner runs but only one gets the prize, so run like that one.\nthen he says he beats his body and makes it his slave. that's the actual language. the man was not into balance."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which string are you tuning, and which one are you just yanking on?","refs":{"buddha":{"lines":"siddhartha gautama, a prince from a small kingdom on the edge of what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago. raised behind palace walls, kept from seeing sickness, old age, or death. when he finally saw them, he left. wife, kid, everything, at twenty nine.\nfor six years he did the hardest practices he could find, starving himself down to skin over bone. it didn't work. he ate a meal, sat down under a tree, and woke up. that's what buddha means, the one who woke up.\nthe first thing he taught after that was the middle way. not the palace, not the starving. the lute string, before he ever said it to sona.\nhe taught for forty five years and died at eighty, lying between two trees, telling his monks to work out their own freedom.","name":"the buddha","where":"the pali canon, the oldest collection of his talks. for his own story, the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26."},"corinthians":{"lines":"corinth was a port city in greece, rich, rough, and famous for it. paul started a church there around the year 50 and stayed about a year and a half.\nafter he left, it fell apart in every direction. factions, lawsuits between members, a man sleeping with his stepmother, rich people eating the shared meal before the poor arrived, arguments about who had the better spiritual gifts.\nfirst corinthians is paul's answer to all of it. it's also where the famous love chapter lives, chapter 13, which people read at weddings without knowing it was written to people who couldn't stand each other.\nchapter 9 is him defending himself against members who questioned whether he was a real apostle. the runner line comes at the end of that argument.","name":"the corinthians","where":"1 corinthians, all sixteen chapters. read 9 for today, then 13 to see what he was actually after."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe used athletes as a picture of the spiritual life more than once. corinth hosted the isthmian games, second only to the olympics, so his readers had seen the runners.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. 1 corinthians 9 for the runners. 2 corinthians 11 for what it cost him."},"sona":{"lines":"sona kolivisa, a rich young man from a merchant family who became a monk under the buddha. before that he'd been a musician, a lute player.\nhe threw himself at the practice the way he'd probably thrown himself at everything. he did walking meditation until the soles of his feet split open and the path was bloody, and he still wasn't free. he started thinking about quitting and going back to his money.\nthe buddha came to find him. instead of a lecture he asked about the lute. that conversation is the whole sutta.\nsona stayed, and the tradition says he got there. the lute simile is one of the most quoted things the buddha ever said about effort.","name":"sona","where":"the sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55. it also shows up in the vinaya, the monks' rulebook, mahavagga 5."},"sutta":{"lines":"a sutta is one talk, or one conversation, that the buddha or one of his students gave. thousands of them were memorized and chanted for a few hundred years before anybody wrote them down.\nthe collection is the pali canon, named for the language it's in. it's sorted into groups. the anguttara nikaya is the numbered collection, where talks are grouped by how many things they list. the sona sutta lives in the book of sixes, which is why it's 6.55.\nnone of it is scripture in the sense of a rulebook handed down from the sky. it's a record of a man talking to people about how to suffer less. some of it is dry. some of it, like sona and his lute, is a story you can hold.","name":"what a sutta is","where":"anguttara nikaya, the book of sixes, sutta 55. any translation of the pali canon."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55 · read the whole conversation, it's short","sourceRef":"sutta","text":"loosen the string.\na monk named [[sona]] walked until his feet bled and still wasn't getting anywhere.\n[[buddha|the buddha]] didn't tell him to try harder. he asked about the lute sona used to play. too tight and the string snaps. too loose and there's no note.\ntune it. then play."},"theme":"how hard to push","id":"2026-09-25"},{"caption":"the light in the cell and the light at the grave are the same light.","date":"2026-09-26","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260926bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260926bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><path d='M 262 110 L 290 86 L 120 256 L 64 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 60 256 L 60 62 L 310 62 L 310 256' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 262 86 L 290 86 L 290 110 L 262 110 Z M 276 86 L 276 110 M 262 98 L 290 98' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='180' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='175' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='185' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 176 178 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 180 188 L 180 221.44'/><path d='M 180 221.44 L 168 256 M 180 221.44 L 192 256'/><path d='M 180.0 196 L 158.0 166'/><path d='M 180.0 196 L 202.0 166'/><path d='M 520 62 L 700 62 L 760 256 L 470 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 540 256 C 567.0 227.4 603.0 227.4 630 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 585 236 L 585 206 M 573 216 L 597 216' stroke-width='2.6'/><circle cx='520' cy='190' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='513' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='523' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 514 196 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 520 204 L 520 227.2'/><path d='M 520 227.2 L 508 256 M 520 227.2 L 532 256'/><path d='M 520.0 212 L 504.0 242'/><path d='M 520.0 212 L 536.0 242'/><circle cx='556' cy='184' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='549' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='559' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 550 190 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 556 198 L 556 225.04'/><path d='M 556 225.04 L 544 256 M 556 225.04 L 568 256'/><path d='M 556.0 206 L 540.0 236'/><path d='M 556.0 206 L 572.0 236'/><circle cx='640' cy='186' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='633' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='643' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 634 192 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 640 200 L 640 225.76'/><path d='M 640 225.76 L 628 256 M 640 225.76 L 652 256'/><path d='M 640.0 208 L 624.0 238'/><path d='M 640.0 208 L 656.0 238'/><circle cx='676' cy='190' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='669' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='679' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 670 196 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 676 204 L 676 227.2'/><path d='M 676 227.2 L 664 256 M 676 227.2 L 688 256'/><path d='M 676.0 212 L 660.0 242'/><path d='M 676.0 212 L 692.0 242'/><circle cx='740' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='731' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='741' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 732 180 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 740 188 L 740 221.44'/><path d='M 740 221.44 L 728 256 M 740 221.44 L 752 256'/><path d='M 740.0 196 L 724.0 226'/><path d='M 740.0 196 L 756.0 226'/><\/g><g transform='translate(68,36.4) scale(0.95)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"philippians 4:4 · read the whole letter, it's short, and notice where he's writing from","sourceRef":"chains","text":"rejoice. and again i say it.\n[[paul]] wrote that [[chains|in chains]]. not from a beach. he said it twice in one sentence like he knew you'd argue.\nhappiness isn't a report on your circumstances. it's a decision you make with your circumstances still standing there."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one do you reach for because it's true, and which one because it's easier?","refs":{"chains":{"lines":"philippians is one of the prison letters. in chapter 1 he says his chains have become known through the whole palace guard, and that being locked up has actually spread the message further, so he's fine with it.\nscholars argue about which prison, rome or ephesus or caesarea. doesn't matter for our purposes. the man who wrote rejoice always was writing it from a cell, to people who were worried about him, and he was the one doing the comforting.\nsame letter, chapter 4, a few lines after the one we used: he says he has learned to be content with plenty and with nothing, well fed and hungry. he wasn't guessing.","name":"in chains","where":"philippians, all four chapters. it takes about fifteen minutes."},"ecclesiastes":{"lines":"the writer calls himself qoheleth, the teacher, or the one who gathers people. son of david, king in jerusalem, which is why tradition says solomon.\nhe tried everything. wisdom, pleasure, wine, building projects, gardens, wealth, women, work. and he calls all of it hevel, a hebrew word that means vapor or breath. something you can see for a second and can't hold. most bibles translate it vanity, which is weaker.\nthe book almost didn't make it in. the rabbis argued over it for a long time. what saved it was the last few verses, fear god and keep the commandments, and plenty of people think somebody nervous added those.\nread it either way. it's the most honest book in there about how little of your life you actually control.","name":"ecclesiastes","where":"ecclesiastes, twelve chapters. start at 1 and don't skip to the ending."},"mourning":{"lines":"chapter 7 opens with a run of better-than sayings. a good name is better than fine oil. the day of death is better than the day of birth. and then this one: better to go to the house of mourning than the house of feasting, because death is where everyone ends up and the living should take it to heart.\nhe keeps going. sorrow is better than laughter. a sad face is good for the heart. the heart of the wise is in the house of mourning, the heart of fools in the house of pleasure.\nthe house of mourning is a funeral, a shiva, the week after. the house of feasting is the party. he isn't against parties. he's against forgetting.","name":"the house of mourning","where":"ecclesiastes 7, the first four verses, then keep reading."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead outside a town, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe's also the one who stood up in athens and quoted their own greek poets back at them, in acts 17, to tell them the unknown god they'd built an altar to had a name. old texts talking to each other. he did it first.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. acts 17 for athens. 2 corinthians 11 for the list of what it cost him, in his own words."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"ecclesiastes 7:2-4 · read the whole chapter, it doesn't get easier","sourceRef":"mourning","text":"go to the funeral.\n[[ecclesiastes]] says it flat out. the [[mourning|house of mourning]] beats the house of feasting, and the wise keep their heart in the first one.\nnot because sadness is holy. because the party lets you forget you're going to die, and forgetting makes you stupid about what matters."},"theme":"the party or the funeral","id":"2026-09-26"},{"caption":"the road only goes as far as the fence. past that, nothing's been drawn yet.","date":"2026-09-27","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260927bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260927bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 400 256 C 437 253 475 259 525 256 S 612 254 650 256'/><path d='M 0 256 C 45 253 90 259 150 256 S 255 254 300 256'/><path d='M 0 246 L 300 246 M 0 266 L 300 266' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 300 246 L 300 266' stroke-dasharray='3 5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 400 246 L 640 246 M 400 266 L 640 266' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='120' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='119' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='129' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 120 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 120 208 L 120 240'/><path d='M 90 252 C 90 238 150 238 150 252 C 150 260 90 260 90 252'/><path d='M 120 216 L 100 244 M 120 216 L 140 244'/><path d='M 196 256 L 196 222 L 276 222 L 276 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='212' cy='258' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='260' cy='258' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 276 236 L 304 226' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 560 68 C 535.8 76.8 535.8 103.2 560 112 C 549.0 101.0 549.0 79.0 560 68 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='620' cy='70' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='700' cy='110' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='760' cy='60' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 650 256 L 650 200 M 710 256 L 710 200' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 640 216 L 720 216 M 640 238 L 720 238' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='676' cy='158' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='674' cy='156' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='684' cy='156' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 675 164 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 676 172 L 680 212'/><path d='M 680 212 L 664 256 M 680 212 L 716 226 L 716 256'/><path d='M 678 186 L 652 206 M 678 186 L 704 196'/><\/g><g transform='translate(650,175.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><g transform='translate(236,205.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:47 · read all of chapter 2, not the verse","sourceRef":"gita","text":"you don't get the ending.\n[[arjuna]] sat down in the chariot and wouldn't fight because he could already see how it goes. [[krishna]] didn't argue with the forecast. he told him the results were never his to begin with.\nyou get the work. that's the whole deal. the fruit goes somewhere you don't control."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you carrying that hasn't happened yet?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side. so is bhishma, the great-uncle who helped raise him, and drona, the man who taught him to shoot.\nin chapter one he asks krishna to drive the chariot out between the two armies so he can look. he looks. his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down on the floor of the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice. krishna just doesn't accept it as the end of the conversation. the other seventeen chapters are what gets said while arjuna is sitting there.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1, which sits inside the mahabharata. any translation. the old edwin arnold one is free everywhere."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic, roughly ten times the length of the iliad and the odyssey put together.\neighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts. hindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts answering. 2:47 is the verse we used: you have a right to your work, never to its fruits. read the whole chapter and you'll see he's not being gentle about it.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2. any translation you can hold in your hand."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher from a small town in galilee, about two thousand years ago. he spent roughly three years walking around telling stories, eating with the wrong people, and healing whoever showed up. the romans executed him.\nchristians say he got up again three days later and that he was god the whole time. you don't have to sign any of that to read him.\nfour short books tell his story: matthew, mark, luke, and john. they don't fully agree with each other on the details, which is part of the honesty of the thing.\nthis book reads him the way a mystic would. less rule-giver, more the guy pointing at the birds and saying look.","name":"jesus","where":"the gospels: matthew, mark, luke, john. start with mark, it's the shortest and the fastest."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't. krishna puts the friendly face back on and they keep talking.\nhe never once tells arjuna the fight will go well. he tells him whose the outcome is, and it isn't arjuna's. do the work, hand over the result. that's the line we used, and it's probably the most quoted line in the book.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapters 2 and 11 for the two faces of him."},"sermon":{"lines":"matthew 5, 6, and 7. jesus goes up a hill, sits down, and talks for three chapters straight.\nit opens with the beatitudes, blessed are the poor in spirit, the ones who mourn, the meek. then anger, lust, oaths, revenge, loving your enemies, giving in secret, the lord's prayer, money, worry, judging other people, and it ends with a house built on rock and one built on sand.\nthe worry part is the end of chapter 6. look at the birds, look at the lilies, they aren't working themselves to death and they're fine. then the line we used. people cut that line into posters. read straight through, it's a lot harder than the posters, because he's just told you to give away your coat and love the people who hit you.","name":"the sermon on the mount","where":"matthew, chapters 5 through 7. any bible, any translation, one sitting."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"matthew 6:34 · read the whole sermon, chapters 5 through 7","sourceRef":"sermon","text":"you don't get tomorrow either.\n[[jesus]] said [[sermon|don't worry about it]], tomorrow has its own problems, today has enough.\npeople read that as comfort. it isn't. it's a fence. you're not allowed past midnight, and you keep climbing it at 2am anyway."},"theme":"what's yours to carry","id":"2026-09-27"},{"caption":"what one of them shouts is the smoke the other lets burn off.","date":"2026-09-28","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260928bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260928bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 60 246 120 266 180 256 S 300 246 400 256 L 800 256'/><path d='M 110 256 L 110 160' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 30 166 C 40 110 180 110 190 166 C 170 150 50 150 30 166 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 40 158 c -10 30 -6 50 -4 54' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 58 158 c -7 30 -4 50 -2 64' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 76 158 c -4 30 -2 50 0 74' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 94 158 c -1 30 0 50 2 84' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 128 158 c 2 30 2 50 4 74' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 148 158 c 5 30 4 50 6 64' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 166 158 c 8 30 6 50 8 54' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 184 158 c 11 30 8 50 10 44' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 140 162 L 140 206 L 168 206 Z' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 147 206 L 147 172.5' stroke-width='1.2'/><path d='M 154 206 L 154 183.0' stroke-width='1.2'/><path d='M 161 206 L 161 193.5' stroke-width='1.2'/><circle cx='200' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='198' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='208' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='203' cy='180' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 200 188 L 200 221.44'/><path d='M 200 221.44 L 188 256 M 200 221.44 L 212 256'/><path d='M 200.0 196 L 178.0 166'/><path d='M 200.0 196 L 222.0 166'/><path d='M 214 176 C 250 160 270 200 300 180 S 350 150 390 168 C 420 180 440 150 470 160 C 510 176 520 210 538 196' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 522.0 256 L 558.0 248.8 M 558.0 256 L 522.0 248.8'/><path d='M 529.2 248.8 C 522.0 229.0 538.2 225.4 540 209.2 C 545.4 223.6 558.0 230.8 550.8 248.8 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 538 196 C 526 180 552 176 540 160 C 530 146 552 140 544 126' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='610' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='612' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 603 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 610 208 L 610 240'/><path d='M 580 252 C 580 238 640 238 640 252 C 640 260 580 260 580 252'/><path d='M 610 216 L 590 244 M 610 216 L 630 244'/><\/g><g transform='translate(150,125.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"psalm 137 · read the whole thing, then 88, the one that ends in the dark","sourceRef":"psalm137","text":"say the ugly thing.\nthere's a [[psalms|psalm]] that ends with a man in [[exile]] hoping somebody smashes his enemy's babies on the rocks. [[psalm137|psalm 137]]. they left it in the book.\nyou're allowed to say what you actually feel to god. apparently he's heard worse.\nwhat you swallow doesn't leave. it just moves in."},"mode":"disagree","question":"what are you swallowing right now, and what's it feeding?","refs":{"bodhicaryavatara":{"lines":"shantideva's book. ten chapters, written in verse in the eighth century, on how to become a bodhisattva, which is someone who's waking up not for their own sake but for everybody else's.\nchapter 6 is on patience and it opens with the line about a single moment of anger burning up a thousand eons of good deeds. then it works through every excuse you've got for staying mad, and takes each one apart.\nthe famous image from the book is in chapter 5: you'll never find enough leather to cover the whole earth, but with leather on the soles of your feet it's as if you had. you don't fix the world. you fix where you stand on it.\nfor today, the book is the strongest case in any tradition for not saying the thing. not swallowing it either. just not feeding it.","name":"the way of the bodhisattva","where":"chapter 6, patience. read the first ten verses, then the stretch near the end of the chapter where the enemy becomes the teacher."},"exile":{"lines":"in 586 bc the babylonian army under nebuchadnezzar took jerusalem, burned the temple, and marched the leadership of judah hundreds of miles east to babylon. that's the exile. it lasted about fifty years.\nit was the worst thing that had ever happened to those people. the temple was where god lived, as far as they knew. now it was rubble and they were singing in a foreign country.\na lot of the hebrew bible got its final shape in that stretch, because people were trying to figure out how to be who they were with nothing left. then the persian king cyrus took babylon and let them go home.\npsalm 137 was written inside that wound. the anger in it isn't a thought experiment. it's people who watched their city die.","name":"the exile","where":"2 kings 24 and 25 tell the fall. lamentations is the grief. psalm 137 is the anger. ezra 1 is the trip home."},"patience":{"lines":"one of the six perfections in mahayana buddhism, the qualities a bodhisattva practices: generosity, discipline, patience, effort, meditation, wisdom. the sanskrit word is kshanti.\nit doesn't mean gritting your teeth and waiting. it means not getting knocked off center by what hurts you, and not making it worse. shantideva says there's no austerity like it, no practice that does more.\nthe trick in his version is that you can't practice it in a nice room. it only exists in the presence of somebody who's pissing you off. that's why he calls the enemy a treasure found lying in your house.\nfor today: the right page isn't telling you to be nice. it's telling you the person you want to say the ugly thing to is your practice, and the fire goes out on its own if you stop feeding it.","name":"patience (kshanti)","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6, all of it."},"psalm137":{"lines":"a song written by people who'd just lost everything. jerusalem burned, the temple gone, and them marched off to babylon. it opens with the line most people know: by the rivers of babylon we sat down and wept. their captors ask them to sing one of the old songs for fun, and they hang their harps in the trees and refuse.\nthen the last two verses turn. the singer asks god to remember what was done to his city, and says whoever grabs babylon's babies and smashes them on the rocks will be happy. that's the end of the psalm. no apology, no take-back.\nnobody edited it out. it's been in the prayer book of jews and christians for twenty five hundred years, and monks still chant it. scholars call these the imprecatory psalms, the cursing ones, and there are more of them than you'd think.\nit matters today because the book makes room for rage. not tidy rage. the real kind. and it puts that rage in front of god instead of behind your teeth.","name":"psalm 137","where":"the book of psalms, psalm 137. then read psalm 88, the only one that ends without a way out."},"psalms":{"lines":"a hundred and fifty songs and poems sitting in the middle of the bible. they were the hymnbook of ancient israel, and later the prayer book of jesus, of paul, of the monks, of pretty much everyone since.\na lot of them are credited to king david, though not all, and plenty are older or later. they were meant to be sung, not read quietly.\nabout a third of them are laments. complaints, really. why have you left me, how long, where are you. the praise ones get quoted on coffee mugs. the lament ones are where the honesty lives.\nfor today: the psalms are proof that saying the worst thing out loud to god was normal, expected, and written down for everyone to sing.","name":"the psalms","where":"the book of psalms, dead center of the bible. try 23, 51, 88, and 137 back to back to get the range."},"shantideva":{"lines":"a buddhist monk in india in the eighth century, living at nalanda, the great monastery and university of the day. the story goes the other monks thought he was useless. he seemed to do nothing but eat, sleep, and go to the toilet, and they gave him a nickname that meant exactly that.\nso they set him up. they asked him to recite scripture in front of everyone, figuring he'd have nothing. he asked if they wanted something old or something new. they said new. he sat down and recited the whole of the way of the bodhisattva from memory, and the legend says partway through the wisdom chapter he rose into the air and was gone.\nwhether that happened or not, the book is real and it's the one tibetan teachers reach for first when they want to talk about how to actually live this stuff. the dalai lama teaches from it constantly.\nhis chapter on patience is the hardest thing in it. he doesn't argue anger is bad. he argues it's expensive, and that the people who hurt you are the only ones who ever give you a place to practice.","name":"shantideva","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, also called the bodhicaryavatara. the padmakara translation reads clean. the dalai lama's book healing anger is a commentary on chapter 6."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"shantideva, the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6 · read the whole chapter, it's on patience","sourceRef":"bodhicaryavatara","text":"don't feed it.\n[[shantideva]] was a monk in india in the eighth century, and he said one flash of anger burns up a thousand lifetimes of good. everything you built. one match.\nhe also said the guy who wronged you is the only one who ever hands you a chance at [[patience]]. so quit trying to get rid of him.\nanger needs a mouth to stay alive. close it."},"theme":"say it or starve it","id":"2026-09-28"},{"caption":"what one of them left alone is growing. what the other one kept is rotting.","date":"2026-09-29","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260929bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260929bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><circle cx='150' cy='186' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='147' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='157' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 148 192 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 200 L 146 234 L 172 256 L 134 256'/><path d='M 149 208 L 183 208'/><path d='M 149 208 L 175 194'/><path d='M 73.2 240 C 75.6 259.2 104.4 259.2 106.8 240 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='84' cy='246' r='3' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='96' cy='246' r='3' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 215 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 224 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 233 256 l 4 -16'/><circle cx='224' cy='236' r='3.5' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 253 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 262 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 271 256 l 4 -16'/><circle cx='262' cy='236' r='3.5' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 291 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 300 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 309 256 l 4 -16'/><circle cx='300' cy='236' r='3.5' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 391 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 400 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 409 256 l 4 -16'/><ellipse cx='416' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='452' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='488' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='524' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='560' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='572' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='584' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='596' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='608' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='620' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='632' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='644' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='656' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='668' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='680' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='692' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='704' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='716' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='728' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='740' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='752' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='764' cy='247' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='776' cy='253' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='788' cy='250' rx='5' ry='2.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 470 256 A 30 30 0 0 1 530 256' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 500 214 l 0 -10 M 478 222 l -6 -8 M 522 222 l 6 -8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 690 256 L 730 170 L 770 256 Z M 730 170 L 730 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 718 256 L 730 222 L 742 256' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='620' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='617' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='627' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 618 180 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 620 188 L 620 221.44'/><path d='M 620 221.44 L 608 256 M 620 221.44 L 632 256'/><path d='M 620.0 196 L 608.0 220'/><path d='M 620.0 196 L 636.0 208'/><path d='M 612 180 L 612 206 L 636 206 L 636 180 Z M 608 180 L 640 180' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 618 174 c -8 -10 8 -20 0 -30 c -8 -10 8 -18 0 -26' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 630 174 c -8 -10 8 -20 0 -30 c -8 -10 8 -18 0 -26' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><\/g><g transform='translate(730,145.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"native","source":"robin wall kimmerer, braiding sweetgrass · read the honorable harvest, then the gift of strawberries","sourceRef":"sweetgrass","text":"leave the first one.\n[[kimmerer|robin wall kimmerer]] is a botanist and a member of the [[potawatomi|citizen potawatomi nation]], and the rules she was raised on for taking anything from the ground start like this: ask first. never take the first one you see. it might be the only one.\nthen take only what you need, only what's given, and say thank you like you mean it. the [[harvest|honorable harvest]], she calls it. none of it is written down. you learn it by watching somebody do it.\nyou were taught to grab what you want before somebody else does. the plants have been here longer than you. you're a guest at their table."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you keeping that was only ever given for today?","refs":{"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible, and the story of the israelites getting out of egypt. the name is greek for the way out.\nthe shape: chapters 1 through 15 are the escape, plagues, the sea. chapter 16 is the manna, three days after the singing on the far shore, when the food ran out and everyone wished they were back in egypt. 17 is water from a rock. 19 through 24 is mount sinai and the ten commandments. the rest is instructions for building a tent for god to live in.\nit reads fast until the tent. the manna chapter is one of the ones that stays with people: the bread is free, the only rule is don't hoard it, and they hoard it.\nfor today, read the whole of 16 and notice the pattern. complaint, gift, rule, broken rule, and god still shows up the next morning.","name":"exodus","where":"the book of exodus, chapter 16. then 15:22 to 27 for the complaint right before it."},"harvest":{"lines":"kimmerer's name for the rules her elders taught about taking anything from the living world: plants, animals, water, wood. she says they aren't written down anywhere, they're passed by story and by example, and she writes them out in the chapter with that title.\nthe gist: know the ones who take care of you. introduce yourself. ask permission before you take, and abide by the answer. never take the first one. never take the last. take only what you need, and only what's given. leave some for others. use it with respect. never waste it. share. give thanks. give something back. sustain the ones who sustain you.\nshe puts it next to the way a market works, where the point of taking is owning, and there's no such thing as enough. she doesn't pretend she's above that either. she writes about walking into a store and trying to hold the same rules there.\nfor today, the first rule is the one that stings. never take the first one you see. you'll want to. that's the point.","name":"the honorable harvest","where":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapter called the honorable harvest."},"israelites":{"lines":"the people of the hebrew bible, descended in the story from a man named jacob, whose other name was israel. his twelve sons became twelve tribes. they went down to egypt during a famine, stayed, and over generations became slaves there.\nthe exodus is the story of getting out. after that they're the people at the foot of mount sinai, the people in the desert, and eventually the people of the land of israel. the jewish people today trace themselves back through them, and christians read their story as the front half of their own.\nin the desert chapters they are not heroic. they complain about water, about food, about moses, about having left egypt at all. the manna shows up in direct answer to one of those complaints. then they break the one rule about it. that's the whole book in miniature.\nfor today, they're you, the morning after you kept some.","name":"the israelites","where":"genesis 32 for jacob getting the name, exodus 1 for how they ended up enslaved, exodus 16 for the manna and the grumbling."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher in roman occupied galilee about two thousand years ago, executed by the romans, and the person christianity is named after. the four gospels are the record of what he said and did, written down in the decades after.\nhe taught his followers one prayer, in matthew 6. one line of it asks for bread for today, not for the week. read next to the manna it's the same rule: enough for now, don't stockpile. in the same sermon he says don't worry about tomorrow, it has enough trouble of its own.\nhe also had a habit of feeding people. in john 6 he feeds a crowd with a few loaves and afterward tells his people to gather up the leftovers so nothing is wasted. take only what's given, and waste none of it. kimmerer's rules, from the other side of the world.\nfor today, he's the one who took the manna story and turned it into a prayer you can say in five seconds.","name":"jesus","where":"matthew 6 for the prayer and the bit about tomorrow. john 6 for the bread and the manna argument."},"kimmerer":{"lines":"a botanist who teaches at the state university of new york's college of environmental science and forestry in syracuse, and an enrolled member of the citizen potawatomi nation. she's been both of those things her whole career, and for a long time the two didn't talk to each other.\nshe tells the story of showing up to college wanting to study why asters and goldenrod look so good together, and being told by her advisor that wasn't science. it took her years to find her way back to the question. her book braiding sweetgrass, published in 2013, is the answer: plants as teachers, and science and indigenous knowledge as two ways of knowing that need each other.\nthe book sold slowly and then, seven years later, hit the bestseller lists on word of mouth alone. she got a macarthur fellowship in 2022. she also founded the center for native peoples and the environment at her school.\nfor today, she's the one who wrote down the rules for taking, and admitted she's had to learn them slowly, one strawberry at a time.","name":"robin wall kimmerer","where":"braiding sweetgrass, milkweed editions, 2013. start with the gift of strawberries, near the front, then the honorable harvest, further in."},"manna":{"lines":"the bread that showed up on the ground in the desert. exodus 16 says when the morning dew lifted there was a fine flaky stuff on the ground, white, and it tasted like wafers made with honey. the people looked at it and said, what is it. in hebrew that's roughly man hu, and the name stuck.\nthe rules were strict. one measure per person per day. gather in the morning, because it melted when the sun got hot. keep none overnight, except on the sixth day, when you gathered double and it kept through the sabbath. anybody who tried to hoard on a normal day found worms in it by morning.\nthe book says it fed them for forty years and stopped the day they ate the first crop from the promised land. a jar of it was supposed to be kept in the ark of the covenant so later generations would know.\njesus gets asked about it in john 6. people want a sign like manna, and he tells them the manna was never the point. your ancestors ate it and died anyway. that's the mystic's reading: it was never about the bread.","name":"manna","where":"exodus 16, the whole chapter. then numbers 11, where they get sick of it, and john 6, where jesus argues about it."},"moses":{"lines":"the man who walked the israelites out of slavery in egypt, and then spent forty years in the desert with them, mostly getting complained at. he's the central human figure of the hebrew bible, and the first five books are traditionally credited to him.\nhis story: born to slaves, hidden in a basket on the nile, raised in pharaoh's own house. killed an egyptian who was beating a hebrew and ran for the desert. spent forty years herding sheep there. then a bush caught fire and didn't burn up, and a voice sent him back to egypt to get everyone out.\nhe wasn't a natural. he told god he couldn't talk right and to send somebody else. later he lost his temper and hit a rock he was told to speak to, and for that he was told he'd never enter the land. he died on a mountain looking at it from across the river.\nfor today, he's the guy delivering the rule about the manna, and the first to watch people ignore it. he'd already learned in the desert, alone, how little you need in a day.","name":"moses","where":"exodus 2 through 4 for the beginning, exodus 16 for the manna, numbers 20 for the rock, deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"potawatomi":{"lines":"a native nation from the great lakes, originally around what's now michigan, wisconsin, indiana and illinois. they're one of the three fires, alongside the ojibwe and the odawa, related peoples who speak related languages. the potawatomi were the keepers of the fire in that alliance.\nin the 1830s the united states pushed them off their land. one group was marched at gunpoint from indiana to kansas in 1838, and enough people died on the way that the potawatomi still call it the trail of death. later they were moved again, to oklahoma. the citizen potawatomi nation is headquartered in shawnee, oklahoma today.\nkimmerer writes that her grandfather was taken to the carlisle indian school in pennsylvania as a boy, where the point was to strip the language and the ways out of native kids. so the potawatomi she carries came to her partly broken, and she's spent her adult life learning it back, including the language, a few words at a time.\nit matters today because the rules on the left page aren't an idea. they're a people's way of staying alive on land, kept through two removals and a boarding school.","name":"the potawatomi","where":"kimmerer tells her family's part in braiding sweetgrass, in the chapter called learning the grammar of animacy, and in the one on her grandfather."},"sweetgrass":{"lines":"robin wall kimmerer's 2013 book, published by milkweed editions, a small nonprofit press in minneapolis. the full title is braiding sweetgrass: indigenous wisdom, scientific knowledge, and the teachings of plants.\nsweetgrass is a fragrant grass that's sacred to a lot of native nations. it gets braided in three strands, and the book is built the same way: her people's teachings, her training as a scientist, and what the plants themselves have to say. the sections are named for working the grass: planting, tending, picking, braiding, burning.\nit's essays, not a manual. one is about wild strawberries and what a gift is. one is about her potawatomi language teacher and why the language has a word for a bay that's a verb, not a noun. one is the honorable harvest. one is the thanksgiving address the haudenosaunee open their gatherings with, which goes on for a while because it thanks everything.\nthe thread through all of it is reciprocity. the world gives, and the only sane response is to give back. that's the whole left page.","name":"braiding sweetgrass","where":"the book itself. the gift of strawberries is near the front. the honorable harvest is in the section called braiding sweetgrass. read those two and you'll know if you want the rest."},"wilderness":{"lines":"the desert between egypt and the land the israelites had been promised, the sinai peninsula, roughly. the trip should have taken about eleven days. deuteronomy says so, flat out. it took forty years.\nwhy forty years: when they got to the edge of the land they sent scouts in, the scouts came back scared, and the people refused to go. so the whole generation that walked out of egypt died in the desert and their kids went in instead. the wandering wasn't the plan. it was the consequence.\ndeuteronomy 8 looks back on it and says the hunger and the manna were there to teach one thing: a person doesn't live on bread alone. centuries later jesus spends forty days hungry in a desert and quotes that exact line back at the devil. old texts talking to each other.\nfor today, the wilderness is where the manna rule gets learned, because it's the one place nobody could pretend they were feeding themselves.","name":"the wilderness","where":"exodus 15 through 18 for the first stretch, numbers 13 and 14 for the refusal, deuteronomy 8 for the point of it, matthew 4 for jesus quoting it."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 16 · read the whole chapter, and count how many times they complain","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"it rots by morning.\nthe [[israelites]] were starving in the [[wilderness]] and bread showed up on the ground at dawn. [[manna]]. [[moses]] told them to gather what they'd eat that day and keep none of it.\nsome of them kept some. by morning it was full of worms and it stank.\nover a thousand years later [[jesus]] taught people to pray for bread one day at a time. same lesson. whatever you're holding back for later is doing what the manna did. you just can't smell it yet."},"theme":"only what's given, only today","id":"2026-09-29"},{"caption":"the trail one of them walked alone is the rope the other gets pulled out with.","date":"2026-09-30","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260930bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260930bg)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='330' cy='58' r='17' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none' opacity='.45'/><path d='M 0 192 C 70 188 150 196 230 192 S 370 190 430 192 L 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 L 800 192'/><path d='M 34 192 C 33 150 35 100 34 48'/><path d='M 28 152 L 40 152 M 28 112 L 40 112 M 28 78 L 40 78'/><path d='M 98 192 C 99 150 97 110 98 70'/><path d='M 92 160 L 104 160 M 92 124 L 104 124 M 92 94 L 104 94'/><path d='M 64 192 L 64 152'/><path d='M 57 152 L 71 152 M 58 174 L 70 174'/><path d='M 34 70 C 44 61 57 61 64 68 C 55 75 43 75 34 70 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 34 122 C 26 113 14 113 7 120 C 15 127 26 127 34 122 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 98 104 C 108 95 121 95 128 102 C 119 109 107 109 98 104 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='230' cy='104' r='14'/><path d='M 217 106 L 210 108'/><path d='M 230 118 L 228 160'/><path d='M 228 160 L 211 192 M 228 160 L 246 192'/><path d='M 229 132 L 209 146 M 229 132 L 245 151'/><path d='M 210 124 L 204 192'/><path d='M 258 201 C 300 205 350 203 398 201' stroke-dasharray='1 13'/><path d='M 400 201 C 450 199 520 192 560 177 C 580 169 592 164 604 162 C 616 168 624 184 628 200 C 632 216 638 228 645 236'/><circle cx='580' cy='118' r='14'/><path d='M 580 132 L 582 168'/><path d='M 582 168 L 571 192 M 582 168 L 595 192'/><path d='M 581 144 L 604 160 M 581 150 L 600 167'/><circle cx='676' cy='256' r='14'/><path d='M 676 270 L 676 292'/><path d='M 676 292 L 666 306 M 676 292 L 687 306'/><path d='M 676 275 L 656 262 L 648 240 M 676 275 L 660 271 L 652 247'/><g transform='translate(52,125) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"khaggavisana sutta, sutta nipata 1.3 · read all forty one verses, it takes ten minutes","sourceRef":"rhino","text":"walk alone.\nthere's an old poem in the [[palicanon|pali canon]], forty one verses, and nearly every one ends on the same line: wander alone, like a [[rhino|rhinoceros]].\nit's kept with the [[buddha]]'s teachings, and its reasons aren't gentle. you get close to people, you start needing them, and the needing is where it hurts. it says family tangles you up like a bamboo thicket. be the one shoot that grows clear of it.\nnobody can walk this for you. quit waiting for company."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one are you hiding in right now, the crowd or the cave?","refs":{"bonhoeffer":{"lines":"a german pastor and theologian, born in 1906, hanged by the nazis in april 1945 at thirty nine. he was one of eight children, had his doctorate at twenty one, and could have had a quiet career writing books.\ninstead he was against hitler in public from the first days. two days after hitler took office he was on the radio warning about him, and the broadcast got cut off. when much of the german church went along with the nazis, he joined the pastors who refused, and ended up training their ministers in secret. in the summer of 1939 he left for new york, where he'd have been safe. he turned around within weeks and sailed home, because he didn't think he'd have the right to help rebuild germany if he sat the war out.\nback home he joined the resistance, with a job in military intelligence as his cover. he was arrested in april 1943 and spent two years locked up. they hanged him at the flossenbürg camp on april 9, 1945, two weeks before american soldiers got there.\nhere's why he's on this page. the man who wrote that you can't do it alone spent his last two years in prison, cut off from every one of those men. what came out of it was letters, and most of the ones in the book made from them were written to one friend, eberhard bethge, who'd been his student at the seminary. the community was gone. he kept one brother, by mail.","name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","where":"life together for the community. letters and papers from prison for the cell. eberhard bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography is the full story, written by the friend who got the letters."},"buddha":{"lines":"siddhattha gotama, born about twenty five hundred years ago near what's now the border of nepal and india. buddha isn't a name. it's a title, and it means the one who woke up.\nthe story as it's handed down: he had a comfortable home, a wife, a son. at twenty nine he left all of it, cut off his hair, and went looking for the end of suffering. he studied under two teachers and outgrew both. he spent years starving himself alongside five companions, and when he quit that and ate a real meal, the five walked out on him in disgust. he was by himself when he sat down under the tree and woke up. he was thirty five.\nthen he did the opposite for forty five years. he went and found those same five and taught them first. he built a community of monks and nuns, and he walked the roads of north india with a crowd around him until he died at eighty.\nso he lived both pages of this spread. he got free alone, and then he never lived alone again. the rhinoceros verses sound like the first half of that life.","name":"the buddha","where":"the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26, is his own account of leaving home, the two teachers, the five companions, and waking up. the mahaparinibbana sutta, digha nikaya 16, is the last months."},"finkenwalde":{"lines":"a village near the city of stettin, close to the baltic coast. germany then, poland now. a seminary is a school that trains pastors, and in 1935 the confessing church, the german protestants who wouldn't let the nazi state run their church, needed one of its own. they gave the job to bonhoeffer. he was twenty nine.\nhe ran it like nothing his students had seen. twenty three young men that first term, in a neglected old estate they had to clean out before they could live in it. the day started in silence. then psalms read back and forth, and scripture out loud, morning and night. after breakfast, half an hour alone and quiet over one short passage.\npeople sneered that he'd started a monastery. some of his own students joked about it and found it heavy going. he thought protestants had thrown out something they needed.\nthe state declared the training illegal, and in the fall of 1937 the gestapo closed the place and sealed it. by the end of that year twenty seven of his former students had been arrested. he kept the training going in secret for a couple more years, in a small town and a village further east.\nso the book on this page is about something that lasted a little over two years and was already gone when he wrote it down.","name":"finkenwalde","where":"bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, the chapters on the preachers' seminary. bonhoeffer's own version of what they did there is life together."},"gestapo":{"lines":"the secret police of nazi germany. the name is short for geheime staatspolizei, secret state police. hitler took power in 1933 and ran germany until 1945, and the gestapo was how the state dealt with anyone it decided was an enemy. no court had to sign off. they could lock you up on their own say so.\nit was a much smaller outfit than its reputation. most of its cases started with a tip from an ordinary german, a neighbor reporting a neighbor, and the fear did the rest.\na state that wanted to be the only thing anybody answered to had no use for pastors who wouldn't go along. they got watched, silenced and jailed. bonhoeffer lost his right to teach at the university in 1936, was banned from speaking in public in 1940, and from publishing in 1941.\nfor today: the thing that ended his community wasn't an argument or a falling out. it was police at the door. he knew, when he wrote the book, that having your people around you is not a given.","name":"the gestapo","where":"bethge's biography tells the closing of the seminary. bonhoeffer's own line on community being grace, nothing you can claim, is in the first chapter of life together."},"lifetogether":{"lines":"a short book bonhoeffer wrote in 1938, the year after the gestapo closed finkenwalde. he wrote it in the empty home of his twin sister sabine. her husband's father was jewish, which made the whole family jews in nazi eyes, and that fall they fled germany. bonhoeffer drove them to the swiss border himself.\nit's what he learned living with his students, put on paper. five chapters: community, the day with others, the day alone, ministry, and confession and communion. when he says brother he means any other christian, but you can read it as whoever is actually walking this with you.\nthe first chapter is where today's page comes from. he says it isn't a given that you get to live among your own people. jesus lived in the middle of his enemies. so if you've got people, that's grace, nothing you can claim. then the hard part: you need another person to say the true thing to you, because in your own heart it's shaky, and in his mouth it's sure.\nhe's rough on the dream version too. whoever loves his dream of a community more than the actual community, he says, ends up destroying it, however honest and earnest he is about it.\nthen the chapter on the day alone turns around and argues the left page. whoever can't be alone should beware of community. whoever isn't in community should beware of being alone. he sets the two warnings back to back and won't pick.","name":"life together","where":"life together, the first chapter, community, for the brother's word being stronger than your own. the third, the day alone, for the two warnings. it's short enough to read in an evening."},"palicanon":{"lines":"the oldest complete set of buddhist scriptures still in use. pali is an old indian language, a cousin of sanskrit, and this is the collection kept by the theravada school, the buddhism of sri lanka, thailand and burma.\nnobody wrote it down for centuries. monks memorized it and chanted it together, which is why it repeats itself so much. repeating is how you don't lose a line. it was finally put in writing in sri lanka around the first century bc, on palm leaves.\nit's called the three baskets. one holds the rules for monks and nuns. one holds the teachings, thousands of talks and poems. one holds the later analysis, which reads like a parts manual for the mind.\nthe rhinoceros poem sits in the teachings basket, in a small book called the sutta nipata, and scholars count parts of that book among the earliest layers of the whole thing. read it and you're about as close to the first generation as the record gets.","name":"the pali canon","where":"the sutta nipata is one of the short books in the khuddaka nikaya, the last of the five collections of teachings. thanissaro bhikkhu and bhikkhu bodhi have both put the whole sutta nipata into english."},"rhino":{"lines":"a sutta is a teaching of the buddha, memorized and handed down. most are talks. this one is a poem, forty one verses, called the khaggavisana sutta in pali, the language it was kept in. it's counted among the oldest material in the whole collection. a copy on birch bark from about two thousand years ago, from the old region of gandhara and now in the british library, is among the oldest buddhist manuscripts anyone has.\nthe refrain is the whole poem. wander alone like a rhinoceros. translators argue about it, because the pali word can mean the animal or its horn, and the indian rhino has just the one. either way the picture is the same. one, not two.\nthe verses go after everything you lean on. friends, children, a spouse, good company. then come the pictures of what free looks like. a deer in the woods that eats where it wants. a lion that doesn't jump at noises. wind a net can't hold. a lotus the water doesn't stick to.\nhere's what the hit line leaves out. one spot breaks the pattern. if you find a companion who's wise and steady, walk with him and be glad. if you don't, go alone, like a king walking away from his kingdom. so the poem was never against people. it was against company that makes you smaller.\ntwo more things. the tradition's own old commentary says these verses weren't first spoken by the buddha. it credits them to solitary buddhas, people who woke up on their own and never taught. and the buddha himself, in another sutta, corrects his attendant ananda for saying good friends are half the path. not half, he says. the whole of it. the collection kept both and never smoothed it over.","name":"the rhinoceros sutta","where":"sutta nipata, chapter 1, sutta 3, verses 35 to 75. the exchange with ananda about friendship is the upaddha sutta, samyutta nikaya 45.2."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, life together · read the first chapter, then the day alone, where he argues the other side","sourceRef":"lifetogether","text":"you can't do this alone.\n[[bonhoeffer]] ran an illegal [[finkenwalde|seminary]] in germany until the [[gestapo]] shut it. then he sat down and wrote [[lifetogether|a short book]] about what living with those men had taught him.\nthe truth in your own head is weak. the same truth out of your brother's mouth is strong. you can't talk yourself out of the hole, because the one doing the talking is the one in it.\nhe called having people around you grace, nothing you can claim. his got taken."},"theme":"walk alone, walk together","id":"2026-09-30"},{"caption":"the dark one of them sits under is the thing the gods couldn't burn or budge.","date":"2026-10-01","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261001bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261001bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><path d='M 84 262 L 84 232 L 104 232 L 104 262 M 90 240 L 98 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 114 262 L 126 230 L 143 236 L 133 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 62 270 C 50 258 68 246 82 252 C 90 242 112 242 120 252 C 132 244 154 250 152 260 C 164 262 162 274 152 274 L 70 274 C 62 274 58 272 62 270 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 128 118 C 100 118 98 90 124 86 C 126 60 166 52 186 70 C 204 44 258 44 272 72 C 300 54 342 62 348 88 C 384 76 430 82 444 100 C 480 92 530 100 548 112 C 500 126 300 130 128 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 548 112 C 580 120 600 160 606 222'/><path d='M 600 214 L 606 224 L 613 215'/><circle cx='226' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 226 198 L 226 240 M 226 240 C 206 240 196 258 211 262 M 226 240 C 246 240 256 258 241 262 M 226 214 L 212 240 M 226 214 L 240 240'/><path d='M 222 176 L 223 174 M 231 176 L 232 174'/><path d='M 608 262 C 608 250 610 242 616 234 M 608 262 C 606 254 603 250 599 247'/><circle cx='516' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 516 194 L 516 234 M 516 234 L 504 262 M 516 234 L 529 262 M 516 208 L 552 228 M 516 208 L 500 228'/><path d='M 562 236 C 551 230 556 218 562 209 C 567 218 574 228 562 236 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='712' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 715 190 L 724 234 M 724 234 L 712 262 M 724 234 L 740 262 M 718 206 L 740 216 M 718 206 L 700 220'/><path d='M 690 178 C 676 174 666 182 652 178 M 688 190 C 676 190 668 196 656 194 M 642 186 L 634 187'/><\/g><g transform='translate(82,205) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"the cloud of unknowing, chapters 4 to 7 · they're short, read the four in one sitting","sourceRef":"cloud","text":"you can't think your way to god.\nyou keep trying. reading, sorting, working out what you believe.\nabout 650 years ago an englishman, nobody knows his name, wrote a guide to prayer called [[cloud|the cloud of unknowing]]. rule one: stop trying to figure god out.\nyou can love him, he says. you can't fit him in a thought. every idea you have about god, even a good one, stands between you and him.\nso [[forgetting|put the ideas down]]. it'll feel like sitting in the dark. stay there."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you sure about that you've never once checked?","refs":{"cloud":{"lines":"a book about one kind of prayer, written in english in the second half of the 1300s. whoever wrote it never signed it. the best guess is a priest from the east midlands of england, and plenty of people call him a monk, but nobody knows. seventeen handwritten copies survive.\nit's a letter from a teacher to one student, and the student is young. in chapter 4 the book has him say he's twenty four and has never once paid attention to how he spent his time.\nthe teaching is two clouds. above you, between you and god, is a cloud of unknowing. he calls it a darkness, and says by darkness he only means not knowing. below you, you put a cloud of forgetting over everything else. then you stay there and keep reaching up with what he calls a sharp dart of longing love. the line the whole book hangs on is in chapter 6: he may well be loved, but not thought.\nhe isn't against thinking. he says thinking about god's kindness is good, and then tells you to tread it down anyway while you're doing this, because whatever you're thinking about is standing between you and god. he got the idea from a much older writer who went by dionysius.\nhere's why it's on this page. it was written for somebody young who wanted to get god right. the first thing it does is take getting it right off the table.","name":"the cloud of unknowing","where":"the cloud of unknowing, chapters 4 to 7. evelyn underhill's edition keeps the old wording and is easy to find. clifton wolters put it into modern english."},"forgetting":{"lines":"the book has two clouds, and this is the one people forget about. the cloud of unknowing is above you, between you and god, and you can't do anything about that one. the cloud of forgetting is the one you make. chapter 5.\nwhat goes under it is everything. every creature that ever was, is, or will be, and everything any of them ever did. not because they're bad. his reason is that whatever you're thinking about is, for that moment, above you, and between you and god.\nthen he goes further. thoughts about god's kindness, the saints, the angels, the joys of heaven. those go under too. in chapter 7 a thought shows up asking, what is this god you want? he says to tell it you have no skill in him, and then tell it to go back down, even if it looks holy and looks like it's trying to help.\nhis trick for doing it: take one little word of one syllable, god or love, and hold on to it, and beat every thought down with it.\nfor today: forgetting isn't being stupid. it's what you do with everything you're sure of while you sit there. you put it down, and you find out what's left without it.","name":"the cloud of forgetting","where":"the cloud of unknowing, chapter 5 for the cloud of forgetting, chapter 7 for the one little word."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel. born around 4 bc. around the year 30 he spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided.\nthe roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him.\nstrip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off.\non this page he's only a yardstick. scholars put the kena around the middle of the thousand years before him. it was already old when he was born.","name":"jesus","where":"mark, the shortest of the four gospels, in one sitting. then matthew 5 through 7, the sermon on the mount."},"kena":{"lines":"the upanishads are the old hindu wisdom texts, written down roughly between 800 and 200 bc, that sit at the end of the vedas, india's oldest scriptures. upanishad means something like sitting down near, as in near a teacher.\nthe kena is one of the short ones. thirty four verses in four parts, two in poetry and two in plain prose. it belongs to the sama veda. kena means by whom, or by what.\nnobody has a firm date for it. scholars put it somewhere around the middle of the thousand years before jesus, and one of them files it as older than buddhism. so older than jesus is safe, by centuries. how many centuries is a guess.\nwhat it's after is brahman, the ground of everything, the thing behind your own seeing and hearing and thinking. part two says you can't hold it as a thing you know. in max müller's old translation: he by whom it is not thought, by him it is thought. he by whom it is thought, knows it not.\npart three tells the same thing as a story, and that's the right page. the gods have a victory and decide it was all them. the text says brahman won it for them.","name":"the kena upanishad","where":"kena upanishad, part 2, verse 3, for the line. part 3 for the story, and the start of part 4 for who the spirit was. max müller's translation calls it the talavakara upanishad."},"yaksha":{"lines":"in indian stories a yaksha is a nature spirit. mostly kind, sometimes mischievous, now and then a lot worse. they guard treasure buried in the ground and in the roots of trees, they change shape, and people honored them as the guardian of a town or a lake or a well.\nin the kena it's just the word the gods use for the thing that shows up in front of them, because they have no idea what it is. max müller translates it sprite. some strange spirit.\nit's brahman, the ground of everything, the one that actually won their victory for them. they're looking straight at where their own power comes from and can't tell what it is. so they send fire over to find out, and then wind.\nwhen indra, the king of the gods, walks up to it himself, it's gone. a goddess named uma is standing there instead, and she tells him what it was, and whose victory it was.\nfor today: the thing the gods couldn't recognize is the thing they were sure they didn't need.","name":"the yaksha","where":"kena upanishad, part 3, and the first lines of part 4 for uma's answer."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"kena upanishad, parts 2 and 3 · it's four short parts, read all of it","sourceRef":"kena","text":"if you think you've got it, you don't.\nthe [[kena|kena upanishad]] is a hindu text a few pages long, written centuries before [[jesus]]. it's about the power behind everything, the thing that lets you see and think at all.\nit says the people who claim to understand that power don't. the ones who admit they can't are closer.\nthen a story. the gods win a battle and take the credit. [[yaksha|a stranger]] sets down one blade of grass. the god of fire can't burn it. the god of wind can't move it.\nit was never their power. it isn't yours either."},"theme":"the ones who are sure","id":"2026-10-01"},{"caption":"what one of them put down is lying right where the other one can reach it.","date":"2026-10-02","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261002bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261002bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><circle cx='84' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 76 186 L 92 186 L 90 226 L 78 226 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 81 226 L 69 262 M 87 226 L 93 262 M 76 192 L 56 210 M 92 192 L 112 210'/><path d='M 56 210 l -5 -3 M 56 210 l -6 2 M 112 210 l 5 -3 M 112 210 l 6 2'/><circle cx='176' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 168 180 L 184 180 L 182 220 L 170 220 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 173 220 L 161 262 M 179 220 L 185 262 M 168 186 L 148 204 M 184 186 L 204 204'/><path d='M 148 204 l -5 -3 M 148 204 l -6 2 M 204 204 l 5 -3 M 204 204 l 6 2'/><circle cx='268' cy='172' r='14'/><path d='M 260 188 L 276 188 L 274 228 L 262 228 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 265 228 L 253 262 M 271 228 L 277 262 M 260 194 L 240 212 M 276 194 L 296 212'/><path d='M 240 212 l -5 -3 M 240 212 l -6 2 M 296 212 l 5 -3 M 296 212 l 6 2'/><path d='M 346 254 L 428 254 M 346 250 L 346 254'/><path d='M 428 251 L 452 247 L 455 258 L 430 258 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 412 254 q 5 8 10 0'/><circle cx='498' cy='190' r='14'/><path d='M 508 200 L 536 228 M 536 228 L 522 262 M 536 228 L 552 262 M 514 207 L 476 240 M 514 207 L 528 236'/><path d='M 476 240 l -7 1 M 476 240 l -3 6'/><circle cx='676' cy='247' r='14'/><path d='M 690 250 L 738 254 M 738 254 L 764 247 M 738 254 L 766 260 M 702 251 L 712 262 M 702 251 L 720 238'/><\/g><g transform='translate(28,244) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"muslim","source":"eknath easwaran, nonviolent soldier of islam · a short warm life of him in english, read all of it","sourceRef":"ghaffarkhan","text":"put it down.\nyou think not hitting back is for soft people.\n[[ghaffarkhan|abdul ghaffar khan]] was a muslim from the [[pashtuns]], mountain people on what's now the pakistan and afghanistan border, raised on the blood feud. when the british ruled india, he built an army of a hundred thousand of them, in red shirts, with one oath: no weapons, no revenge.\nhe told them he was handing them the weapon of the prophet muhammad. patience, and being in the right.\nin 1930 british troops opened fire on them in [[qissakhwani|a market in peshawar]]. they kept walking forward, unarmed, row after row.\ntoday is [[gandhi]]'s birthday. people called khan the frontier gandhi."},"mode":"disagree","question":"who would you have to watch get hurt before you'd pick it up?","refs":{"bonhoeffer":{"lines":"a german pastor and theologian, born in 1906. he saw what hitler was the minute he took over in 1933 and said so, while most of the churches in germany went along. his book on what it costs to follow jesus, the cost of discipleship, came out in 1937.\nfriends got him to new york in june 1939 to keep him safe. he lasted a few weeks and sailed home. then he joined the resistance, a circle inside german military intelligence who tried more than once to kill hitler. he carried messages abroad for them. he knew of the plans to kill hitler and approved of them, but he took no part in the attempts himself.\nhe was arrested in april 1943, at first for helping jews get out of germany. he was hanged at flossenbürg, a nazi concentration camp, on april 9, 1945, two weeks before the americans got there and about a month before germany surrendered. he was thirty nine.\nhere's the trouble this page is about. he'd been a pacifist, someone who won't use violence at all. back in 1933 he had written that the church's job was not just to bandage the victims under the wheel but to put a spoke in the wheel itself. when it came to it, he helped men who meant to kill. he never called it innocent. he accepted the guilt and did it anyway.","name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","where":"eberhard bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, written by his closest friend, for what he did. ethics and letters and papers from prison for what he thought about it."},"ethicsbook":{"lines":"the book bonhoeffer meant to be his big one. he worked on it during the years he was in the resistance, and it was still in pieces when he was arrested in april 1943. his friend eberhard bethge put the pieces together and published them in 1949, four years after he was hanged.\nthe part today's page uses is called the structure of responsible life. he drafted it in the summer of 1942, while he was carrying messages.\nthe line is this, in the newer english translation: everyone who acts responsibly becomes guilty. an older translation has the rest of it. when a man takes guilt upon himself in responsibility, he puts it on himself and no one else. before other men he is justified by dire necessity. before himself he is acquitted by his conscience. but before god he hopes only for grace.\nhe doesn't make it about his own year. he says it about everybody who ever acts for somebody else. his reason is jesus, who took on guilt that wasn't his. so a conscience that's been freed, he says, is free to bear guilt for the sake of the neighbor.\nfor today: he's not saying killing is fine if the cause is good. he's saying it isn't fine, and doing it anyway, and answering for it himself. before god, he says, all he hopes for is grace.","name":"ethics","where":"dietrich bonhoeffer, ethics, the section called the structure of responsible life, inside the chapter history and good. the newer translation is volume 6 of the dietrich bonhoeffer works in english."},"gandhi":{"lines":"an indian lawyer, born october 2, 1869, who became the leader of india's fight to get out from under british rule. he died in delhi on january 30, 1948.\nhe figured out how to fight an empire without firing a shot, by getting millions of ordinary people to break unfair laws on purpose and take the beating without hitting back. every protest you've seen where people sit down in the road and let themselves get dragged off runs on his method.\nhe read a hindu scripture and the sermon on the mount side by side. the thing to take from him is that you can refuse to hate someone and still refuse to obey them.\nhe's on both of today's pages. ghaffar khan was his close friend, close enough to get called the frontier gandhi, and the two toured the frontier together in 1938. bonhoeffer, over in europe, had a chance in the 1930s to go to india and study nonviolent resistance with him. he talked about it, made plans, and never went. he went back to germany.\nso today's two pages are two people who took him seriously and came out in different places.","name":"mohandas gandhi","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth. then the sermon on the mount, matthew chapters 5 through 7, which he said went straight to his heart."},"ghaffarkhan":{"lines":"a landowner's son from the pashtuns, the hill people on both sides of the border between what's now pakistan and afghanistan, where an insult could be paid back in blood generations later. born in 1890, died in 1988.\nin 1929 he started an army that swore off violence, men in red shirts, somewhere over a hundred thousand of them by the usual estimates. he said he got the idea from the prophet muhammad. he told them he was going to give them a weapon the police and the army would not be able to stand against. it is the weapon of the prophet, he said, but you are not aware of it. that weapon is patience and righteousness. every account gives those same words. none gives the day or the place he said them.\nhe was in gandhi's inner circle, which is how he got called the frontier gandhi. he spent something like a third of his long life locked up, first by the british and then by pakistan. somewhere between twenty seven and thirty years, depending on who's counting.\nthe movement didn't last. pakistan banned the red shirts in 1948 and broke them. he kept saying the same thing into his nineties.\nhere's why he's on this page, on gandhi's birthday. everybody knows the one. hardly anybody knows the muslim next door who did the same thing, with the people everyone said couldn't.","name":"abdul ghaffar khan","where":"eknath easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam, first published in 1984 as a man to match his mountains. then my life and struggle, his own memoir, in english since 1969."},"nazi":{"lines":"hitler's party, which ran germany from 1933 to 1945. they built a police state, started the second world war, and murdered six million jews along with millions of others in the holocaust.\nthe word nazi gets thrown around a lot now. on these pages it means the actual thing, a government that jailed and killed people like bonhoeffer while most of the churches went along.\nfor today: this is what he was looking at when he decided. he wasn't weighing nonviolence against an insult or a bad law. he was weighing it against this.","name":"nazi","where":"bethge's biography of bonhoeffer tells it from inside one family. for the whole of it, any history of the holocaust."},"pashtuns":{"lines":"a people who live mostly in the south and east of afghanistan and the north-west of pakistan, somewhere around sixty to seventy million of them. they speak pashto, and they're often called the world's largest tribal society, split into hundreds of tribes. in india the usual word for them was pathan, and the british used it too.\nalongside islam, what holds them together is pashtunwali, their code of honor, older than their islam. it tells you to take in any guest, whoever he is, and ask nothing back. it also has badal, justice or revenge. badal has no time limit, and even a taunt can count as a mortal insult, which is how feuds ran for generations.\noutsiders made that the whole story. the british and the people who wrote for them had the pathan down as a warrior, hotheaded and unpredictable, violent by nature. one scholar's book on the red shirts, the pathan unarmed, is about that picture and how badly it missed.\nfor today: the red shirts' oath had them swear off badal by name. ghaffar khan didn't go looking for gentle people. he went to the ones whose own code said hit back, and asked them for the opposite.","name":"the pashtuns","where":"easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam for the world he grew up in. mukulika banerjee's the pathan unarmed for how it happened."},"qissakhwani":{"lines":"on april 23, 1930, after abdul ghaffar khan was arrested, a crowd gathered in the qissa khwani bazaar, the storytellers' market in the old city of peshawar. british armored cars drove into the square at speed and killed several people, and then troops opened fire on the unarmed crowd.\nthe account most people quote comes by way of the writer gene sharp. when those in front fell down wounded by the shots, those behind came forward with their chests bared. it says this went on from eleven in the morning till five in the evening. the same accounts say stones were thrown and an army dispatch rider was killed.\nthe official british count was twenty dead. indian and pakistani sources say up to four hundred, and ghaffar khan put it at two hundred to two hundred and fifty. the numbers are still argued over.\ntwo platoons of the garhwal rifles, indian soldiers in the british army, refused to be sent into the city against that crowd. their leaders got years in prison for it.\nfor today: this is what the oath cost. the movement was only months old. nobody in that square had been at it long enough to be good at it.","name":"the qissa khwani massacre","where":"easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam. gene sharp's account of the rows coming forward is quoted in most histories of the day."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, ethics, the part on the structure of responsible life · then eberhard bethge's biography for what he actually did","sourceRef":"ethicsbook","text":"sometimes you help kill him.\n[[bonhoeffer|dietrich bonhoeffer]] was a german pastor who took jesus at his word about loving your enemies. he'd even planned a trip to india to learn nonviolence from [[gandhi]].\nthen the [[nazi|nazis]] took over his country. he took a job in military intelligence as cover, and carried messages for the officers who were planning to kill hitler. he knew the plan. he was for it.\nhe never called it clean. in [[ethicsbook|the book he was writing]] when he was arrested, he says anyone who acts responsibly becomes guilty. you take that guilt on yourself, and before god all you can hope for is grace.\nthey hanged him."},"theme":"never hit back","id":"2026-10-02"},{"caption":"the pile one of them keeps building is the trail the other one leaves behind.","date":"2026-10-03","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261003bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261003bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><path d='M 38 118 L 116 118 M 50 118 L 50 130 M 104 118 L 104 130'/><path d='M 56 262 L 56 214 L 148 214 L 148 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 74 230 L 130 230 L 130 252 L 74 252 Z'/><ellipse cx='100' cy='208' rx='27' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 127 207 L 160 202'/><circle cx='100' cy='207' r='3.5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 90 194 C 86 186 94 182 90 174 M 108 194 C 104 186 112 182 108 174'/><circle cx='196' cy='166' r='14'/><path d='M 183 171 C 176 143 216 143 209 171 L 212 186 M 183 171 L 180 186'/><path d='M 196 180 L 196 228 M 196 228 L 185 262 M 196 228 L 208 262 M 196 197 L 160 202 M 196 197 L 224 192'/><path d='M 222 180 L 234 186 L 246 180 L 246 195 L 234 201 L 222 195 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 234 186 L 234 201'/><path d='M 300 250 L 346 250 L 346 262 L 300 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 238 L 350 238 L 350 250 L 304 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 297 226 L 343 226 L 343 238 L 297 238 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 302 214 L 348 214 L 348 226 L 302 226 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 296 202 L 342 202 L 342 214 L 296 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 303 190 L 349 190 L 349 202 L 303 202 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 300 178 L 346 178 L 346 190 L 300 190 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 298 166 L 344 166 L 344 178 L 298 178 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 304 154 L 350 154 L 350 166 L 304 166 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 299 142 L 345 142 L 345 154 L 299 154 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 344 118 L 388 132 L 385 143 L 341 129 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 396 146 C 420 162 432 200 436 240' stroke-dasharray='1 12'/><path d='M 418 252 L 456 252 L 456 262 L 418 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 486 253 L 518 253 L 518 262 L 486 262 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 546 254 L 572 254 L 572 262 L 546 262 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 598 255 L 618 255 L 618 262 L 598 262 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 642 257 L 655 257 L 655 262 L 642 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='716' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 716 198 L 716 240 M 716 240 C 696 240 686 258 701 262 M 716 240 C 736 240 746 258 731 262 M 716 214 L 704 238 L 716 236 M 716 214 L 728 238 L 716 236'/><\/g><g transform='translate(66,91) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"sor juana inés de la cruz, the reply to sor filotea · it's a letter, it's short, and she tells her whole life in it","sourceRef":"replyfilotea","text":"read everything.\n[[sorjuana|sor juana]] was a girl in mexico in the 1600s who could read by three and wanted to learn everything. women weren't allowed in the university. so she became a nun, because a convent was the one place a woman could keep a library.\n[[bishoppuebla|a bishop]] told her, in print, to drop the books and think about god.\n[[replyfilotea|her reply]] is one of the great fuck-yous in church history, and it's polite the whole way through. she says she can't stop. once she was banned from books for three months, so she studied everything else. even how an egg fries.\ngod gave her the mind. using it was the prayer."},"mode":"disagree","question":"what do you keep studying so you don't have to do it?","refs":{"bishoppuebla":{"lines":"manuel fernández de santa cruz, a spaniard born in 1637 who was bishop of puebla, a city in new spain, from 1676 until he died in 1699.\nin 1690 sor juana wrote out her objections to a famous sermon by a portuguese priest. the bishop printed it without asking her and put a letter of his own in front, dated november 25, 1690, and signed with a nun's name, sor filotea de la cruz. sor means sister.\nthe letter praises her mind and then says it's a pity that so great a mind stoops to the low news of the earth. he didn't want her to give up books, he wrote, only to better them by sometimes reading the book of jesus christ. that's the knock it off on today's page. it came with compliments.\nscholars split on him. most, the mexican poet octavio paz included, count him her friend. the 1990 film about her makes him one of the men who betrayed her.\nfor today: he's the reason the letter exists. he told the smartest person in the colony to aim lower, under a fake name, in print, and she answered in her own.","name":"the bishop of puebla","where":"his letter is printed in front of the reply in most editions, including alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology."},"replyfilotea":{"lines":"sor juana's answer to the bishop of puebla, dated march 1, 1691, from her convent in mexico city. it's addressed to sor filotea, the nun's name he had signed.\nit's part life story and part argument. she tells how she learned to read before she was three, lists learned women from the bible to her own time, and reads paul's order that women keep silent in church as a rule about pulpits, not about study or writing. it's been called the first feminist manifesto.\nthe part on today's page goes like this. a superior at the convent, very holy and very simple, decided study was a matter for the inquisition and ordered her not to read. she obeyed for the three months or so the woman was in charge, and didn't pick up a book. but not studying at all, she says, is not in her power. so she studied everything god had made. how the walls of a long room seem to lean toward each other. a top some girls were spinning, and why it kept going.\nthen she adds the kitchen. an egg holds together and fries in butter or oil, and falls to shreds in syrup. and the line, in margaret sayers peden's translation: had aristotle prepared victuals, he would have written more.\nshe's careful the whole way through. she says she wants no trouble with the inquisition and calls herself ignorant. reading it, you can't always tell where the humility stops and the irony starts. that's the polite part.","name":"the reply to sor filotea","where":"the reply to sor filotea. the ban and the kitchen come a little past the middle. margaret sayers peden's translation, a woman of genius, or alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology."},"sorjuana":{"lines":"a poet and nun in mexico city in the late 1600s, when mexico was a spanish colony called new spain. born in 1648 or 1651, nobody's sure. she taught herself nearly everything, because girls weren't let into the university.\nalmost all of what's known about her childhood is her own telling. before she was three, she says, she tagged along behind an older sister to a woman who taught reading, told the teacher her mother wanted her taught too, and could read before her mother found out. at six or seven she heard there was a university in mexico city and begged her mother to change her clothes and send her. translators give it as dress me as a boy. her mother said no, so she read her grandfather's books.\nshe took her vows in 1669 and was blunt about why. she had no wish at all to marry, and the convent was the least unsuitable choice open to her. what she'd wanted was to live alone, with nothing to get in the way of study. she got a cell of her own, instruments, visitors, and a library. the number that gets repeated is four thousand books. nobody has a catalog.\nthe hard parts. she owned a person, an enslaved woman named juana de san josé, and in 1684 sold her to her own sister. and about two years after her great letter she went quiet. her books went too, sold for the poor in most tellings, taken in some. she died in april 1695 nursing her sisters through a plague.\nfor today: the right page would tell her to put the books down. in the end they did go. whether that was surrender or peace, the record doesn't say.","name":"sor juana inés de la cruz","where":"the reply to sor filotea, where she tells her own life. margaret sayers peden's translation is called a woman of genius, and alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology has the reply and the poems together."},"taoteching":{"lines":"the short chinese book credited to lao tzu, eighty one tiny chapters, about five thousand characters in all. the title means something like the book of the way and its power. it probably took shape over a couple of centuries before about 250 bc. so it's not about her. it's close to two thousand years older than she is.\nit's about water, emptiness, not forcing, and how the soft outlasts the hard. it's also, oddly, advice to rulers, telling them the less they meddle the better things go.\nchapter 48 is six lines. in gia-fu feng and jane english's translation: in the pursuit of learning, every day something is acquired. in the pursuit of tao, every day something is dropped. less and less is done until non-action is achieved. when nothing is done, nothing is left undone. the last two lines turn to the ruler. the world is ruled by letting things take their course.\nthe other line is, in the same translation, give up learning, and put an end to your troubles. in most translations it's the first line of chapter 20. james legge has it there too: when we renounce learning we have no troubles. d.c. lau prints it with chapter 19 instead. chapter 20 runs about thirty short lines and goes somewhere else after that.\nfor today: it isn't saying be stupid. it's saying the piling up can turn into the problem, and that there's a kind of getting somewhere that only happens by setting things down.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapter 48, and the first line of chapter 20, or chapter 19 in d.c. lau's translation. gia-fu feng and jane english's version is the plainest."},"wuwei":{"lines":"chinese for doing without doing, or effortless action. it's the heart of the tao te ching. translators of chapter 48 usually give it as non-action.\nit's not laziness. it's acting without forcing, the way a good carpenter follows the grain of the wood or a swimmer floats instead of thrashing. you do things, but you stop fighting the way things want to go.\nathletes call it being in the zone. musicians call it the song playing itself.\nfor today: it's where all the dropping is headed. the chapter doesn't end with a person who does nothing. it ends with nothing left undone.","name":"wu wei","where":"tao te ching, chapter 48. less and less is done until non-action is achieved."}},"right":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 48 · then chapter 20. any translation, they're both short. a few print that line in 19","sourceRef":"taoteching","text":"drop one thing a day.\nthe [[taoteching|tao te ching]] is a short chinese book, well over two thousand years old, about how to live without forcing things. tao just means the way.\none chapter is aimed right at people like her. chase learning, it says, and every day you add something. follow the way and every day you drop something. you keep dropping until you've [[wuwei|stopped pushing]], and then everything gets done anyway.\nanother chapter is blunter. give up learning and your troubles are over.\nthat stack of books by your bed might be one more way to avoid sitting still."},"theme":"learn it or unlearn it","id":"2026-10-03"},{"caption":"the wide circle one of them used to keep is the mouth the other one walks into.","date":"2026-10-04","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261004bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261004bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><ellipse cx='98' cy='216' rx='32' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 226 L 72 262 M 88 228 L 90 262 M 108 228 L 106 262 M 120 226 L 124 262'/><path d='M 124 208 L 142 182'/><path d='M 142 182 L 160 188 L 157 197 L 140 193 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 144 182 l 1 -9 M 68 212 C 58 216 56 230 60 240'/><path d='M 176 258 C 186 96 388 96 400 258' stroke-dasharray='1 13'/><circle cx='262' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 258 199 L 250 236 M 250 236 L 238 262 M 250 236 L 262 262 M 256 210 L 292 206 M 256 214 L 290 222'/><circle cx='306' cy='188' r='14'/><path d='M 294 182 L 318 186 M 294 190 L 316 194' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 308 202 L 312 238 M 312 238 L 302 262 M 312 238 L 324 262 M 310 214 L 292 214 M 310 214 L 330 230'/><circle cx='291' cy='214' r='4' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 326 232 L 340 232 L 337 222 L 329 222 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 333 232 L 333 237'/><path d='M 400 258 C 412 60 744 60 756 262' fill='var(--paper)' fill-opacity='.35'/><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><circle cx='606' cy='148' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='664' cy='148' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='152' r='3'/><circle cx='660' cy='152' r='3'/><path d='M 582 126 L 622 134 M 688 126 L 648 134'/><path d='M 548 218 C 580 186 690 186 722 218 C 700 262 570 262 548 218 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 566 208 l 9 13 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -13'/><circle cx='492' cy='192' r='14'/><path d='M 494 206 L 498 238 M 498 238 L 486 262 M 498 238 L 512 262 M 496 216 L 522 214 M 496 216 L 480 232'/><\/g><g transform='translate(84,176) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"francis of assisi, the testament · it's a few pages. then thomas of celano's first life for the two miles, and the legend of the three companions for the horse and the coin","sourceRef":"testament","text":"kiss the thing that makes you gag.\n[[francis|francis of assisi]] was a rich italian kid, around 1200, who couldn't stand [[leper|lepers]]. he'd hold his nose two miles from their houses.\nat the end of his life he wrote down [[testament|what changed him]], and it starts there. he says god led him in among them, and he took care of them. and the thing that used to turn his stomach turned sweet.\nthe early biographies give the moment: he got off his horse, put a coin in a leper's hand, and kissed it.\nhe didn't wait for the disgust to go away. he went, and going is what changed it."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you keeping two miles away from?","refs":{"demon":{"lines":"this is a story, not a record. it's the first one in the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, a book put together in 1488, more than three hundred years after he lived.\nin the book there are five of them, with eyes as large as saucers. one is sitting on his bed preaching, two are listening, one is cooking, and the last is studying his books. he tries a polite song first, in case they're the local gods. then a fierce meditation and a spell. then he preaches to them, and means it. they stay where they are and stare.\nthen he remembers that all beings and everything he sees are of his own mind, and he stops trying to throw them out. he sings them a welcome. i pray you, stay. we will discourse and play together. then he gets up and goes straight at them, and they shrink back, swirl together like a whirlpool, merge into one, and vanish. that's how the book ends it. there's no mouth.\nthe mouth is in another telling. jack kornfield quotes it, and the american nun pema chödrön tells it her own way. there it's seven demons. three go when he praises them, three more vanish like a rainbow when he sings, and the last is the vicious one. he places himself in its mouth. it can't eat him, and it vanishes. in pema chödrön's words he says, just eat me up if you want to. today's page takes the cave from the book and the ending from that telling.\nfor today: every version agrees on what doesn't work. arguing with it, teaching it, and ordering it out. what works is letting it stay, and then going closer than you want to.","name":"the demons in the cave","where":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, the first story, the tale of red rock jewel valley. the telling with the mouth is quoted in jack kornfield's after the ecstasy, the laundry."},"francis":{"lines":"a rich cloth merchant's kid from assisi, a hill town in the middle of italy, born in 1181 or 1182. he spent his youth on parties, songs and good clothes, and he wanted to be a knight. he went to war against the town next door, got captured, and spent about a year in prison. he came home sick and not quite the same.\na few years later, riding outside assisi, he met a leper, and instead of riding past he got down and kissed him. after that the rest went fast. he gave back his father's money in front of the bishop, took off all his clothes and handed those back too, and walked out owning nothing.\nbrothers gathered around him, begging and preaching, and became the franciscans. the stories have him preaching to birds, making a deal with a wolf, and turning out to be the happiest person anyone had ever met.\nhe died on the evening of october 3, 1226, laid on the bare ground at his own asking. his feast day is october 4. that's today.\nhere's why the leper is the page. when he was dying and looked back at how it started, he didn't start with the war or the prison or the voice in the church. he started with the people he couldn't stand to look at.","name":"francis of assisi","where":"his own testament, which is a few pages. the little flowers of st francis for the legends. thomas of celano's first life, written within a few years of his death."},"leper":{"lines":"leprosy is a bacterial disease that eats away at nerves and skin, so people lose feeling and then fingers, toes, and faces. in the middle ages it was the most feared disease there was, and people thought it was a punishment for sin.\nlepers had to live outside the town, carry a clapper or bell so people could get out of the way, and nobody touched them.\nit's curable now with antibiotics. calling someone a leper still means the person nobody will go near.\nfor today: francis's disgust wasn't unusual. it was what everybody felt, and he had more of it than most. his first biographer says that in his wild years he'd hold his nostrils at the sight of their houses from two miles off. that's the man who got down off the horse.","name":"leprosy","where":"thomas of celano's first life of francis, the chapter on the lepers, for the two miles. francis's testament for his own three sentences about it."},"milarepa":{"lines":"a tibetan of the eleventh century, and tibet's favorite saint. nearly everything told about him comes from a life story and a book of songs written down about three hundred and fifty years after he died, so very little is known about the real man.\nin the story, an uncle robbed his family blind after his father died, and his mother sent him off to learn black magic. he used it to kill thirty five people. then the guilt ate him alive. he spent years hauling rocks for a brutal teacher named marpa, and then decades alone in mountain caves, half starved, living on nettles, singing what he'd learned.\nhunters and villagers came across him and he sang to them, and some of them stayed. the songs are still sung.\nfor today: he's in a cave by himself because of what he did. so when the story fills the cave with demons and he remembers they're his own mind, that isn't a figure of speech for him. he knows exactly what's in there.","name":"milarepa","where":"the life of milarepa, in lobsang lhalungpa's or andrew quintman's translation. then the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, where the cave stories are."},"testament":{"lines":"the last thing francis left his brothers. he spent his final days dictating it, and died on the evening of october 3, 1226.\nit opens with the lepers. in the standard english translation: when i was in sin, it seemed too bitter for me to see lepers. and the lord himself led me among them and i showed mercy to them. and when i left them, what had seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body. then he says he waited a little and left the world. that's his whole account of how he changed. three sentences.\nnotice what isn't in it. no horse, no coin, no kiss. those come from the lives written after he died. thomas of celano's first one, from 1228, has the two miles and the held nose, and says that one day he met a leper and kissed him. the legend of the three companions, begun in the 1240s, has him riding near assisi, getting down, giving the man a coin and kissing his hand.\nthe rest of the testament is a dying man telling his brothers not to let it go soft. nobody showed him what to do, he says. god did. work with your hands. don't go asking rome for favors. don't add explanations to the rule, or to these words.\nfor today: he says god led him in and the bitter turned sweet when he left. he doesn't say he felt ready first. the order on the page is his order.","name":"the testament of francis","where":"the testament, the first four sentences. it's in any collection of francis's own writings. the collection called francis of assisi: early documents has it alongside the early lives."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, the first story, the tale of red rock jewel valley · the mouth is from another telling, quoted in jack kornfield's after the ecstasy, the laundry","sourceRef":"demon","text":"walk into its mouth.\n[[milarepa]] was a tibetan hermit about a thousand years ago who lived in mountain caves. this is a story they tell about him.\nhe comes home to his cave and it's full of [[demon|demons]]. one's on his bed. one's reading his books.\nhe tries teaching them. they don't move. then it hits him that they're his own mind, and he says fine, stay, let's talk. all but one leave.\nthe last one is the worst. he walks up and puts himself in its mouth. go ahead, eat me.\nit leaves too.\nyou know which one is still sitting in your cave."},"theme":"walk toward it","id":"2026-10-04"},{"date":"2026-10-05","theme":"praying wrong","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"muslim","text":"god isn't grading your prayer.\nthere's a story [[rumi]] tells in [[masnavi|the masnavi]], and it's worth going slowly with, because you're in it.\n[[moses]] is walking along and he hears a shepherd praying out loud. and the prayer is ridiculous. god, where are you? let me fix your sandals. let me comb your hair and kill your lice. i'll kiss your little hand and sweep your room at bedtime.\nnow, moses is the one god actually talks to. he has credentials. so he does what the part of us that knows better always does. he corrects him. that's blasphemy. stuff cotton in your mouth. and the shepherd, who a minute ago was completely in love, tears his shirt and walks off into the desert.\nthen god has a word with moses. you drove my servant away from me. i don't look at the words. i look at what's burning inside.\nwe carry both of these people. there's the shepherd, who loves and says whatever comes. and there's moses, who got it right and stands guard over the right way. most mornings moses gets there first. before you've opened your mouth, something in you has already said, that's not how it's done.\nso notice him today. you don't have to throw him out. he's very devoted. just notice that the one who was sure how to pray got corrected, and the one talking about lice was the one god wanted back.","source":"rumi, the masnavi, book 2, the story of moses and the shepherd · nicholson's translation is the literal one","sourceRef":"masnavi"},"right":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"and then the words run out.\nhere's the other side, and don't be too quick to pick a team.\n[[maimonides]] was a rabbi and a doctor in egypt about eight hundred years ago, and he'd have stopped that shepherd too. it wasn't fussiness. it was awe.\nwhat he saw, in [[guideperplexed|the guide for the perplexed]], is that every word we have was made down here, for us. wise. strong. alive. the minute you hang one on god, you've made god a very large person. somebody like you, only more. and then you're praying to your own picture.\nhe tells about a man leading prayers who keeps piling it on. great, mighty, awesome. a rabbi lets him finish and says, that's like praising a king who owns millions in gold for having silver.\nthen he reaches into [[psalms|the psalms]], a whole book of songs to god, and pulls out one line. to you, silence is praise.\nyou may know this place. you've said everything you know how to say, it's all a little off, and you run out. he'd say that isn't prayer failing. that's where it starts to be true. each thing you stop claiming to know brings you a little closer.\nso one of them says too much and the other says nothing, and both are trying to get out of the way.","source":"maimonides, the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 58 and 59","sourceRef":"guideperplexed"},"question":"when you go quiet this morning, which one shows up first: the one who loves, or the one who knows better?","caption":"follow one man's babble far enough and it turns into the other man's silence.","refs":{"rumi":{"name":"rumi","lines":"jalal ad-din rumi, 1207 to 1273. a muslim scholar and preacher in konya, a city in the middle of what's now turkey. a serious man with students, the kind of religious professor people came to for rulings on how to live.\nat thirty seven a wandering holy man named shams stopped him in the street, and the two of them were inseparable for a couple of years. his students hated it. then shams vanished for good. some say he was murdered, some say he just walked away. the scholar fell apart and came back a poet.\nhe spent the rest of his life turning that loss into poems, tens of thousands of lines. in america he shows up on wedding programs, in soft versions with the islam taken out. in the originals the god he's in love with is allah, and the lines are full of the quran, islam's holy book.\nthat's why he's on this page. he'd been the man with the rulings. he knew everything moses knows in this story, and he wrote it so the one who knows the rules is the one who gets corrected.","where":"the masnavi for the stories. the essential rumi, coleman barks's collection, is the easy doorway, knowing it's loose. jawid mojaddedi's masnavi goes straight from the persian and keeps the rhymes."},"masnavi":{"name":"the masnavi","lines":"rumi's big book. six volumes, about twenty five thousand rhyming couplets in persian. he spoke it aloud over the last dozen or so years of his life, walking around konya, while his friend husam al-din wrote it down. sufis, the people inside islam who go after god directly, call it the quran in persian.\nit's stories inside stories. a king and a slave girl, a merchant and a parrot, dirty jokes, and then a passage that stops you cold. moses and the shepherd is one of them, in the second volume. it's a story in a poem, told to make a point, and everything god says in it is rumi's telling.\nthe page leaves out moses's argument, and it's a real one. hands and feet are fine for us, he says, but said of god they're filth. whatever has a body got born, and whatever got born isn't god. that's the right page, almost word for word. rumi gives the careful man his full say and then has god side with the shepherd anyway.\nit leaves out the ending too. moses runs into the desert after the shepherd and tells him he's allowed. no rules, no method, say what your heart needs to say. and the shepherd says he's already gone past that.\nthe old full english translation is nicholson's, a hundred years old and stiff, all thee and thou. there are newer ones that read much better.","where":"the masnavi, book 2. the story starts at line 1720 in nicholson's numbering. god's answer to moses starts at line 1750."},"moses":{"name":"moses","lines":"the central figure of the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures that christians call the old testament. the story is set maybe thirty three hundred years ago. as it's told, he led a nation of slaves out of egypt and came down a mountain with the law.\nhe belongs to muslims too. in the quran, islam's holy book, he's musa, a prophet and messenger of god, and he's named there more than anyone else, a hundred and thirty six times. his title in islam means the one god spoke to.\nthat's why rumi picked him. if anybody knew the right way to talk to god, it was the man god talked to directly. and in this story he's the one who gets it wrong.\none more thing. as the bible tells it, he was out tending his father in law's sheep when the bush caught fire and the voice came. the man he tells to shut up has his old job.","where":"exodus 3 and 4 in the bible for the bush and the argument. today's story about him is rumi's, in the masnavi, book 2."},"maimonides":{"name":"maimonides","lines":"moses ben maimon, a rabbi and a doctor. a rabbi is a jewish teacher and judge of the law. born in cordoba, in muslim spain, in 1138, though older books say 1135. died in egypt in 1204. jews call him the rambam.\nhis family was driven out of spain when he was a boy, and they wandered for years before settling in egypt, next to cairo. his younger brother traded in gems and paid the bills, until his ship went down with the family's money on it. maimonides stayed in bed for about a year. then he got up and went to work as a doctor, and ended up a physician at the sultan's court.\nhe wrote the mishneh torah, a code of all of jewish law with the arguments taken out, and the guide for the perplexed, the hard book this page comes from. he lived his whole life among muslims and wrote his philosophy in arabic.\nhe also wrote a list of what a jew has to believe, and it starts with god existing, being one, and having no body. so a god with hair to comb isn't sweet to him. it's the exact mistake he wrote a whole book to get people out of.\nhe isn't sneering at the shepherd for being simple. he thinks the wrong picture puts you further from god, and that each thing you stop claiming gets you a little closer.","where":"the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 51 to 59, on what can and can't be said about god. his letters are the quick way to meet the man, a page or two each."},"guideperplexed":{"name":"the guide for the perplexed","lines":"maimonides' book of philosophy, finished around 1190 and written in arabic in hebrew letters. it's set up as a long letter to a former student, joseph ben judah, who couldn't see how the torah, the first five books of the hebrew bible, and the science of his day could both be true.\nits most famous move is about god. the bible's pictures of god aren't meant literally. god has no body. and the most you can truly say about god is what god is not. not dead. not weak. not ignorant. every no, he says, gets you a little nearer.\nchapters 58 and 59 of the first part are where he lands it. we can know that god is. we can't know what god is. so a longer list of praises is a worse one, and the best praise is to stop talking. he backs it with the story from the talmud and the line from the psalms on this page.\neven great, mighty and awesome, he says, we only get to say because moses said them first and the old sages put them in the prayers. nobody gets to add to the list.\nchristians read it too. thomas aquinas cites him as rabbi moses. and the shepherd in rumi's poem is exactly who it was written against.","where":"the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 58 and 59. m. friedländer's english translation is the old public one."},"psalms":{"name":"the psalms","lines":"a book of the bible made of a hundred and fifty songs and prayers, many credited to king david. they cover everything. praise, thanks, rage, grief, and god why have you left me. they're still sung in synagogues and monasteries every day.\nso it's a strange place to find a line against talking. psalm 65 opens, word for word in the hebrew, something like: to you, silence, praise.\nmost english bibles smooth it out. the king james has praise waiteth for thee. the standard jewish translation has praise befits you. a few keep the silence in.\nmaimonides read it straight. silence is praise to you. a hundred and fifty songs to god, and he goes and finds the one line that says the best song is none.","where":"psalm 65, the first line after the heading. it's verse 2 in jewish bibles and verse 1 in most christian ones, because they count the heading differently."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261005bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261005bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 80 252 160 260 260 256 S 420 254 520 256 S 700 258 800 256'/><path d='M 0 204 C 36 170 110 168 158 206'/><circle cx='72' cy='138' r='8'/><path d='M 72 146 L 72 166 M 72 166 L 67 180 M 72 166 L 78 180 M 72 152 L 83 156 M 84 134 L 84 181'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='238' rx='20' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 47 247 L 46 256 M 55 249 L 55 256 M 66 249 L 66 256 M 73 247 L 74 256 M 40 234 L 35 230'/><circle cx='85' cy='224' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 218 q -5 -8 -11 -7 M 87 217 q -2 -9 -8 -11 M 88 231 l 1 6'/><ellipse cx='160' cy='238' rx='20' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 147 247 L 146 256 M 155 249 L 155 256 M 166 249 L 166 256 M 173 247 L 174 256 M 140 234 L 135 230'/><circle cx='185' cy='226' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 182 220 q -5 -8 -11 -7 M 187 219 q -2 -9 -8 -11 M 188 233 l 1 6'/><circle cx='270' cy='172' r='14'/><ellipse cx='278' cy='177' rx='3' ry='4'/><path d='M 268 186 L 262 224 M 262 224 L 288 252 L 250 254 M 267 198 L 244 172 M 267 198 L 298 200'/><ellipse cx='318' cy='250' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='342' cy='250' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 313 247 L 320 253 M 337 247 L 344 253'/><path d='M 362 243 L 390 243 M 364 243 L 364 253 M 371 243 L 371 253 M 378 243 L 378 253 M 385 243 L 385 253'/><path d='M 286 170 C 300 150 318 138 326 150 C 334 164 308 168 314 146 C 320 118 350 112 356 128 C 362 146 334 148 342 124 C 350 96 384 92 388 110 C 392 128 366 126 374 106 C 382 84 410 84 424 96 C 438 108 450 84 468 92 C 484 99 496 88 512 92 C 526 95 536 91 548 92 L 580 92'/><path d='M 596 92 L 642 92' stroke-dasharray='11 13'/><path d='M 658 92 L 712 92' stroke-dasharray='1 16'/><circle cx='612' cy='172' r='14'/><path d='M 616 178 L 623 178'/><path d='M 612 186 L 612 228 M 612 228 L 638 228 L 640 256 M 612 228 L 630 233 L 630 256'/><path d='M 612 198 L 633 200 L 627 181 M 612 202 L 640 214 L 666 213'/><path d='M 597 233 L 624 233 M 601 233 L 599 256 M 620 233 L 621 256'/><path d='M 656 218 L 742 218 M 664 218 L 664 256 M 734 218 L 734 256'/><path d='M 678 215 Q 690 205 702 215 Q 714 205 726 215 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 752 256 L 752 246 L 790 246 L 790 256 M 757 246 L 757 237 L 786 237 L 786 246'/><\/g><g transform='translate(759,210) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i don't have to get it right to be heard.","id":"2026-10-05"},{"date":"2026-10-06","theme":"the priest who kept drinking","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"christian","text":"he never got sober for good.\n[[manning|brennan manning]] spent his life standing in front of crowds saying one thing. god loves you as you are, not as you should be.\nand the whole time he was saying it, he was drinking. he'd preach it, check into a motel, drink himself into a stupor, and fly to the next talk when the bender was over. he lied to the people who loved him. his marriage ended.\nnow, the mind wants a certain story here. the one where he hits bottom, gets clean, and then gets to talk about grace because he's earned it. we love that story. it lets us keep the idea that love comes after you get it together.\nhe didn't give us that one. near the end, old and sick, he wrote [[allisgrace|his last book]] and told on himself. the benders, the lying, all of it. and he still called the whole thing grace, which is the church's word for love you didn't earn.\nso look at the part of you that's waiting to be better before you'll let yourself be loved. the one that says, after i fix this. if love can't find the man in the motel, it was only ever a prize for people who were already fine. and none of us is one of those.","source":"brennan manning, all is grace · his last book, the memoir. then the ragamuffin gospel, chapter 1","sourceRef":"allisgrace"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"i do the thing i hate.\nthat's [[paul]]. he started churches across the roman world, about half the books in the new testament carry his name, and in the middle of [[romansletter|his longest letter]] he stops explaining and just tells the truth.\ni don't understand what i do. what i want to do, i don't. what i hate, i do.\nyou know this one from the inside. you make the promise at night, and you mean it, and by the next afternoon you're watching yourself do it again. you're the one doing it and the one watching, both.\nhe doesn't tidy it up. he writes it in the present tense, and then he just yells. what a wretched man i am. who's going to get me out of this body?\nand here's the part to stay with. he doesn't follow it with a plan. no method, no trying harder. a verse later he says there's no condemnation, and he says it before anything has been fixed.\nthat's a different order than the one we run on. we think, first i change, then i'm okay. he's saying you're held first, and whatever changes, changes from there. the man who built the church had your problem, and he never says it went away.","source":"romans 7:15 to 8:1 · read 7 and 8 together, don't stop at the chapter break","sourceRef":"romansletter"},"question":"what have you sworn off more than once?","caption":"the page one of them wrote is the curb the other one sat down on.","refs":{"manning":{"name":"brennan manning","lines":"born richard manning in new york in 1934. a marine, then a franciscan priest, ordained in 1963. the franciscans are an order of friars, men who promise to own nothing. he took the name brennan along the way. he was funny, charming, and a very good preacher.\nhe was also an alcoholic. he went into treatment in the seventies, got sober, relapsed, got sober, relapsed. in 1982 he left the franciscans and the priesthood to marry a woman named roslyn, which a catholic priest isn't allowed to do. so for the years he was famous he was an ex-priest. the marriage ended in divorce in 2000.\nin 1990 he wrote the ragamuffin gospel, the book that told a generation of church kids that god loved them as screwups, not after they got it together. then he spent twenty years on the road saying it to crowds. by his own account he'd stand up and preach it, check into a motel, drink himself into a stupor, and fly to the next talk when the bender was over.\nat the end he was nearly blind and could hardly speak or get around. the cause of death on record is wernicke-korsakoff syndrome, a brain disorder the drinking may have sped along. he died in 2013.\nthe line on this page was his whole message. he said it in talks for decades, and it's in the memoir too. he said it like a man who needed it to be true.","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, the first chapter. then all is grace, which is the same man telling you what was going on while he wrote the first one."},"allisgrace":{"name":"all is grace","lines":"brennan manning's memoir, from 2011, and his last book. he wrote it near the end with a co-writer, john blase, when he was old and sick. he died two years later.\nit admits what the crowds didn't know. he'd been drinking far more than anyone knew, all through the years he was on the road preaching grace, and lying to cover it. friends had been confronting him about the lying for years. it wasn't so much the big lies, he writes, as the little ones, the need to lie at all.\nit tells the marriage too. the last years before the divorce, he says, he was traveling way too much, isolated and drunk.\nwhat it doesn't do is take the message back. he calls his life a witness to vulgar grace, a grace that amazes and offends. to live by grace, he says, means owning the whole story, the light side and the dark.\nthe words all is grace are also how the diary of a country priest ends, a 1936 french novel about a sick young priest in a village that doesn't want him. they're the last thing he says as he dies.","where":"all is grace: a ragamuffin memoir, by brennan manning with john blase. philip yancey's foreword is the short version."},"paul":{"name":"paul","lines":"a strict religious jew in the first century, from tarsus, near the south coast of what's now turkey. he went after the first followers of jesus, dragging them off to jail, until, as the book of acts tells it, a light knocked him flat on the road to damascus. he got up blind and switched sides.\nhe never met jesus in the flesh. he spent the next thirty years walking and sailing around the roman empire, starting little churches in people's houses, getting beaten, getting shipwrecked, and writing letters back to those churches when they screwed up, which was constantly. those letters are the oldest christian writing there is, older than the gospels.\nso when the page says he more or less built the church, and calls him the founder, that's what it means. christians would say jesus is the founder. paul is the one who carried it across the empire and wrote it down.\nhe had something wrong with him that he never names. a thorn in the flesh, he calls it. he says he begged god three times to take it away and got told, my grace is enough for you. he stopped asking.\nthat's the man talking in romans 7. tradition says rome cut his head off under the emperor nero.","where":"romans 7 and 8 for today. acts 17 for his speech in athens. then philippians, the whole thing. he wrote it from prison and it's short."},"romansletter":{"name":"the letter to the romans","lines":"paul's longest letter, written around the year 57 to the christians in rome, a church he hadn't started and hadn't visited yet. it's his big statement of what he believed.\nchapter 7 is where he says he can't do the good he wants and keeps doing the thing he hates. he cries out, who will rescue me. then comes one line of thanks to god, and then he says the problem over again, flat: in my mind i serve god's law, and in my flesh i serve sin. chapter 8 opens right there, with no condemnation.\npeople have argued for a very long time about who the i is. one side says it's paul himself, a christian, right now. he switches from the past tense into the present for this stretch, and augustine, luther and calvin all read it that way. the other side says he's speaking as a man before christ, or without him, to show what the law can't fix, and that chapter 8 is the way out. serious scholars sit on both sides.\nthe page takes the first reading. it's fair to know there's a second.\nchapter 8 ends with nothing in all creation being able to separate us from the love of god. it's the letter that set off martin luther and the whole protestant reformation fifteen hundred years later.","where":"romans 7:14 to 8:4 for today, then both chapters whole. the last two verses of chapter 8 are the ones people memorize."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261006bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261006bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 266 L 150 266 M 752 266 L 800 266'/><path d='M 150 250 C 230 247 310 253 400 250 S 600 252 724 250 L 724 266 C 640 268 500 263 400 266 S 230 263 150 266 C 138 266 138 250 150 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='738' cy='252' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 738 252 m -6 0 a 6 6 0 1 1 6 6'/><path d='M 470 258 L 500 258 M 516 258 L 560 258 M 576 258 L 596 258 M 690 258 L 712 258'/><g opacity='.5'><path d='M 70 40 l -6 16 M 140 78 l -6 16 M 200 34 l -6 16 M 290 66 l -6 16 M 350 28 l -6 16 M 100 124 l -6 16 M 330 120 l -6 16 M 440 50 l -6 16 M 520 96 l -6 16 M 580 36 l -6 16 M 690 70 l -6 16 M 760 34 l -6 16 M 470 140 l -6 16 M 740 130 l -6 16'/><\/g><path d='M 168 250 L 168 218 L 214 218 L 214 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 182 218 C 182 207 200 207 200 218 M 168 230 L 214 230'/><circle cx='276' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 264 196 L 246 246 M 246 246 L 288 210 L 298 250 M 246 246 L 278 216 L 284 250 M 261 206 L 290 212 L 304 228'/><path d='M 308 243 L 330 243 L 336 245.5 L 344 245.5 L 344 248.5 L 336 248.5 L 330 250 L 308 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='640' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 640 190 L 640 232 M 640 232 C 620 232 610 248 625 250 M 640 232 C 660 232 670 248 655 250'/><path d='M 640 204 L 620 198 L 628 180 M 640 206 L 664 224 L 674 244 M 674 247 L 682 232'/><\/g><g transform='translate(88,248) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i am loved before i'm fixed.","id":"2026-10-06"},{"date":"2026-10-07","theme":"holy or a sack of meat","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"other","text":"your body is the holy thing.\n[[whitman|walt whitman]] has [[bodyelectric|a poem]] where he does something very simple. he reads out the human body, part by part, the way a preacher reads scripture. head, neck, ears. ribs, belly, hips. the armpit. the man-balls and the man-root. his words.\nhe doesn't skip anything and he doesn't blush. and it cost him. [[leavesofgrass|leaves of grass]], the book it's in, most likely got him fired from a government job, and years later a prosecutor in boston called it obscene. he didn't back off.\nif anything is sacred, he wrote, the human body is sacred. and then he goes further. these parts aren't only the body, he says. they're the soul.\nmost of us were handed the other idea a long time ago. that the real you is somewhere else, somewhere cleaner, and this is the meat it rides around in. so we live a few inches above ourselves. we manage the body. we judge it in mirrors. we wait for it to be different.\nhe says no. this is you. this is where it's all happening. so just for today, be in it. feel your feet on the floor and the breath going in. the sweat counts.","source":"walt whitman, i sing the body electric, in leaves of grass · read the last section out loud","sourceRef":"bodyelectric"},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"it's a sack of shit. literally.\nthe [[buddha]] gave his monks [[satipatthana|a practice]] that also goes through the body part by part. hair, nails, teeth, skin. and he doesn't make any of it pretty. kidneys, bowels, shit, bile, snot, pus, blood, sweat, spit, piss.\nlook at it, he says, like a sack of mixed grain, open at both ends, and you're just sorting through what's in it.\nand then further. picture a corpse, three days dead, swollen, leaking. then one the crows and dogs have been at. then bones. and each time you tell yourself, quietly, my body is the same kind of thing. it's headed there.\nnow, this can sound like hatred, and it isn't. he'd tried hating the body, years of starving it, and he quit because it got him nowhere. this is something else. it's taking the spell off.\nbecause look how much of a day goes to this body. how it looks, how it's holding up, whether somebody else's is better. look how fast we fall for a face. see it plainly and there's some room. you can still take care of it. you just quit being so damn impressed. with yours, and with hers.","source":"satipatthana sutta, majjhima nikaya 10 · the sections on the body, then the whole thing. it's the root of mindfulness practice","sourceRef":"satipatthana"},"question":"which do you need to hear today, that it's holy or that it's meat?","caption":"same body, same list. from one side it's lit up and from the other it's bones.","refs":{"whitman":{"name":"walt whitman","lines":"born on long island in 1819, raised in brooklyn, out of school at eleven. a printer's boy, a teacher, a newspaperman. at thirty six he paid to print a thin book of poems that sounded like nothing before it, loose and huge and in love with everybody. then he wrote rave reviews of it himself and got them printed without his name.\nduring the civil war he spent about three years in the army hospitals in washington, sitting with wounded and dying soldiers, bringing oranges and tobacco, writing letters home for the ones who couldn't. so when he talks about bodies he isn't being dreamy. he'd seen a great many of them torn up.\nin 1865 he had a clerk's job at the department of the interior. the new secretary, james harlan, came across his copy of leaves of grass at his desk, called the book obscene, and fired him the next day. harlan said afterward it was only about saving money, and the record is murky, but the book is most likely why. friends had him in another government job within a day.\nin 1882 the district attorney in boston told his publisher the book was obscene literature and sent a list of poems and lines to cut, this poem among them. whitman wrote back that the whole list was rejected and wouldn't be thought of under any circumstances. the publisher quit. he found another one, and that edition went through five printings of a thousand copies each.\nhe kept revising that one book until he died in 1892.","where":"song of myself, sections 1, 6 and 52, to get his voice. then specimen days, his prose memoir, for the war hospitals."},"bodyelectric":{"name":"i sing the body electric","lines":"a poem in nine sections from leaves of grass. it was one of the twelve poems in the first edition, in 1855, and like the rest it had no title. the next year he called it poem of the body and added the ending, the long list. the title it has now, and the first line that gives it, came in 1867.\nthe list is the last section. it starts o my body and then just names things, top to bottom. head, neck, hair, ears. upper-arm, armpit, elbow-socket. the lung-sponges, the stomach-sac, the bowels sweet and clean. food, drink, pulse, digestion, sweat, sleep, walking, swimming. womanhood, and all that is a woman.\nthe line about the body being sacred comes near the end of the section before it. the last two lines of the poem are the ones the page ends on. these are not the parts and poems of the body only, but of the soul. o i say now these are the soul.\nralph waldo emerson, the most famous american writer of the day and the man who'd written him the great fan letter, argued that he should cut the most sexual and bodily poems. whitman's account is that he heard him out and decided to disobey all and pursue his own way.\nso the list is the argument. he doesn't say the body is nice. he says there's nowhere else the soul is, and nothing on the list is too low to name.","where":"leaves of grass, in the group of poems called children of adam. section 9 is the list. the sacred line is near the end of section 8."},"leavesofgrass":{"name":"leaves of grass","lines":"walt whitman's book of poems, first printed at his own expense in 1855 with twelve poems, no author's name on the title page, and a picture of him in work clothes. it sounded like nothing before it. long loose lines, the body and sex and the whole country crammed in.\nhe spent the rest of his life adding to it and rewriting it. same title, a bigger book each time. the last edition, which he approved from his deathbed in 1892, had about four hundred poems.\nit got him in trouble twice. it's most likely what got him fired in washington in 1865, and in 1882 the district attorney in boston called it obscene literature and his publisher gave it up.\nit's the root of most american poetry since. and the part that upset people is the part on this page, a man naming his body out loud and calling it holy.","where":"leaves of grass, the last edition, from 1892, for the whole thing. the 1855 first edition is only twelve poems."},"buddha":{"name":"the buddha","lines":"siddhattha gotama, born about twenty five hundred years ago near what's now the border of nepal and india. buddha isn't a name. it's a title, and it means the one who woke up.\nthe story, written down centuries later, says he was a rich man's son whose father tried to keep him from ever seeing anything sad. it didn't work. out in the town he saw an old man, then a sick man, then a corpse being carried off. at twenty nine he left home to find out why we suffer and whether it can stop.\nhe tried hating the body first. six years of starving himself, the way the holy men of his day did, and it got him nowhere but thin. he ate a bowl of rice, sat down under a tree, and woke up. then he taught for forty five years.\nso the man on this page isn't disgusted by bodies. he'd tried that and quit. the corpse is what sent him out the door in the first place, and here he's handing his monks the same sight on purpose.\nhe didn't ask anyone to believe him. he said try it and see.","where":"the satipatthana sutta for today. the dhammapada, a short book of his sayings in verse, is where most people start."},"satipatthana":{"name":"the satipatthana sutta","lines":"a sutta is one of the buddha's recorded talks, memorized and chanted by monks for centuries before anyone wrote it down. this one is number 10 in the majjhima nikaya, the middle length talks, in the pali canon, the oldest complete collection of his teachings. the name means something like the foundations of mindfulness. he gives it to his monks in a town called kammasadhamma.\nit's instructions for paying attention to four things: the body, feelings, the mind, and the patterns his teaching points out. modern insight meditation is built on it, and the word mindfulness is a translation of the word in its title.\nthe body section starts gently, with breathing, then your posture, then whatever you happen to be doing. then it turns. thirty one things the body is made of, from head hair down to urine, sorted like grain out of a sack. then the body as earth, water, fire and wind, laid out like a butcher's cuts of a cow at a crossroads.\nthen the corpses, nine of them. as if you were to see one, it says, left out in the open. three days dead and bloated. picked at by crows and dogs. a skeleton with some flesh still on. bones scattered, bones gone white, bones gone to powder. after each one: this body too. such is its nature, such is its future.\nwhitman reads out his list and says holy. the buddha reads out nearly the same list and says look, it's just this. neither of them looks away.","where":"majjhima nikaya 10. a longer version is digha nikaya 22. thanissaro bhikkhu's english translation is free to read; he calls it frames of reference."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261007bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261007bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 90 253 180 259 280 256 S 460 254 560 256 S 720 258 800 256'/><circle cx='70' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 26 256 l -6 -24 M 36 256 l 2 -34 M 46 256 l 9 -22 M 94 256 l -7 -26 M 104 256 l 1 -36 M 114 256 l 9 -20 M 224 256 l -7 -24 M 234 256 l 2 -32 M 244 256 l 9 -20 M 298 256 l -6 -22 M 308 256 l 2 -30 M 340 256 l 7 -18'/><circle cx='170' cy='112' r='14'/><path d='M 148 102 L 192 102 M 157 102 C 159 86 181 86 183 102'/><path d='M 170 126 L 170 190 M 170 190 L 156 254 M 170 190 L 186 254 M 170 142 L 216 120 M 170 144 L 150 168 L 160 184'/><path d='M 154 180 L 170 180 L 170 196 L 154 196 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='400' cy='112' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='407' cy='109' r='3.5'/><path d='M 403 122 l 0 5 M 408 121 l 0 5'/><path d='M 400 128 L 400 200'/><path d='M 400 144 L 368 118 M 400 200 L 382 254'/><path d='M 352 96 L 344 88 M 342 116 L 330 114 M 366 84 L 362 72 M 384 78 L 382 66 M 348 140 L 336 146'/><path d='M 400 156 C 409 153 416 156 419 162 M 400 168 C 409 165 416 168 419 174 M 400 180 C 408 177 414 180 416 185'/><path d='M 400 142 L 437 148 M 442 153 L 446 190 M 400 200 L 416 224 M 418 230 L 420 254'/><circle cx='440' cy='149' r='3'/><circle cx='417' cy='227' r='3'/><path d='M 548 256 C 540 236 544 222 556 214 L 584 214 C 596 222 600 236 592 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 556 214 C 560 206 580 206 584 214'/><circle cx='606' cy='250' r='1.6'/><circle cx='616' cy='252' r='1.6'/><circle cx='611' cy='244' r='1.6'/><circle cx='524' cy='252' r='1.6'/><circle cx='534' cy='247' r='1.6'/><circle cx='680' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 680 194 L 680 236 M 680 236 C 660 236 650 252 665 255 M 680 236 C 700 236 710 252 695 255 M 680 210 L 666 236 M 680 210 L 694 236'/><\/g><g transform='translate(58,229) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can live in this body all the way, and hold it lightly.","id":"2026-10-07"},{"date":"2026-10-08","theme":"eat something, go to sleep","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"take a nap.\n[[elijah]] had just had the biggest day of his life. fire from heaven, the whole country watching, and he'd won.\nand the very next thing, [[jezebel|the queen]] sends word that he'll be dead by this time tomorrow. one sentence. and the man who called down fire runs for his life. a day into the desert he sits down under a bush and says, enough. kill me.\nyou'd think a win like that would carry you a while. it doesn't. anybody who has poured everything into one day knows the day after. you're emptied out, and the mind takes the emptiness and builds a whole case out of it. i'm no good. it was all for nothing. and it feels like the truth about your life.\nwatch what heaven does with that. no lecture. nobody argues with his conclusions. he falls asleep, and [[angel|an angel]] wakes him and says, get up and eat. there's bread baking on hot stones and a jar of water. he eats and lies back down. the angel comes a second time. eat more. the road's too long for you.\nso before you believe what your mind is telling you about your whole life, check. have you eaten? have you slept? a man asked to die, and heaven sent a meal and a second nap.","source":"1 kings 19:1 to 8 · read chapter 18 first, so you know what he'd just pulled off","sourceRef":"elijah"},"right":{"lineage":"tao","text":"stop before it's full.\nthe [[taoteching|tao te ching]] has a chapter, the ninth, that's only a few lines long, and all of it is about the moment just past enough.\nkeep filling a cup and it spills. keep sharpening a blade and the edge won't last. fill your house with gold and you can't guard it. get proud of what you've got and you've set up your own fall.\nand then: do the work, then step back. it calls that the way of heaven.\nnow, this goes against something deep in us. we were raised on the extra mile. one more rep, one more hour, one more thing before bed. that's where we think the virtue lives, in the part that hurts. and sometimes it does. but notice how often the last push isn't devotion at all. it's fear. fear that what you did wasn't enough, or that you aren't.\n[[laotzu|lao tzu]], the man the book is credited to, says the extra mile is where you ruin the knife. there's a point where the work is done, and going on past it is only not being able to stop.\ncan you feel that point today? you already ran the race. go to bed.","source":"tao te ching, chapter 9 · any translation. it's shorter than this page","sourceRef":"taoteching"},"question":"is it really that bad, or are you just hungry and tired?","caption":"the water that gets one of them back on his feet goes into the other one's cup, and he stops it short of full.","refs":{"elijah":{"name":"elijah","lines":"a prophet in the hebrew bible, from around the 800s bc. a prophet there isn't a fortune teller. it's someone who speaks for god, usually to people in charge who don't want to hear it. elijah is the loud one, fire and thunder.\nwhat he'd just pulled off: the queen had brought in a god called baal and was killing off the prophets of israel's god. elijah had the whole country called up to mount carmel for a contest. four hundred and fifty prophets of baal on one side and him, alone, on the other. whichever god answers with fire is god. his did. fire came down and burned up the offering, the wood and the stones.\nthe story doesn't stop at the win, and it isn't pretty. he had the four hundred and fifty seized and killed. then he ran ahead of the king's chariot through a downpour, all the way to the town of jezreel. the next thing he hears is the queen's message.\nso the man under the bush isn't soft. he's spent. he says he's no better than his fathers and asks to be done.\nafter the two naps and the two meals he walks forty days to a mountain, and there god does talk to him. not in the wind, not in the earthquake, not in the fire. in a small quiet sound after all of it. the food came first.","where":"1 kings 18 for the fire on mount carmel. 1 kings 19 for the bush, the angel, and the quiet sound on the mountain."},"jezebel":{"name":"jezebel","lines":"the queen of israel in the 800s bc. she was a princess from the city of tyre, the daughter of its king, married to israel's king ahab.\nshe brought her own gods with her, baal and asherah. as the book of kings tells it, hundreds of their prophets ate at her table and she was killing off the prophets of israel's god. elijah's contest on the mountain was aimed at her, and it ended with him slaughtering her prophets.\nso her message answers in kind. may the gods do worse to me, she says, if by this time tomorrow i haven't made you like one of them.\nthat's all it takes. the man who'd just called down fire in front of the whole country hears one sentence from her and runs for his life. winning the big one didn't leave him anything for the day after.\nthe story was written by her enemies, and scholars say it shows. in it she ends up thrown from a window, and the dogs get her.","where":"1 kings 18 and 19. she's named at 18:4 and 18:19, and her message to elijah is 19:2."},"angel":{"name":"the angel","lines":"the hebrew word is malakh, and it means messenger. that's the whole word. the story gives it no wings and no glow. something touches him, says a few words, and there's food.\nthree verses earlier jezebel sends a messenger to elijah with the death threat. same hebrew word. english bibles call hers a messenger and god's an angel, but the text doesn't draw that line. one messenger tells him he'll be dead by tomorrow. the next one brings breakfast.\nlook at what this one says. get up and eat. the second time: get up and eat, or the road will be too much for you. nothing about god, no correction, nothing to believe. bread and water, twice.\nthat's why it's on this page. the first help heaven sends a man who wants to die is a meal and more sleep.","where":"1 kings 19:5 to 7. jezebel's messenger is at 19:2."},"taoteching":{"name":"the tao te ching","lines":"the short chinese book credited to lao tzu. eighty one tiny chapters, about five thousand characters in all. the title means something like the book of the way and its power. it probably took shape over a couple of centuries before about 250 bc.\nit's about water, emptiness, not forcing, and how the soft outlasts the hard. it's also, oddly, advice to rulers, telling them the less they meddle the better things go.\nchapter 9 is five plain statements. a vessel is better left short of the brim. a point sharpened too far won't keep its edge. a hall full of gold and jade can't be kept safe. pride in wealth and rank brings its own disaster. when the work is done, withdraw. that last one, it says, is the way of heaven.\ntranslators don't agree on the wording, and you can watch it happen here. the old scholars' versions have a vessel you shouldn't fill. a loose modern one has a bowl that spills and a knife that goes blunt. the page takes its pictures from that one. the point doesn't move.\nit's one of the most translated books in the world. you can read the whole thing in an evening, and people spend their whole lives on it.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 9. d.c. lau's translation is the plain scholar's one, james legge's is the old one, stephen mitchell's is loose and pretty."},"laotzu":{"name":"lao tzu","lines":"the name given to the old chinese sage credited with the tao te ching. it just means old master. the dates people give him run from the sixth century bc to the fourth to never.\nthe story is that he kept the royal library for the zhou kings, got sick of the whole thing, and headed west. old paintings show him riding an ox. at the last gate out of the country the guard wouldn't let him leave until he wrote down what he knew. he wrote a short book and went on, and nobody knows where he ended up.\nthere may never have been a lao tzu. scholars are split on it, and the book is most likely a collection that grew over time. so when the page says lao tzu says, read it as the book says.\nthe legend fits today's chapter anyway. a man does his work at court for a long time, sees it's finished, and leaves without waiting around for the credit. do the work, then step back.","where":"the tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4, then 8, 9, 11, 76 and 78. each chapter is a few lines."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261008bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261008bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 90 253 180 259 280 256 S 460 254 560 256 S 720 258 800 256'/><path d='M 700 50 C 676 58 672 92 700 102 C 684 90 684 62 700 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 256 C 88 220 62 196 40 186 M 96 256 C 94 212 88 176 78 152 M 96 256 C 102 214 116 180 134 160 M 96 256 C 114 224 150 204 186 200'/><path d='M 52 188 l -6 -8 M 66 200 l -8 -6 M 84 170 l -8 -5 M 86 192 l 7 -6 M 120 182 l 8 -3 M 110 202 l 8 -4 M 150 208 l 4 -8 M 170 203 l 5 -8'/><circle cx='128' cy='252' r='3'/><circle cx='138' cy='253' r='3'/><circle cx='148' cy='252' r='3'/><ellipse cx='138' cy='244' rx='13' ry='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='176' cy='242' r='13'/><path d='M 172 244 q 3 3 6 0'/><path d='M 189 243 L 244 245 M 244 245 L 268 228 L 292 254 M 244 245 L 284 250 M 202 243 L 214 254 L 232 254'/><circle cx='330' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 330 164 L 330 214 M 330 214 L 318 256 M 330 214 L 342 256 M 330 180 L 364 172 M 330 182 L 312 206'/><path d='M 360 158 C 354 174 368 186 382 176 L 376 154 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 382 168 C 404 162 432 178 451 204' stroke-dasharray='9 7'/><path d='M 441 206 L 445 226 L 459 226 L 463 206' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 444 217 L 460 217'/><circle cx='540' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 536 198 L 522 246 M 522 246 L 562 228 L 580 256 M 522 246 L 552 238 L 566 256'/><path d='M 532 212 L 494 224 L 463 218 M 532 210 L 508 196 L 504 174'/><path d='M 648 251 L 694 246 L 706 250 L 694 254 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 648 251 L 630 252'/><path d='M 722 256 L 724 246 L 752 246 L 754 256 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(122,133) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i've done enough for today. i can rest.","id":"2026-10-08"},{"date":"2026-10-09","theme":"grading the trees","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"quit grading the trees.\nsomebody once asked [[ramdass|ram dass]] how to stop being so hard on themselves. he didn't give them a technique. he sent them to the woods.\nwhen you walk in the woods, he said, you see all kinds of trees. some are bent and some are straight. some are evergreens and some have dropped everything. and you look at the crooked one and you don't get upset about it. you sort of understand. it didn't get enough light over there, so it leaned and grew that way. you allow it. \"you appreciate the tree.\"\nthen you walk back into town and it all falls apart. now everybody is too something. too loud, too slow, too needy, wrong about everything. the judging mind comes back on like a porch light. so he made a practice of it. he'd [[trees|turn people into trees]]. look at the person and wonder what bent them.\nyou know who gets graded hardest. it isn't the neighbor. it's the one in the mirror at five in the morning, already behind. that one grew toward whatever light there was, too.\ntry it once today, on purpose. pick the person who's bugging you most. it might be you. and before the grade comes out, look for which way the light was.","source":"ram dass, from his talks · his foundation keeps this one under the title judging less harshly","sourceRef":"trees"},"right":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"some things you don't let be.\n[[amos]] was a shepherd. he also looked after fig trees, which is a nice detail for today. he wasn't a priest, and he'd tell you so. he walked into a rich town in a good year, when business was strong and the worship was beautiful, and he said the thing nobody had hired him to say.\nhe said god was sick of it. i hate your festivals. i can't stand your solemn gatherings. keep your offerings and spare me the noise of your songs. and then the line people still carry into the street. let [[justice]] roll down like water.\nbecause out behind all that music, poor people were being sold for the price of a pair of sandals, and a judge could be bought. he looked at that and he did not say, well, that's just how they grew. he graded it, out loud. and the priest in charge told him to go home.\nthere's a part of you that knows the difference between a person who is bent and a thing that is being done to somebody. the first one gets your patience. the second one gets your voice.\nnotice today where you've been calling it acceptance, when really you just didn't want the trouble.","source":"amos 5:21 to 24 · read the whole chapter, then 7:10 to 15, where the priest tells him to leave","sourceRef":"amos"},"question":"who needs your patience today, and what needs your voice?","caption":"the water one of them is shouting for runs downhill to the roots of the other one's crooked tree.","refs":{"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nthe short version of what he brought home from india in 1967 was two instructions from an old man in a blanket. love everyone, and tell the truth. he spent the next fifty years finding out how hard the first one is.\nthe trees are how he worked at it. he didn't claim to be above any of this. he said the judging mind came on for him the minute he got near people, the same as it does for you, and that turning people into trees was something he had to practice.","where":"be here now, the first part, which is his own story. then still here, the book he wrote after his stroke. his foundation keeps the trees answer under the title judging less harshly."},"trees":{"name":"turning people into trees","lines":"somebody asked ram dass how to judge themselves less harshly, and this was his answer. in the woods you see bent trees and straight ones and you don't hold it against any of them. you figure each one grew the way it did for a reason, and you let it be a tree. around people that goes right out the window. so he practiced looking at people the way he looked at trees.\nnotice what it is and what it isn't. it isn't approving of everything somebody does. it's dropping the grade long enough to see what shaped them. the understanding comes first, and whatever you do next comes out different because of it.\nthis place is named for it. it's the same forest whether you're walking through trees or through people. and it's why the other page is here today, because a practice this gentle needs somebody across the fold asking what happens when the thing in front of you isn't a tree. it's a man with a saw.","where":"the answer is on his foundation's site, under the title judging less harshly. he tells it in several of his recorded talks too."},"amos":{"name":"amos","lines":"a shepherd and fig farmer who became a prophet in israel in the 700s bc, the first whose words got written down as a book. he told the rich that god hated their religious festivals and wanted justice instead.\nlet justice roll down like waters, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream. martin luther king quoted that line from amos again and again.\nhe came from tekoa, a village in the hills of the southern kingdom, and did his preaching up north at bethel, the royal shrine, during a long boom. the priest who ran the place, amaziah, reported him to the king and told him to go back south and earn his bread there. amos answered that he was no prophet and no prophet's son. he was a herdsman who tended sycamore figs, and god had taken him from following the flock.\nwhat set him off was specific. the poor sold for a pair of sandals. bribes taken, and people with no money turned away at the town gate, where cases got heard. he never says the worship was done badly. he says it can't cover for that.","where":"the book of amos, in the hebrew bible. it's nine short chapters. chapter 5 is today's, and 7:10 to 15 is the run-in with the priest."},"justice":{"name":"justice, the way the prophets mean it","lines":"the hebrew word is mishpat. it isn't a mood or a principle. it's what happens when a person with no money and no connections brings a complaint and gets a straight hearing. in amos's day that happened at the town gate, where the elders sat.\nin the line on the page he pairs it with a second word, tzedakah, which gets translated righteousness and means something closer to doing right by people. he wants the two of them to come down like water. not a trickle that dries up in summer. a stream that runs all year.\nmartin luther king quoted that line again and again, in sermons, in his letter from the birmingham jail, and in front of the lincoln memorial in 1963. a shepherd's sentence, about twenty seven hundred years old by then, and still the best thing anybody had to say.","where":"amos 5, the whole chapter. verse 24 is the line. then isaiah 1:10 to 17, where another prophet says the same thing not long after."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261009bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261009bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 70 253 141 259 235 256 S 399 254 470 256'/><circle cx='92' cy='66' r='26' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 126 66 L 138 66'/><path d='M 116 90 L 125 99'/><path d='M 92 100 L 92 112'/><path d='M 68 90 L 59 99'/><path d='M 58 66 L 46 66'/><path d='M 68 42 L 59 33'/><path d='M 92 32 L 92 20'/><path d='M 116 42 L 125 33'/><path d='M 318 256 C 322 206 300 176 262 150 C 244 138 232 124 224 104'/><path d='M 150 96 C 150 56 212 44 232 70 C 262 54 300 76 286 108 C 296 132 262 150 236 136 C 212 152 168 142 170 116 C 152 114 146 104 150 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 300 190 L 330 172'/><circle cx='196' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='193' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='203' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 194 178 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 196 188 L 196 221.44'/><path d='M 196 221.44 L 184 256 M 196 221.44 L 208 256'/><path d='M 196.0 196 L 182.0 224'/><path d='M 196.0 196 L 234.0 192'/><circle cx='193' cy='172' r='4.5' stroke-width='1.8'/><circle cx='203' cy='172' r='4.5' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 185 180 C 187 197 205 197 207 180' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 800 118 C 700 122 600 170 470 256'/><path d='M 42 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 51 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 60 256 l 4 -16'/><path d='M 410 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 419 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 800 150 C 720 150 640 196 560 226 C 500 248 420 262 340 268 L 300 280 C 420 288 520 268 600 244 C 680 214 740 190 800 186 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 420 262 q 14 -6 28 0 M 520 240 q 14 -6 28 0 M 640 204 q 14 -6 28 0 M 716 174 q 14 -6 28 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='640' cy='74' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='633' cy='72' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='643' cy='72' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 634 80 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 640 88 L 640 118.88'/><path d='M 640 118.88 L 628 152 M 640 118.88 L 652 152'/><path d='M 640.0 96 L 604.0 110'/><path d='M 640.0 96 L 660.0 118'/><path d='M 664 96 L 660 152'/><ellipse cx='730' cy='120' rx='17' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='713' cy='114' r='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 722 130 l -2 10 M 738 130 l 2 10'/><\/g><g transform='translate(318,149.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can let a person be a tree and still say what's wrong.","id":"2026-10-09"},{"date":"2026-10-10","theme":"where nobody knows you","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"go where nobody knows your name.\n[[mingyur|mingyur rinpoche]] had been somebody since he was a little boy. a title, a seat, monasteries, students all over the world. everything a person could want out of a religious life, and most of it handed to him before he could ask.\none night in june of 2011 he left a letter behind and walked out. no attendants, no plan, almost no money. he was going to do [[wanderingretreat|the old wandering retreat]], the way the yogis used to, and live on whatever the road gave him.\nand he tells what happened next without making himself look good. the first night he's on the floor of a train station, and he's hungry, and all he has to do is hold out a bowl. monks have done it for twenty five hundred years. he can't. he's too ashamed. the man who teaches letting go of the self can't stand to be seen as a beggar.\nthat's who he went out there to meet. not the hunger. the somebody.\nwe've all got one. the name, the job, the one who's known for being good at it. it's hard to see while it's working. notice today where you'd rather go hungry than be seen without it. you don't have to leave home. just catch the moment the somebody flinches.","source":"yongey mingyur rinpoche, in love with the world · the first chapters, where he leaves and can't beg","sourceRef":"mingyur"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"or go where everybody does.\n[[jesus]] is on a roll when he decides to go home. crowds along the lake, sick people getting well, everybody talking. then he walks back into [[nazareth]], the little village that raised him, and stands up to teach.\nat first they're amazed. then you can feel the room turn. wait a minute. isn't this the carpenter? mary's boy? we know his brothers. his sisters live right here. and the amazement sours into something else. who does he think he is.\n[[markgospel|mark]], who tells it, says a hard thing next. he says jesus could do almost nothing there. he laid his hands on a few sick people, and that was it. the same man, the same hands. and it says he was amazed too, at them.\nthey weren't bad people. they just already knew who he was. they had the file. and once a town has the file on you, it stops seeing you. it sees the file.\nnow, you are somebody's hometown. there are people you've known so long you quit looking years ago. your kid. your father. the one across the table.\npick one of them today and look again, as if you'd just met. see what they can do when you don't already know.","source":"mark 6:1 to 6 · six verses. then read matthew 13:53 to 58 and see what he changed","sourceRef":"markgospel"},"question":"who are you without the name, and who have you stopped seeing because of theirs?","caption":"one road. one of them is walking out of the place where everybody knows him, and the other is walking back into his.","refs":{"mingyur":{"name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","lines":"a tibetan monk born in 1975 who had panic attacks as a kid and figured out how to stop them by sitting still and letting them in. now he teaches that all over the world with a big grin. in his thirties he walked out on his own monasteries in the night to live as a beggar, and nearly died doing it. if you've ever had your chest lock up at 3am, he's the one on these pages who actually knows what that's like.\nrinpoche is a title, it means precious one, and he was given it as a small boy, named the rebirth of a teacher who'd died long before. so he never had a day as nobody. by 2011 he was running monasteries and teaching on several continents.\nthat's why the train station matters. the panic he'd beaten as a kid by letting it in. this was a different animal. this was finding out how much of him was the title. he says the shame was the teaching, and that later, lying sick in the dirt with every last bit of being a rinpoche gone, he'd never been happier.","where":"in love with the world, his book about the wandering years. the train station is in the first chapters. the joy of living, his first book, is the panic and the brain science."},"wanderingretreat":{"name":"the wandering retreat","lines":"in religion, a retreat is time you take out of ordinary life to practice, usually in silence, usually somewhere away from home. it can be a weekend at a monastery or years.\nthe tibetan three year retreat is the extreme version. three years, three months, in a closed building with a small group, meditating from before dawn into the night. the wandering retreat is the opposite, walking out with nothing and living as a beggar on the road.\na silent weekend at a retreat center is the gentle modern version, and it's harder than it sounds.\nhis started in june 2011, when he left his monastery in bodh gaya, the town in india where the buddha woke up, in the middle of the night, with a farewell letter left behind. within weeks he was close to dying of food poisoning at kushinagar, the town where the buddha died. he stayed out more than four years and then walked back in and went back to work.","where":"in love with the world tells his. for the old pattern, the life of milarepa, tibet's favorite saint, who lived in caves on nettles."},"jesus":{"name":"jesus","lines":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel who, around the year 30, spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided, until the roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him. strip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off. if what you got in church felt like rules, go read what he actually said.\nby chapter 6 of mark he's been out on the road for a while and it's going well. he's calmed a storm, healed a woman who touched his coat, and brought a twelve year old girl back. then he goes home, and home is the one place it doesn't work.\nhis own line about it has been a proverb ever since. a prophet gets honor everywhere except in his hometown, among his relatives, and in his own house. he's saying it about nazareth. it's true of most kitchens.","where":"the gospel of mark, start to finish. it's the shortest and you can read it in about ninety minutes. today's story is at the top of chapter 6."},"nazareth":{"name":"nazareth","lines":"the village in galilee, in the north of what's now israel, where jesus grew up. back then it was a few hundred people, a nowhere place nobody expected anything from.\ntoday it's a city of about eighty thousand, the biggest arab city in israel.\nthat's the point of the carpenter line. in a place that small everybody knew what he did for a living, who his mother was, and which houses his brothers and sisters lived in. mark even gives the brothers' names. james, joses, judas and simon.\nthe word mark uses for their reaction is that they took offense at him. it means they tripped over him. he was too familiar to be anything new.","where":"mark 6:1 to 6. luke tells a rougher version in chapter 4, where the hometown crowd tries to throw him off a cliff."},"markgospel":{"name":"the gospel of mark","lines":"the shortest of the four gospels, sixteen chapters, and probably the first written, around the year 70. it's fast. the word immediately shows up constantly. jesus heals, argues, gets misunderstood by everybody including his friends, and gets killed.\nthe oldest copies end with the women running from the empty tomb, scared, telling nobody. you can read the whole thing in about ninety minutes. start here.\nmark is blunt in a way the later gospels aren't. here he says jesus could do no deed of power in nazareth, apart from laying hands on a few sick people. matthew, who was working from mark, tells the same story and writes that he did not do many there. could not turned into did not.\nthat's why the page trusts mark on this one. he lets the story say what it says. what a person can do has something to do with who's looking at them.","where":"mark 6:1 to 6. then set it next to matthew 13:53 to 58 and watch one word change."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261010bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261010bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><path d='M 110 40 C 85.8 48.8 85.8 75.2 110 84 C 99.0 73.0 99.0 51.0 110 40 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 256 L 40 150 L 70 150 L 70 256 M 150 256 L 150 150 L 180 150 L 180 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 150 L 55 122 L 80 150 M 140 150 L 165 122 L 190 150'/><path d='M 62 150 L 158 150'/><path d='M 110 256 C 250 236 330 300 400 268 C 470 238 560 292 690 256' stroke-width='7' opacity='.28'/><circle cx='294' cy='180' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='292' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='302' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 293 186 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 294 194 L 292 224.88'/><path d='M 292 224.88 L 280 258 M 292 224.88 L 304 258'/><path d='M 293.5 202 L 277.5 232'/><path d='M 293.5 202 L 315.5 218'/><path d='M 310.8 196 C 312.4 208.8 331.6 208.8 333.2 196 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 548 214 L 548 174 L 608 174 L 608 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 540 174 L 578.0 150.0 L 616 174'/><path d='M 572.0 214 L 572.0 194.0 L 585.2 194.0 L 585.2 214'/><path d='M 628 206 L 628 154 L 704 154 L 704 206 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 620 154 L 666.0 123.6 L 712 154'/><path d='M 658.4 206 L 658.4 180.0 L 675.12 180.0 L 675.12 206'/><path d='M 722 214 L 722 174 L 778 174 L 778 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 714 174 L 750.0 151.6 L 786 174'/><path d='M 744.4 214 L 744.4 194.0 L 756.72 194.0 L 756.72 214'/><circle cx='470' cy='180' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='467' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='477' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 468 184 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 470 194 L 470 227.44'/><path d='M 470 227.44 L 458 262 M 470 227.44 L 482 262'/><path d='M 470.0 202 L 436.0 204'/><path d='M 470.0 202 L 504.0 204'/><circle cx='590' cy='208' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='582' cy='206' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='592' cy='206' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 583 214 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 590 222 L 590 240.08'/><path d='M 590 240.08 L 578 266 M 590 240.08 L 602 266'/><path d='M 590.0 230 L 576.0 252'/><path d='M 590.0 230 L 604.0 252'/><circle cx='664' cy='202' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='656' cy='200' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='666' cy='200' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 657 208 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 664 216 L 664 239.2'/><path d='M 664 239.2 L 652 268 M 664 239.2 L 676 268'/><path d='M 664.0 224 L 650.0 246'/><path d='M 664.0 224 L 678.0 246'/><circle cx='738' cy='216' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='730' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='740' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 731 222 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 738 230 L 738 242.96'/><path d='M 738 242.96 L 726 266 M 738 242.96 L 750 266'/><path d='M 738.0 238 L 724.0 260'/><path d='M 738.0 238 L 752.0 260'/><\/g><g transform='translate(153,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i'm more than what they know me as, and so is everybody i think i know.","id":"2026-10-10"},{"date":"2026-10-11","theme":"do it for nothing","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"muslim","text":"burn down heaven. put out hell.\nthe story about [[rabia]] goes like this. people in the city of [[basra]] see her hurrying down the street with a burning torch in one hand and a bucket of water in the other. somebody asks where she's headed with those.\nto heaven, she says, to set it on fire. and then to hell, to put it out. so they're both gone, and nobody has a reason left to look at god except god.\nnow, it's a late story, written down hundreds of years after she lived, and the scholars will tell you so. but it stuck to her because it fit. she'd been sold as a slave. she owned next to nothing, and when rich men offered to change that, she said no. what she's remembered for is one stubborn idea. if you love god to win the prize, or to stay out of the fire, you're doing business. you're not in love.\nyou can feel the difference in your own chest. there's the good you do with one eye on what comes back. the credit, the thank you, the points. and there's the other kind, which is quiet and doesn't keep a tab.\nwatch for the tab today. do one decent thing and tell nobody. see what's left when the reward is gone.","source":"the story is told of rabia of basra · margaret smith's rabi'a the mystic sorts the legend from the little that's known","sourceRef":"rabia"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"what if there's nothing to be scared into?\n[[bell|rob bell]] was the pastor of a church the size of an arena. ten thousand people a week. and somewhere in there he started asking, out loud, the one question you're not supposed to ask from that stage.\ndoes a loving god really torture most of the people who ever lived, forever? is that the good news?\nhe put it in a book called [[lovewins|love wins]], and his answer was probably not. love gets the last word. before the book was even in the stores, one of the most powerful pastors in his world sent out three words. farewell, rob bell. within a year he'd left his church.\nnow, why would a question cost a man that much? because he'd pulled the thread that holds a lot of religion together. if nobody's going to burn, what are we selling? what gets people in the door and keeps them in line?\nit's worth asking in your own life too. how much of what you do, you do because you're afraid of some fire. losing the job, the approval, the love. fear will get a person to behave. it has never once gotten anybody to love something.\nask it about one thing today. if nobody were keeping score, would i still do this? then go do the ones where the answer is yes.","source":"rob bell, love wins · read the preface and the chapter on hell","sourceRef":"lovewins"},"question":"what are you doing for the reward, or to stay out of trouble?","caption":"she's throwing water on the fire, and he's the one asking who needed it lit.","refs":{"rabia":{"name":"rabia of basra","lines":"a woman in eighth century iraq who, the stories say, was sold as a slave when she was a kid, got freed, and then stayed poor and single on purpose her whole life. she's the one credited with bringing the idea of loving god for god's own sake, not for a reward and not out of fear, into sufism, the mystical side of islam. almost everything about her was written down centuries after she died, so she's part person and part legend. it's the difference between the job you do for the paycheck and the thing you'd do for free, and she said god should be the second one.\nalmost everything told about her was written down long after she died, and none of it by her. the torch and the bucket show up with her name on them in the 1300s, more than five hundred years late. so the page says the story goes, and means it.\nwhat's solid is the idea she's credited with. the holy men of her city preached poverty and a trembling fear of god. she's remembered as the first to say you should love god for god, with no hope of paradise and no fear of hell mixed in. the prayer that goes with it asks to be burned in hell if she worships out of fear, and shut out of heaven if she worships to get in.","where":"margaret smith's rabi'a the mystic, from 1928, which says plainly which stories are legend. then the rabia chapter in attar's memorial of the saints, for the legend in full."},"basra":{"name":"basra","lines":"a city in the far south of what's now iraq, on the shatt al-arab, the river that runs down into the persian gulf. it started in 636 as a camp for the muslim army and was made a proper city a few years later.\nby rabia's time it was one of the great cities of learning and trade in the islamic world, home to famous grammarians, writers and mystics.\nthe best known of those mystics before her was hasan al-basri, a preacher famous for his poverty and his dread of the last judgment. that was the style of the place. give up the world and tremble.\nso picture her idea landing there. a freed slave in a town full of men famous for being afraid of god says the fear is beside the point. the stories have her turning down the governor's gold and every proposal that came with it. she said her peace was in solitude.","where":"smith's rabi'a the mystic has a chapter on the city and the holy men around her."},"bell":{"name":"rob bell","lines":"an evangelical pastor from michigan who built one of the hottest churches in america before he was thirty five, then wrote a book asking whether god really sends most people to burn forever. the answer he gave, probably not, got him run out of the whole world he grew up in. now he lives in los angeles, does a podcast, and tours with comedians. if you were raised on hell and quietly stopped buying it, he's the guy who said it out loud from the stage and paid for it.\nwhat he did after matters as much as the book. he was forty one with young kids, no church, and no denomination to catch him. he didn't get bitter and he didn't go quiet. he kept saying the same thing he'd said from the big stage, just without the sound system.\na documentary about him came out in 2018. it's called the heretic, and he's fine with that. he says he's more interested in what's true than in who's allowed to say it.","where":"velvet elvis, then love wins. his podcast, the robcast, is where he's loosest."},"lovewins":{"name":"love wins","lines":"rob bell's 2011 book asking whether a loving god really sends most people to be tortured forever. his answer was probably not, that love gets the last word.\nnothing in the book was new. christians have argued it for two thousand years. but coming from a megachurch star it blew up. it made the cover of time magazine, and it ended his life as a pastor.\nthe three words came from john piper, one of the most powerful pastors in american evangelical christianity, the born again, bible first side of the protestant church. he sent them out before the book was in stores. it worked the way being thrown out of a church used to work, only faster.\nwhat the book threatened wasn't a doctrine so much as a motive. a great deal of preaching runs on the fear of where you'll end up. take the fire away and you have to give people some other reason to be good, and the only one left is that they want to.","where":"love wins, 2011. it's short. read the preface and the chapter on hell first."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261011bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261011bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><circle cx='170' cy='170' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='168' cy='168' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='178' cy='168' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 169 174 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 170 184 L 170 220.0'/><path d='M 170 220.0 L 154 256 M 170 220.0 L 186 256'/><path d='M 170.0 192 L 140.0 158'/><path d='M 170.0 192 L 204.0 186'/><path d='M 124 108 C 116 86 136 82 132 64 C 150 78 154 98 140 110 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 140 150 L 138 112'/><path d='M 196 166 L 222 166 L 218 196 L 200 196 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 166 C 202 152 216 152 222 166'/><path d='M 224 172 C 280 110 340 120 384 190' stroke-dasharray='9 8'/><path d='M 376 196 l 10 -8 M 388 204 l 12 -4 M 366 204 l 4 -12' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 356 256 C 330 220 352 196 372 170 C 376 196 392 190 398 150 C 420 176 414 200 430 206 C 436 190 446 186 452 172 C 468 204 470 232 446 256 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 384 256 C 374 236 390 222 400 204 C 412 224 424 236 414 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='580' cy='172' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='572' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='582' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='577' cy='178' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 580 186 L 580 220.72'/><path d='M 580 220.72 L 568 256 M 580 220.72 L 592 256'/><path d='M 580.0 194 L 546.0 200'/><path d='M 580.0 194 L 596.0 220'/><path d='M 523.6 187.0 L 552.4 187.0 L 552.4 205.0 L 523.6 205.0 Z M 538 187.0 L 538 205.0' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 636 256 L 636 200 L 692 200 L 692 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 628 200 L 700 200'/><circle cx='734' cy='210' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='726' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='736' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 727 216 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 734 224 L 734 235.68'/><path d='M 734 235.68 L 722 258 M 734 235.68 L 746 258'/><path d='M 734.0 232 L 718.0 262'/><path d='M 734.0 232 L 750.0 262'/><circle cx='774' cy='218' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='766' cy='216' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='776' cy='216' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 767 224 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 774 232 L 774 238.56'/><path d='M 774 238.56 L 762 258 M 774 238.56 L 786 258'/><path d='M 774.0 240 L 758.0 270'/><path d='M 774.0 240 L 790.0 270'/><\/g><g transform='translate(652,173.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can do the good thing for nothing.","id":"2026-10-11"},{"date":"2026-10-12","theme":"be useful, be useless","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"other","text":"get up. you've got work.\nhere's a man with every excuse to stay in bed. [[marcus|marcus aurelius]] is the emperor of rome. he's in his fifties, he's sick a lot, and he's spending his last years in army camps on a cold river at the edge of the empire, running a war he never wanted. nobody alive can make him do anything.\nand in [[meditations|the notebook he kept for himself]], the one we were never supposed to read, he writes down the argument he has with himself at dawn.\nyou don't want to get up. fine. so is this what you were made for? to lie under the blankets and stay warm? look at the plants, he says. the little birds, the ants, the spiders, the bees. every one of them up and doing its part to keep the world in order. and you won't do yours?\nbut it's nice here, the other voice says. and he answers, so you were born to feel nice?\ni love that the most powerful man on earth had to talk himself out of bed like the rest of us. and what he lands on isn't glory. it's that a person is for something. you have a part, and somebody's short if you don't show.\nso when the covers feel like the whole world this morning, don't argue with how you feel. ask what your part is today. then put your feet on the floor.","source":"marcus aurelius, meditations, book 5, section 1 · read all of book 5. gregory hays's translation","sourceRef":"meditations"},"right":{"lineage":"tao","text":"be good for nothing. live a long time.\n[[zhuangzi]] tells a story about a carpenter and his apprentice walking past an enormous old oak. it stands beside a village shrine, and it's so big a whole herd of oxen could shelter under it. people come from all over just to look. the apprentice can't stop staring. the carpenter doesn't even slow down.\nworthless, he says. make a boat out of it and it sinks. a coffin, it rots. a door, it sweats sap. that's [[uselesstree|a useless tree]]. that's how it got to be so old.\nthat night the tree comes to him in a dream, and it isn't pleased. what are you comparing me to, it says. those nice useful trees? the pear, the orange, the apple? the minute their fruit is ripe they get stripped and torn. their branches get broken. being useful makes their lives miserable, and they die young. i've been working at being useless for a long time. it's the most useful thing that ever happened to me.\nnow hold that next to all your getting up and showing up. how much of your worth have you tied to being needed? to being the one who delivers?\nsome part of you is not for anything. it was never lumber. sometime today, do one thing that's no use to anybody. and don't apologize for it.","source":"zhuangzi, chapter 4, the oak at the shrine · burton watson's translation. the story is a page long","sourceRef":"uselesstree"},"question":"what's your part today, and what part of you isn't for anything?","caption":"the emperor is getting out of bed to go do his part. the tree over his tent got that big by never doing one.","refs":{"marcus":{"name":"marcus aurelius","lines":"a roman emperor who spent the last ten years of his life in cold army camps running a war he never wanted, and at night wrote in a private notebook telling himself to be patient, be decent, and remember he'd be dead soon. he never meant anyone to read it, and now it's on every nightstand from locker rooms to silicon valley. picture the most powerful person on earth keeping a notes app full of pep talks to himself, and the pep talks turn out to be good. take from him that the people running the world have to talk themselves out of bed too.\nnothing went right. a war with parthia, the rival empire in what's now iran. the soldiers came home carrying a plague, probably smallpox, that killed people across the empire for years. then the germanic tribes came over the danube, the river that was rome's northern border, and he spent most of the last decade of his life in army camps along it, a sick, tired man in his fifties running a war he never wanted.\nso when he tells himself to get up and do the work of a human being, he isn't selling anything. nobody was ever going to read it. it's a tired man in a camp trying to get himself moving, and he had to do it again the next day.","where":"meditations, book 5, section 1 for today. then book 2, section 1. gregory hays's translation reads like it was written this year."},"meditations":{"name":"the meditations","lines":"the private notebook marcus aurelius kept, mostly in army camps on the danube in the 170s, while he was running a war and the roman empire. he wrote it in greek, to himself. twelve short books of notes, reminders, and pep talks.\nhe never meant anyone to read it. it survived by luck and was first printed in the 1550s. now it's on every nightstand from locker rooms to silicon valley, because it sounds like someone talking himself out of bed in the morning, which it is.\nbook 5 opens with the morning argument. he gives the lazy voice its lines and then answers them. you were born for this. the plants, the birds, the ants, the spiders and the bees all do their part. he even answers the objection that a person needs rest. sure, he says, and nature set a limit on that, the same as on eating and drinking, and you're past it.\nthe reasoning under it is the stoic idea that people are built to work together, the way hands and feet are. he isn't telling himself to grind for its own sake. he's telling himself that other people are counting on his part of it.","where":"book 5, section 1 is the one about getting out of bed. the whole notebook is twelve short books. start with 2 and 5."},"zhuangzi":{"name":"zhuangzi","lines":"a chinese thinker from about twenty three hundred years ago who wrote the funniest serious book there is, full of talking trees, happy fish, a cook who never sharpens his knife, and a man who turns down a kingdom because he'd rather be a turtle in the mud. he's the patron saint of everyone who ever turned down the promotion and never regretted it. take from him that being useless to the machine might be how you get to have a life.\nhe lived it, too. the story goes that a king sent two officials to offer him the job of running the state. he was fishing and didn't look up. he asked them whether a sacred tortoise, dead three thousand years and kept wrapped in silk in the king's temple, would rather be dead and honored or alive and dragging its tail in the mud. alive, they said. go away, he said. i'll drag my tail in the mud.\nthat's the tree's speech with a fishing pole in its hand. he isn't lazy, and the book isn't an argument for doing nothing. it's a warning about letting other people's uses for you become the whole of you.","where":"the inner chapters, the first seven, in burton watson's translation. the oak is in chapter 4. thomas merton's the way of chuang tzu has loose, short versions."},"uselesstree":{"name":"the useless tree","lines":"a story zhuangzi tells more than once. a carpenter walks past a huge old tree without looking. its wood is no good, too knotted for boards, it makes coffins that rot. so nobody's ever cut it down, and it's grown enormous.\nthat night the tree comes to the carpenter in a dream and asks what he thinks usefulness got the fruit trees, which get stripped and broken every year. being useless to the saw is how it got to live. the patron saint of everyone who turned down the promotion.\nin the telling in chapter 4 the carpenter is named shi, and the tree is an oak at a village shrine, broad enough to shade thousands of oxen. the tree's point in the dream is sharper than it first sounds. it says it nearly died getting to be this useless, and that it's been of great use to itself. then it turns on the carpenter. you're a worthless man about to die, it says. what do you know about worthless trees?\nthe last line of that chapter is the one people remember. everybody knows the use of the useful. nobody knows the use of the useless.","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 4, the story of carpenter shi and the oak at the shrine. there's another useless tree at the very end of chapter 1."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261012bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261012bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><circle cx='60' cy='226' r='30' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 96 256 L 150 150 L 250 150 L 300 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 150 L 204 256' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 150 256 L 150 232 L 262 232 L 262 256'/><path d='M 150 232 L 262 232'/><circle cx='196' cy='190' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='193' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='203' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 194 196 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 196 204 L 198 232 M 198 214 L 222 226 M 198 214 L 178 228'/><path d='M 430 256 C 428 216 412 190 418 150 M 470 256 C 474 214 492 188 482 150 M 418 150 C 400 120 372 118 352 96 M 482 150 C 500 118 536 120 560 96 M 448 150 C 446 120 452 104 448 84'/><path d='M 418 150 C 438 162 462 162 482 150'/><path d='M 236 104 C 216 52 300 14 356 40 C 400 4 500 6 536 44 C 604 26 668 70 640 116 C 660 150 600 168 566 146 C 540 172 480 168 452 146 C 420 170 356 170 340 142 C 290 164 222 146 236 104 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 430 256 C 420 262 410 262 400 264 M 470 256 C 482 262 492 262 502 264'/><circle cx='612' cy='178' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='611' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='621' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 612 184 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 612 192 L 612 222.88'/><path d='M 612 222.88 L 598 256 M 612 222.88 L 626 256'/><path d='M 612.0 200 L 594.0 228'/><path d='M 612.0 200 L 632.0 178'/><path d='M 632 172 L 640 132 M 630 132 L 652 136 L 650 148 L 630 144 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='712' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='703' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='713' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='708' cy='200' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 712 208 L 712 228.64'/><path d='M 712 228.64 L 700 256 M 712 228.64 L 724 256'/><path d='M 712.0 216 L 682.0 194'/><path d='M 712.0 216 L 678.0 208'/><\/g><g transform='translate(266,235.0) scale(1.4)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i show up for my work, and i'm worth something when i'm no use at all.","id":"2026-10-12"},{"date":"2026-10-13","theme":"everybody has lost somebody","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"find me a house where nobody has died.\na young mother named [[kisagotami|kisa gotami]] has a little boy, just old enough to walk, and he dies. she has never seen death up close. she won't let them take him. she puts him on her hip and goes from door to door asking for medicine, and people start saying she's lost her mind.\nsomebody finally sends her to [[buddha|the buddha]]. and he doesn't explain anything to her. he says, i can help. bring me a pinch of mustard seed. only it has to come from a house where no one has ever died. no son, no daughter, nobody.\nso she goes. every house has mustard seed. it's the cheapest thing in the kitchen. and at every door, when she asks the second part, the face changes. there's been a father, a daughter, a husband. by evening she understands what he sent her out to find. the living are few, somebody tells her. it's the dead who are many.\nshe carries her boy out to the forest and lays him down.\nhe could have just told her that. he let the doors tell her. grief makes you the only one in the world, and you aren't. somebody near you is carrying one today, too. you could knock.","source":"the story of kisa gotami, from the old commentary on the dhammapada, at verse 114 · in english, burlingame's buddhist legends, book 8","sourceRef":"kisagotami"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"don't tell me everybody dies.\n[[cslewis|c.s. lewis]] was the man england went to for answers. he'd written the book explaining why a good god lets people suffer. he'd gone on the radio during the war and made the whole thing make sense.\nthen his wife, joy, died of cancer, and he found out none of it worked on him.\nhe kept notebooks through those months, and later he published them as [[griefobserved|a grief observed]], under a made-up name, so nobody would know it was him. the first thing he says is that nobody ever told him grief felt so much like fear. the same fluttering in the stomach. the same yawning. he goes to god and what he gets, he says, is a door slammed in his face, and the sound of it being bolted from the inside.\nand he has no patience for comfort. she's in god's hands, people tell him. he's seen what those hands did to her. he doesn't want the general truth. he wants her. this one woman, and the quick mind that never let him get away with anything.\nthat's true too, and you don't have to pick. a loss is the most common thing there is, and it is yours alone, both at once. so if you're carrying one, let it be exactly as big as it is today. don't shrink it to make anybody comfortable.","source":"c.s. lewis, a grief observed · four short notebooks. read it in one sitting","sourceRef":"griefobserved"},"question":"is the grief you're carrying the one everybody has, or the one only you have?","caption":"she's knocking at every door in town. he's sitting behind the one that's bolted.","refs":{"kisagotami":{"name":"kisa gotami","lines":"a young woman from a poor family in savatthi, a city in north india where the buddha spent many of his rainy seasons. kisa means lean. it was a nickname, because she was so thin. gotami was her family name.\nthe story as we have it was written down many centuries after the buddha, in a commentary that collects the tales behind his sayings. so read it as the tradition's story about her. it's one of the best loved stories in all of buddhism.\nwhat the page leaves out is what happened next. she came back and asked to join him, and became a nun. the story says that one evening, lighting the lamps, she watched the little flames flare up and go out, and saw that's how it is with everything alive. the poems of the early nuns keep some verses under her name.\nnotice what he didn't do. no talk about how all things pass. he gave her an errand that would take all day and put her on a hundred doorsteps, and the people behind the doors did the teaching.","where":"the story is in the old commentary on the dhammapada, at verse 114. in english it's in eugene burlingame's buddhist legends, book 8, story 13. her own verses are in the therigatha, the poems of the first buddhist nuns."},"buddha":{"name":"the buddha","lines":"the son of a rich ruling family in what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago, who, as the old stories tell it, had everything and walked out on it at twenty nine to figure out why everybody suffers. six years later, sitting under a tree, he figured it out, and spent the next forty five years walking around northern india teaching it. he didn't ask anyone to believe him. he told them to try it and see, which is why so much of it sits comfortably in a therapist's office today. if you want a method more than a belief, start here.\nhe came to his own teaching through this exact door. the story of his youth is that he was kept inside a palace so he'd never see anything painful, and then on a few trips outside he saw an old man, a sick man and a dead man. it wrecked him, and he left to find out what a person does about it.\nso when a mother shows up carrying her dead child, he's the last person who'd tell her it isn't real. he knew what she was holding. he just also knew she couldn't hear it from him yet.","where":"the dhammapada, a short book of his sayings in verse, is the easy way in. the kisa gotami story is told with verse 114."},"cslewis":{"name":"c.s. lewis","lines":"the oxford professor who wrote the narnia books, the ones with the wardrobe and the lion, and who before that was a hard-headed atheist until his friend tolkien, the lord of the rings guy, talked him the rest of the way into christianity on a walk one night. he became the most famous defender of christianity in the english speaking world, with an answer for everything. then he married late, lost his wife to cancer four years later, and wrote a small, furious notebook saying god felt like a door slammed in his face. if you've ever had your neat answers fall apart when somebody died, he wrote the answers and then watched them fall apart.\nhe married late. joy davidman was an american writer who'd first written to him as a reader. they had a quick civil wedding in 1956 so she could stay in britain, and months later she had cancer. they were married again by a priest at her hospital bed. she got better for a while, and they had a few good years. she died in 1960.\nthe book he'd written twenty years earlier on why god allows pain is careful and reasonable. the notebooks are neither. that's the gap the page is about. he'd had the answer for everybody else's suffering, and it was no use at all when it was his.","where":"a grief observed, which you'll read in one night. then surprised by joy, his memoir, for how he came to believe in the first place."},"griefobserved":{"name":"a grief observed","lines":"the notebooks c.s. lewis kept after his wife joy davidman died of cancer in 1960. the first line is no one ever told me that grief felt so like fear.\nhe writes that god feels like a door slammed in his face, bolted from the inside, and wonders whether god is a cosmic sadist. he published it in 1961 under a fake name, n.w. clerk, because the famous defender of christianity had come apart. it doesn't end with an answer. it's short, and you'll read it in one night.\nthe line about god's hands is from the second notebook. people tell him she's in god's hands now. he answers that if so, she was in god's hands all along, and he saw what they did to her here.\nit does move, by the end. he doesn't get an answer. he gets quieter. he writes that maybe his own frantic need was what slammed the door, and that the less he clutched at her, the nearer she seemed. he never took back the first three notebooks, though. he left the anger in.","where":"a grief observed, 1961. four short notebooks. read it straight through."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261013bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261013bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><path d='M 34 250 L 34 200 L 104 200 L 104 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 26 200 L 69.0 172.0 L 112 200'/><path d='M 62.0 250 L 62.0 225.0 L 77.4 225.0 L 77.4 250'/><path d='M 128 256 L 128 200 L 202 200 L 202 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 200 L 165.0 170.4 L 210 200'/><path d='M 157.6 256 L 157.6 228.0 L 173.88 228.0 L 173.88 256'/><path d='M 250 256 L 250 208.0'/><path d='M 217.36 208.0 C 202.0 179.2 230.8 155.2 250 164.8 C 274.0 152.32 299.92 179.2 282.64 208.0 C 269.2 217.6 230.8 217.6 217.36 208.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='300' cy='192' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='297' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='307' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 298 199 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 300 206 L 296 240 L 322 262 L 284 262'/><path d='M 299 214 L 317 228'/><path d='M 299 214 L 321 220'/><ellipse cx='326' cy='230' rx='13' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 372 256 L 372 96 L 428 96 L 428 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='418' cy='180' r='3'/><path d='M 362 96 L 438 96' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 372 150 L 428 150 M 372 204 L 428 204' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 346 150 l -10 -6 M 344 164 l -12 0 M 346 178 l -10 6' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 428 170 L 452 170 L 452 182 L 428 182' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 520 256 L 520 208 L 640 208 L 640 256'/><path d='M 508 208 L 652 208'/><path d='M 581.6 189.0 L 610.4 189.0 L 610.4 207.0 L 581.6 207.0 Z M 596 189.0 L 596 207.0' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='560' cy='160' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='552' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='562' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 553 167 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 560 174 L 562 208 M 561 186 L 584 196 M 561 186 L 536 200'/><path d='M 690 256 L 690 120 L 760 120 L 760 256' opacity='.45'/><path d='M 690 150 L 760 150 M 690 180 L 760 180 M 690 210 L 760 210' stroke-width='2' opacity='.45'/><\/g><g transform='translate(160,151.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"my loss is mine, and i'm not the only one carrying one.","id":"2026-10-13"},{"date":"2026-10-14","theme":"you asked to see it","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"you asked to see it.\nten chapters into [[gita|the gita]], [[arjuna]] gets brave. he's been listening to his chariot driver explain god to him on a battlefield, and he believes it, mostly, the way you believe a thing you've been told. so he asks. if you think i can stand it, show me what you really are.\nand [[krishna]] says all right. you can't see it with those eyes, so here, borrow these.\nand it's too much. it's a thousand suns coming up at once. it's every face and every mouth, with no beginning to it and no end. then it gets worse, because he sees the armies, his cousins, his teachers, all of them pouring into those mouths like moths into a flame. i am time, the voice says. i'm what takes everything.\narjuna's hair stands up. he's shaking. he says, i'm sorry. i called you friend. i joked around with you like it was nothing. i didn't know. and then he says the most human thing in the whole book. please. be the one i know again.\nand krishna is. the gentle face comes back, and arjuna can breathe.\nwe say we want the truth, the whole thing. mostly we want it in a size we can talk to, and that's allowed. notice today how much of it comes to you wearing an ordinary face. that's the mercy.","source":"bhagavad gita, chapter 11 · read 10 and 11 together. he's told, and then he's shown","sourceRef":"gita"},"right":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"be careful what you demand.\n[[job]] has lost everything he had, and for thirty chapters he's asked for exactly one thing. not his money back. not even his children. he wants god to show up and explain it. he says it over and over. let him answer me.\nand then one day, out of [[whirlwind|a storm]], he does.\nonly there's no explanation in it. it's all questions. where were you when i laid the foundation of the earth? have you ever told the morning to come up? do you know where the snow is kept? can you feed a lion? it goes on like that for four chapters. the wild donkey, the ostrich, the war horse, and a long proud stretch about the hippopotamus. there's not one word about why.\nand job, who had so much to say, puts his hand over his mouth. i had heard of you, he says. now i see you. and he's quiet.\nhe doesn't get his answer. he gets the size of what he'd been arguing with, and somehow that holds him.\nyou may be waiting on an explanation yourself, for something that happened. it may not be coming. but go outside this morning before it's light. look up for one whole minute. then see if the question weighs the same when you come back in.","source":"job 38 through 42:6 · read it out loud. then 42:7, where the friends get told off","sourceRef":"whirlwind"},"question":"what have you been demanding to see, and could you stand it?","caption":"two men asked god to show up. one got every face at once, and the other got a hippopotamus.","refs":{"gita":{"name":"the bhagavad gita","lines":"the song of god, seven hundred verses of sanskrit poetry, eighteen chapters, maybe two thousand years old. it's a conversation on a battlefield between a prince named arjuna, who's frozen and won't fight, and his chariot driver, krishna, who turns out to be god.\nit's a small piece of the much bigger mahabharata, but it's the part everybody reads. the core of it is do your work, let go of how it turns out, and know that the real you can't be killed.\nit's the closest thing hinduism has to a single bible. gandhi read it every day. thoreau took it to walden pond. oppenheimer quoted it at the first atomic bomb test.\nchapter 11 is the one where talking stops. for ten chapters krishna has described himself. now arjuna asks to see, and gets what the tradition calls the universal form. everything at once, the making and the destroying, with nothing left out to make it easier to look at.","where":"chapter 11. read chapter 10 first, where he's told about it, and then 11, where he's shown. eknath easwaran's translation is plain and warm."},"arjuna":{"name":"arjuna","lines":"the third of the five pandava brothers in the mahabharata, the greatest archer of his time, and krishna's close friend and brother-in-law.\nin the gita he's the one who looks across the battlefield at his cousins, teachers, and elders, drops his bow, and says he can't do it. everybody who's ever frozen in front of something they had to do is arjuna.\nwhat makes chapter 11 so good is how he comes apart. he praises what he's seeing, and then he panics. he remembers every time he called this being hey, friend, every joke at dinner, every time he was careless with him, and begs to be forgiven for it the way a father forgives a son.\nthen he asks for the form he knows, the one with the crown and the familiar hands. he says he's glad he saw it and that his mind is shaken with fear. both. nobody in the book scolds him for it.","where":"the gita, chapter 1 for the collapse and chapter 11 for the vision. his apology is verses 41 and 42. his request for the old face back is verses 45 and 46."},"krishna":{"name":"krishna","lines":"one of the most loved gods in hinduism, the blue-skinned one with the flute, and the voice of the bhagavad gita, a seven hundred verse poem where he talks a terrified soldier through the morning of a battle. picture a guy frozen in the passenger seat, and the driver turns around, and the driver is god, and instead of telling him it'll be fine he says get up, do your job, and let go of how it turns out. gandhi read it every day. if you're stuck in front of something you have to do, that's the whole book.\nthe line on the page is one of the most famous in any scripture. i am time, the destroyer of worlds. robert oppenheimer, who ran the project that built the first atomic bomb, said it was what came to his mind when he watched the first test in 1945.\nand then look what the god does. he takes the terrible form back and shows the gentle one, and tells arjuna not to be afraid. he says nobody gets that sight by study or by punishing themselves. the one way in is love.","where":"the gita, all eighteen chapters, is his long talk. chapter 11, verse 32, is the line about time."},"job":{"name":"job","lines":"the main character of a book in the bible, probably a story and not a history, about a good man who loses everything, his ten kids included, and then breaks out in sores from head to foot. his friends spend most of the book telling him he must have done something to deserve it. he refuses to buy it and demands that god explain himself, and god shows up and doesn't. if anyone's ever told you everything happens for a reason while you were at the bottom, job is on your side, and so, the book says, is god.\nthe demand on the page is real, and it's fierce. in chapter 31 he swears out his whole defense like a man in court and ends it, here is my signature, let the almighty answer me. his friends think that's blasphemy. the book ends up siding with him.\nafter the storm, god tells the friends they didn't speak the truth about him the way job did. the man who yelled got it right. and job's fortunes turn, the book says, when he prays for those same friends.","where":"the book of job. read chapters 1 through 3, then skip to 38 and read to the end. stephen mitchell's translation moves fast."},"whirlwind":{"name":"the whirlwind","lines":"in the book of job, chapter 38, god finally answers job out of a whirlwind, a storm. he doesn't explain anything. he asks job where he was when the world was made, and walks him through the stars, the sea, the wild animals.\nit's four chapters of the biggest speech in the bible, and it's all questions.\nthe hippopotamus is in chapter 40. the hebrew calls it behemoth, and god spends ten verses admiring it. bones like bronze, tail like a cedar, lying under the lotus plants with the river rushing at its mouth and not caring. then comes leviathan, the sea monster nobody can hook.\npeople have argued for a long time about whether this is an answer or a brush off. the plainest reading is that it's a change of scale. job asked a question about his own life. he's shown a world that was running long before he got here, full of creatures that have nothing to do with him, and loved anyway.","where":"job 38 through 41. read it out loud if you can. it's built to be heard."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261014bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261014bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 259 240 265 400 262 S 680 260 800 262'/><path d='M 492 128 L 518 128'/><path d='M 486 159 L 524 173'/><path d='M 470 187 L 512 222'/><path d='M 446 208 L 459 230'/><path d='M 416 219 L 423 258'/><path d='M 384 219 L 375 272'/><path d='M 354 208 L 341 230'/><path d='M 330 187 L 299 213'/><path d='M 314 159 L 263 178'/><path d='M 308 128 L 282 128'/><path d='M 314 97 L 276 83'/><path d='M 330 69 L 288 34'/><path d='M 354 48 L 341 26'/><path d='M 384 37 L 377 -2'/><path d='M 416 37 L 425 -16'/><path d='M 446 48 L 459 26'/><path d='M 470 69 L 501 43'/><path d='M 486 97 L 537 78'/><circle cx='400' cy='128' r='84' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='400' cy='128' r='56' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='400' cy='128' r='26' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='466.8735542387924' cy='148.68641446629377' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='463.8735542387924' cy='147.68641446629377' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='469.8735542387924' cy='147.68641446629377' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='441.9426725073407' cy='184.0429498058584' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='438.9426725073407' cy='183.0429498058584' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='444.9426725073407' cy='183.0429498058584' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='400.99111545709064' cy='197.99298314938943' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='397.99111545709064' cy='196.99298314938943' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='403.99111545709064' cy='196.99298314938943' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='359.6609859890073' cy='185.2080759038524' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='356.6609859890073' cy='184.2080759038524' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='362.6609859890073' cy='184.2080759038524' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='333.738988800465' cy='150.5716280940276' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='330.738988800465' cy='149.5716280940276' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='336.738988800465' cy='149.5716280940276' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='333.1264457612076' cy='107.31358553370626' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='330.1264457612076' cy='106.31358553370626' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='336.1264457612076' cy='106.31358553370626' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='358.0573274926593' cy='71.9570501941416' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='355.0573274926593' cy='70.9570501941416' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='361.0573274926593' cy='70.9570501941416' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='399.00888454290936' cy='58.007016850610555' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='396.00888454290936' cy='57.007016850610555' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='402.00888454290936' cy='57.007016850610555' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='440.3390140109927' cy='70.7919240961476' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='437.3390140109927' cy='69.7919240961476' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='443.3390140109927' cy='69.7919240961476' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='466.261011199535' cy='105.4283719059724' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='463.261011199535' cy='104.4283719059724' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='469.261011199535' cy='104.4283719059724' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='393' cy='124' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='407' cy='124' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 392 134 q 8 6 16 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 60 262 L 60 226 L 170 226 L 190 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='84' cy='262' r='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='164' cy='262' r='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 84 262 l 0 -1 M 164 262 l 0 -1'/><path d='M 190 240 L 232 234'/><circle cx='238' cy='192' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='235' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='245' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='240' cy='198' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 238 206 L 234 240 L 260 262 L 222 262'/><path d='M 237 214 L 257 184'/><path d='M 237 214 L 263 196'/><path d='M 196 262 C 210 244 224 244 232 262' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 200 262 L 228 262' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 560 262 C 600 232 660 232 700 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='630' cy='196' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='622' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='632' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 623 202 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 630 210 L 630 238 M 630 238 L 606 246 L 640 252 M 630 238 L 660 250 M 630 220 L 618 214 L 622 204 M 630 220 L 648 236'/><path d='M 480 262 C 520 250 470 232 520 220 C 560 210 500 196 540 184 C 572 174 520 162 552 152' stroke-dasharray='7 7' stroke-width='2.4'/><ellipse cx='744' cy='244' rx='32' ry='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='708' cy='238' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 724 258 l 0 6 M 760 258 l 0 6 M 700 234 l -3 -5'/><circle cx='704' cy='236' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><\/g><g transform='translate(96,199.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i don't need the whole answer. i can stand in the size of it.","id":"2026-10-14"},{"date":"2026-10-15","theme":"march, or keep the fire","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"pray with your legs.\n[[heschel|abraham joshua heschel]] came from a long line of rabbis, men who prayed for a living. he knew the prayer book the way you know your own kitchen. and he had more reason than most to stay home with it. he'd gotten out of warsaw six weeks ahead of the german army. his mother and his sisters didn't get out.\nso here's a man who could have said, i've seen what the world does. i'll be in my study.\nin march of 1965 he's in alabama instead, in the front row of [[selma|the march out of selma]]. a rabbi with a big white beard and a flower lei around his neck, walking next to martin luther king, two weeks after state troopers had beaten people bloody on that same road. for the right of black americans to vote. it wasn't his town and it wasn't, strictly, his fight.\nafterward he wrote that he'd felt his legs were praying.\nhe'd spent his life reading the hebrew prophets, and what he took from them is that god has no use for a religion that stays indoors while somebody's getting beaten outside.\na question to walk around with. what do you already believe that your legs haven't heard about yet? pick one. take it for a walk today.","source":"abraham joshua heschel on selma, in moral grandeur and spiritual audacity · then the first chapter of the prophets","sourceRef":"heschel"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"somebody has to stay by the well.\n[[thurman|howard thurman]] wrote the book they carried. [[jesusdisinherited|jesus and the disinherited]] is about a hundred pages on what jesus has to say to people with their backs against the wall. martin luther king read it as a student, and people say he kept it with him on the road.\nand thurman himself didn't march. not once. i'm not a movement man, he said late in his life. it's not my way.\nyou could take that for a dodge. his grandmother was born a slave. he knew exactly what was at stake. but look at what he did do. when king was stabbed in new york in 1958, thurman went to the hospital and told him not to hurry back. ask your doctors for two more weeks. get quiet. look again at yourself, and at this thing you're carrying. and king wrote to say he was doing it.\nthat was his post. he was who the people out front came to when they were running dry.\nnow, there's a voice in you that says the only real work is the visible kind. the march, the launch, the finish line. it isn't so. the people out front are drinking from somewhere.\nask it today and don't flinch. are you avoiding the street? or are you the well? only you know. be honest, and then be that.","source":"howard thurman, jesus and the disinherited · a hundred pages. then with head and heart, his own story","sourceRef":"thurman"},"question":"is your place out front right now, or is it keeping the water?","caption":"one of them is praying with his legs. the other is the reason there's a cup of water at the end of the road.","refs":{"heschel":{"name":"abraham joshua heschel","lines":"a rabbi from a long line of hasidic holy men in poland who got out of warsaw six weeks before the nazis came, lost his mother and sisters to them, and ended up in new york writing books about wonder. then he marched in the front row at selma with martin luther king, and came out against the vietnam war, when plenty of people thought a rabbi should stay in his lane. picture an old-world rabbi with a huge white beard at the front of a civil rights march with a hawaiian flower lei around his neck. take from him that prayer can be something you do with your feet.\nhis doctorate was on the hebrew prophets, the bible's angry spokesmen for god, like amos and isaiah, and it turned into a big book called the prophets. his point about them was that they aren't fortune tellers. they're people who can't stand cruelty, who feel what god feels about it, and who say so to whoever's in charge.\nthat's what put him in alabama. he met king in 1963 and they became close. he marched, he spoke against the vietnam war beside him, and ten days before king was killed he introduced him to a room full of rabbis. the line about his legs comes from his own notes on the march.","where":"the sabbath, which is short. then moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, his collected essays, for selma and vietnam. the prophets is the big one."},"selma":{"name":"selma","lines":"a small city in alabama where the fight for black voting rights came to a head in 1965. on march 7, bloody sunday, state troopers beat peaceful marchers on the edmund pettus bridge on live television.\ntwo weeks later, martin luther king led thousands, rabbi abraham heschel in the front row, on a five day march from selma to the state capital in montgomery. that summer congress passed the voting rights act.\nthe photograph from the front row is the one the country saw. king in the middle, and a few steps over, a rabbi with a white beard and flowers around his neck. marchers who'd come from hawaii had brought the leis.\nheschel was fifty eight, a scholar with no need to be there. in his notes he wrote that even without words, the march was worship. for a man from a long line of rabbis that was not a small thing to say.","where":"taylor branch's at canaan's edge tells the whole month. heschel's own lines about it are in moral grandeur and spiritual audacity."},"thurman":{"name":"howard thurman","lines":"a black baptist preacher from florida, raised by a grandmother who had been enslaved, who became the quiet teacher behind the american civil rights movement. in 1949 he wrote a short book saying jesus was a poor jew living under roman rule, and that what he taught was meant first for people with their backs against the wall. martin luther king read it, and so did plenty of the people around him. thurman himself stayed out of the marches. think of the person who never posts and never takes the stage, and who turns out to be the one the organizers all go to see when they're coming apart.\nhe'd been asked the hard question long before the marches. in 1935, on a trip through south asia, a man in ceylon called him a traitor to all the darker peoples of the earth for being a christian, since christians had run the slave trade. a few months later gandhi asked him nearly the same thing. it took him thirteen years to answer, and the answer was the book.\nthe not marching is on the record with no apology attached. he told an interviewer in 1978 that he was not a movement man and that it wasn't his way. what he did is in the next door. he ran churches and chapels, taught, wrote, and kept the people at the front supplied with something to stand on.","where":"jesus and the disinherited, a little over a hundred pages. then with head and heart, his autobiography. meditations of the heart is the quiet side of him."},"jesusdisinherited":{"name":"jesus and the disinherited","lines":"howard thurman's best known book, published in april 1949 and a little over a hundred pages long. he'd been giving the argument as talks since the 1930s, and the book itself came out of a week of lectures at a college in austin, texas, in april 1948.\nit starts from the plain facts that jesus was a poor jew and belonged to a minority living under a bigger power, and asks what his teaching says to people with their backs against the wall. the chapters after that are called fear, deception, hate and love.\nmartin luther king read it as a student. people say he and others in the civil rights movement carried it around with them, and how literally to take that is hard to pin down.\nthe hospital visit was in 1958, after a woman stabbed king in the chest at a book signing in harlem. thurman, who'd known the king family for years, came and told him to ask his doctors for two extra weeks, away from the pressure of the movement, to look again at himself and the cause. king wrote back that he was following the advice.","where":"jesus and the disinherited, 1949. read chapter one, then the chapter on fear."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261015bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261015bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 0 258 C 120 252 260 266 380 258 C 470 252 520 262 560 258' stroke-width='9' opacity='.22'/><circle cx='84' cy='190' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='82' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='92' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 83 196 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 84 204 L 84 229.76'/><path d='M 84 229.76 L 70 260 M 84 229.76 L 98 260'/><path d='M 84.0 212 L 62.0 232'/><path d='M 84.0 212 L 108.0 226'/><circle cx='148' cy='182' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='146' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='156' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 147 188 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 148 196 L 148 226.88'/><path d='M 148 226.88 L 134 260 M 148 226.88 L 162 260'/><path d='M 148.0 204 L 124.0 218'/><path d='M 148.0 204 L 172.0 218'/><circle cx='214' cy='176' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='212' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='222' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 213 180 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 214 190 L 214 224.72'/><path d='M 214 224.72 L 199 260 M 214 224.72 L 229 260'/><path d='M 214.0 198 L 190.0 212'/><path d='M 214.0 198 L 238.0 210'/><path d='M 203 184 C 203 208 225 208 225 184 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><ellipse cx='214' cy='212' rx='17' ry='7'/><circle cx='199' cy='214' r='3.4' fill='var(--peach)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='214' cy='219' r='3.4' fill='var(--peach)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='229' cy='214' r='3.4' fill='var(--peach)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='292' cy='180' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='290' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='300' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 291 184 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 292 194 L 292 226.16'/><path d='M 292 226.16 L 277 260 M 292 226.16 L 307 260'/><path d='M 292.0 202 L 268.0 214'/><path d='M 292.0 202 L 328.0 200'/><path d='M 690 258 L 690 183.0'/><path d='M 639.0 183.0 C 615.0 138.0 660.0 100.5 690 115.5 C 727.5 96.0 768.0 138.0 741.0 183.0 C 720.0 198.0 660.0 198.0 639.0 183.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 520 258 L 520 214 L 590 214 L 590 258 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 512 214 L 598 214'/><path d='M 526 214 L 526 170 L 584 170 L 584 214'/><path d='M 520 170 L 590 170'/><path d='M 555 170 L 555 196'/><path d='M 546 196 L 564 196 L 561 210 L 549 210 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='730' cy='200' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='722' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='732' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 723 204 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 730 214 L 730 246'/><path d='M 700 258 C 700 244 760 244 760 258 C 760 266 700 266 700 258'/><path d='M 730 222 L 706 240 M 730 222 L 752 244'/><path d='M 440 252 L 432 234 L 456 234 L 448 252 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 332 226 C 360 216 400 218 428 230' stroke-dasharray='3 9' stroke-width='2.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(700,121.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i know which work is mine today, and i do that one.","id":"2026-10-15"},{"date":"2026-10-16","theme":"the dishes","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"wash the dishes to wash the dishes.\na friend of [[thay|thich nhat hanh]] tells this one on himself. it's paris, the early seventies, a small apartment, an evening with vietnamese friends. after supper the good conversation moves into the other room, and he's the one stuck at the sink with the pile, annoyed, washing as fast as he can so he can get back to where life is happening.\nthich nhat hanh wanders in and asks him, what's the best way to wash dishes?\nto get them clean, the friend says. obviously.\nno, he says. you should wash the dishes to wash the dishes.\nthat's the whole teaching, and it's in [[miracleofmindfulness|a little book]] he wrote. if you're washing this cup only to be done with it, you aren't here for the cup. and if you can't be here for the cup, he says, you won't be here for the tea afterward either. you'll drink it thinking about the next thing. a person can go through a whole life like that, always leaning into a minute that isn't this one.\nyou know the lean. it's how most of a day gets spent.\ntoday, pick the chore you can't stand. the dishes will do. warm water, one plate, the weight of it. don't make it holy. just be somebody who is washing a plate.","source":"thich nhat hanh, the miracle of mindfulness · the first chapter. the friend at the sink was jim forest","sourceRef":"miracleofmindfulness"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"god is in the kitchen too.\nthree hundred years earlier, in that same city, there's a big clumsy man named [[lawrence|brother lawrence]] working in a monastery kitchen. he'd been a soldier and then a servant in a rich man's house, and by his own account he was a great awkward fellow who broke everything. he came in as a lay brother, one of the ones who do the work. they put him on pots.\nand he didn't like it. he says so. he had a real aversion to the kitchen. he was in it for fifteen years.\nwhat he did with those years turned into [[practicepresence|a small book]] that people have been handing each other ever since. he just decided to do every little thing for the love of god. he said he was glad to pick a straw up off the floor, if it was for that. and something moved. he said that in the noise and clatter of his kitchen, with several people calling for different things at once, he had god as quietly as he ever did on his knees in the chapel.\nhe never got a better job. the job turned out to be the place.\nwe keep waiting for the real part to start, after the errands. he'd say you're standing in it. take the dullest ten minutes you've got today and do them for love. then watch what the ten minutes turn into.","source":"brother lawrence, the practice of the presence of god · the four conversations, twenty minutes","sourceRef":"practicepresence"},"question":"what are you rushing through to get to your life?","caption":"same city, three hundred years apart, and it's one long counter with the sink in the middle.","refs":{"thay":{"name":"thich nhat hanh","lines":"a vietnamese buddhist monk who spent the vietnam war telling both sides to stop, got locked out of his own country for it, and spent the next forty years in france teaching people to wash the dishes like it mattered. a lot of what's in the meditation app on your phone traces back to him, usually with the hard parts sanded off. if you've ever tried to be present and felt like it was one more thing to fail at, he's the one who makes it small enough to actually do.\nhe wasn't in paris on retreat. he was there leading the vietnamese buddhist peace delegation, a handful of exiles trying to get a war stopped from the other side of the world, with very little money and their own country closed to them. he'd been shut out of vietnam since 1966.\nso the man teaching the dishes had every reason to be somewhere else in his head. friends were being killed at home. that's when he said it. the dishes weren't a break from the work. they were how you stayed a person while you did it.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness. then peace is every step, which is even shorter."},"miracleofmindfulness":{"name":"the miracle of mindfulness","lines":"a short book thich nhat hanh wrote in 1975, first as a long letter to a young monk working in the war zone in vietnam. it's about breathing, walking, and washing the dishes with your full attention.\nit's one of the books the western mindfulness world grew out of. you can read it in an evening.\nthe friend at the sink was jim forest, an american peace worker who spent a lot of time with him in those years. forest told the story for the rest of his life, and added that thich nhat hanh also said you should wash each dish as if it were the baby jesus.\nthe book makes the point about the tea in so many words. if we can't wash the dishes, he writes, chances are we won't be able to drink our tea either. we'll be thinking of other things, barely aware of the cup in our hands. he calls that being sucked away into the future, and not being able to actually live one minute of life.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness, 1975. the dishes and the tea are in the first chapter."},"lawrence":{"name":"brother lawrence","lines":"his name was nicholas herman, from lorraine, in the east of france. he was a soldier as a young man and then a footman, a household servant, and he joined the barefoot carmelites in paris as a lay brother. the carmelites are a catholic order built around silent prayer. a lay brother took vows but wasn't a priest. he did the cooking, the hauling and the repairs.\nhe traced the whole thing to one winter day when he was about eighteen. he looked at a tree with every leaf gone and thought about how, in a little while, the leaves would be back, and then the flowers and the fruit. it hit him so hard, he said, that it never wore off.\nhe had no schooling to speak of and he wrote no book. people who came to talk with him wrote down what he said, and a few of his letters were saved. he died an old man, at about eighty, still in the same house.\nhe's on the page because he was honest about the kitchen. he doesn't say the work was secretly wonderful. he says he had a great aversion to it, and found a way to be happy there anyway.","where":"the practice of the presence of god. the four conversations come first and take twenty minutes. the letters after them are shorter still."},"practicepresence":{"name":"the practice of the presence of god","lines":"a very short book made out of four conversations and a handful of letters. a churchman who came to see brother lawrence, beginning in august of 1666, wrote down what the cook told him, and after lawrence died the notes and letters were printed together.\nthe method is almost nothing. you keep up a quiet, running conversation with god all day while you do whatever is in front of you. no special words. when you notice you've wandered off, you come back, and you don't beat yourself up about it. he said he flipped between the two all day and just kept returning.\nthe line the page paraphrases is the famous one. the time of business, he said, was no different for him from the time of prayer. catholics and protestants both took the book up, which almost never happens, and it's been in print for more than three hundred years.","where":"any edition. it's about fifty small pages, and it's been free to read for a long time."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261016bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261016bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 259 240 265 400 262 S 680 260 800 262'/><path d='M 120 262 L 120 190 L 700 190 L 700 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 108 190 L 712 190' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 330 190 C 334 214 466 214 470 190' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 352 190 q 10 -8 20 0 M 392 190 q 10 -8 20 0 M 432 190 q 10 -8 20 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='366' cy='168' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='388' cy='156' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='420' cy='162' r='11' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='446' cy='146' r='5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='404' cy='134' r='7' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><ellipse cx='264' cy='184' rx='30' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='264' cy='176' rx='30' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='264' cy='168' rx='30' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='264' cy='160' rx='30' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='190' cy='106' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='188' cy='104' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='198' cy='104' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 189 110 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 190 120 L 190 190 M 190 134 L 230 150 M 190 134 L 222 164'/><ellipse cx='236' cy='152' rx='16' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='86' cy='150' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='84' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='94' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 85 156 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 86 164 L 86 222 M 86 222 L 74 262 M 86 222 L 98 262 M 86 178 L 108 196 M 86 178 L 68 200'/><circle cx='590' cy='96' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='582' cy='94' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='592' cy='94' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 583 100 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 590 112 L 590 190 M 590 130 L 548 150 M 590 130 L 630 156'/><path d='M 506 190 L 510 150 L 566 150 L 570 190 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 500 150 L 576 150'/><path d='M 548 150 L 540 118'/><path d='M 524 140 c -8 -10 8 -14 0 -26 M 546 108 c -8 -10 8 -14 0 -26' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 610 60 L 760 60' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 640 60 L 640 84 M 632 84 C 632 100 648 100 648 84 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 690 60 L 690 80'/><circle cx='690' cy='92' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 736 262 l 22 -6' stroke-width='2.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(720,33.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"this, right here, is my life. i'm in it.","id":"2026-10-16"},{"date":"2026-10-17","theme":"hold on, or lay it down","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"don't let go until it blesses you.\n[[jacob]] is about to face the brother he cheated twenty years ago, and he's scared. he sends his whole family across the river ahead of him. the wives, the kids, the animals, everything he owns. and then he's alone on the bank in the dark.\nand somebody jumps him. the story just says a man. they [[wrestling|wrestle]] all night, in the dirt, and neither one can win. toward dawn the stranger hits him in the hip and throws it out of joint. and jacob, with his leg ruined, still won't let go.\nlet me go, the stranger says. it's getting light.\nno. not until you bless me.\nso he gets a blessing, and a new name with it. israel. the one who struggles with god. he walks away from that river at sunrise with both of them, limping.\nyou know a night like that. the thing has you on the ground, you can't win, and it won't end. and some stubborn part of you says, i am not walking away from this empty handed. you're going to give me something.\nthat's allowed. it's right there in the book. so if you're in one, hang on today. don't ask to win. ask what it has for you. and expect to limp a little after.","source":"genesis 32:22 to 32 · read all of 32 and 33. the fear, the night, and the morning","sourceRef":"wrestling"},"right":{"lineage":"native","text":"there's a time to put it down.\n[[chiefjoseph|joseph]] didn't start the war. the army ordered his people out of their valley, some angry young men killed settlers, and after that there was nothing to do but run. so they ran. about seven hundred and fifty people, most of them not fighters. old people, mothers, babies. more than eleven hundred miles through the mountains, with soldiers coming from every direction, headed for canada.\nthey beat or outran one army after another. they got within forty miles of the line.\nthen it snowed. at [[bearpaw|the bear paw mountains]] the army caught them for the last time. after five days the horses were gone, people were freezing in pits they'd dug in the ground, and some of the children were out in the hills with no blankets.\nhe could have held on. men have died for a lot less than forty miles. he looked at the children, and he stopped. the words that come down to us end, i will fight no more forever.\nit didn't save them the way he was promised, and i won't dress that up. but he knew what he'd been holding on for, and it wasn't the fight.\nlook at what you've been gripping. ask what it's for. if it has started to cost the very people it was for, you're allowed to set it down.","source":"chief joseph, an indian's view of indian affairs, 1879 · his own account. then kent nerburn's book on the flight","sourceRef":"chiefjoseph"},"question":"what are you holding on to, and what is it for?","caption":"one of them won't let go until the sun is up. the other one lets go so the children will be there to see it.","refs":{"jacob":{"name":"jacob","lines":"one of the fathers of the jewish people in the book of genesis, grandson of abraham, and a hustler from the start. he's a twin, and the story says he was born holding his brother esau's heel. his name means something like heel grabber.\nhe lived up to it. he got his hungry brother to trade away his rights as the firstborn for a bowl of stew. then he put on goatskins to feel like hairy esau and tricked their blind old father into giving him the blessing meant for the older son. esau swore to kill him, and jacob ran. he spent twenty years working for an uncle who cheated him right back.\nthe night on the page is the night before he finally has to face esau, who is coming to meet him with four hundred men. he's sent presents ahead. he's split his camp in two so at least half might survive. then he's alone.\nthe morning after is worth knowing. he limps out to meet his brother, bowing to the ground seven times. and esau runs to him, throws his arms around his neck and kisses him, and they both cry.","where":"genesis 25 through 33 is his whole story. the night at the river is 32:22 to 32. read chapter 33 right after, for the morning."},"wrestling":{"name":"the wrestling at the jabbok","lines":"the jabbok is a river east of the jordan, in what's now the country of jordan. the story never says who the stranger is. the text calls him a man. he won't give his name when jacob asks. a later prophet, hosea, calls him an angel. jacob himself names the place peniel, which means the face of god, because, he says, i have seen god face to face and i'm still alive.\nthe new name is the heart of it. israel is explained right there in the story. you have struggled with god and with people, and you've come through. a whole people ends up carrying that name. they aren't called the ones who obey god or the ones who understand god. they're called the ones who wrestle.\nand the limp stayed in the tradition. the story ends by saying that's why, to this day, the people of israel don't eat the sinew of the hip. jewish dietary law still keeps that rule.","where":"genesis 32:22 to 32. the prophet hosea looks back on it in hosea 12:3 to 4."},"chiefjoseph":{"name":"chief joseph","lines":"a nez perce leader from a green valley in oregon who, in 1877, when the government ordered his band off their land, ended up leading about seven hundred and fifty men, women and kids on a fighting retreat of more than a thousand miles toward canada, beating the army again and again, and gave up forty miles from the border because the children were freezing. his surrender line is one of the most quoted sentences in american history. then the government broke every promise it made him, and he spent the rest of his life asking to go home. think of a refugee column with a leader who became famous for saying he was done. take from him that saying enough, out loud, for the sake of the people behind you, is its own kind of courage.\na word about the speech. an army officer named charles erskine scott wood wrote it down, and historians argue over how much of it is joseph's words and how much is wood's. one historian says the famous scene of joseph handing over his rifle while making it never happened that way. so the page says the words that come down to us. the last line is the part nobody has been willing to give up.\nwhat happened after is in his own words, and those aren't in doubt. he was promised his people could go home. they were shipped to kansas and then to indian territory, where many of them died. he spent twenty seven years asking to go back to his valley and never got there.","where":"his own account, an indian's view of indian affairs, from 1879. then kent nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce."},"bearpaw":{"name":"the bear paw mountains","lines":"low mountains in northern montana, about forty miles from the canadian border, where the army caught chief joseph and the nez perce at the end of their flight in the fall of 1877. after five days of fighting in the snow, joseph surrendered on october 5.\nabout two hundred and thirty nez perce slipped away in the night and made it to sitting bull's camp in canada.\nthe people who ran were his band of the nez perce, a name french traders gave them. their own name is nimíipuu, the people. their home was the wallowa valley in northeastern oregon, and his father, dying, had told him never to sell the ground that held his parents' bones.\nthat's the weight of the choice on the page. he wasn't a man who gave things up easily. he'd refused to sell, refused to move for years, and then kept seven hundred and fifty people alive across eleven hundred miles. when he stopped, it was because going on meant watching the children freeze.","where":"nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce. the battlefield is a national park site near chinook, montana."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261017bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261017bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 63 255 126 261 210 258 S 357 256 420 258'/><path d='M 420 258 C 480 252 540 232 600 236 C 680 240 740 222 800 226'/><path d='M 326 258 A 74 74 0 0 1 474 258 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 400 168 L 400 150 M 344 190 L 330 178 M 456 190 L 470 178 M 320 232 L 302 228 M 480 232 L 498 228' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 0 286 C 120 276 260 296 400 284 C 520 274 660 296 800 282 L 800 320 L 0 320 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 40 300 q 14 -6 28 0 M 200 304 q 14 -6 28 0 M 360 298 q 14 -6 28 0 M 560 302 q 14 -6 28 0 M 700 298 q 14 -6 28 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='168' cy='176' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='166' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='176' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 167 182 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='214' cy='170' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 209 168 h 3 M 217 168 h 3' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 168 190 L 176 222 L 150 258 M 176 222 L 196 258'/><path d='M 214 184 L 208 222 L 190 250 M 208 222 L 236 258'/><path d='M 170 198 L 206 206 L 224 192 M 212 194 L 182 190 L 160 206'/><path d='M 244 150 l 10 -8 M 252 166 l 12 -2 M 138 150 l -10 -8' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 500 252 L 526 212 L 552 246 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 556 244 L 578 214 L 600 240 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='650' cy='152' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='642' cy='150' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='652' cy='150' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 643 159 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 650 166 L 650 200.72'/><path d='M 650 200.72 L 637 236 M 650 200.72 L 663 236'/><path d='M 650.0 174 L 632.0 202'/><path d='M 650.0 174 L 676.0 144'/><path d='M 640 150 L 632 126 M 652 148 L 652 122 M 662 152 L 672 130' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 596 250 L 640 256' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 592 246 l 6 8' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='720' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='712' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='722' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 713 201 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 720 208 L 720 213.28'/><path d='M 720 213.28 L 708 232 M 720 213.28 L 732 232'/><path d='M 720.0 216 L 704.0 246'/><path d='M 720.0 216 L 736.0 246'/><circle cx='768' cy='200' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='760' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='770' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 761 207 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 768 214 L 768 214.16'/><path d='M 768 214.16 L 756 230 M 768 214.16 L 780 230'/><path d='M 768.0 222 L 752.0 252'/><path d='M 768.0 222 L 784.0 252'/><circle cx='450' cy='90' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='520' cy='60' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='600' cy='110' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='690' cy='70' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='760' cy='120' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='560' cy='150' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='480' cy='170' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='720' cy='170' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='640' cy='40' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><\/g><g transform='translate(84,237.0) scale(1.4)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can hold on when it's worth it, and i can lay it down when it isn't.","id":"2026-10-17"},{"date":"2026-10-18","theme":"same water","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"he went and checked.\nmost of us have an opinion about other people's religions, and most of us got it secondhand. [[ramakrishna]] did something almost nobody does. he tried them.\nhe was a temple priest outside calcutta, a village man with hardly any schooling, so in love with his goddess that his family thought he'd lost his mind. and one year he set her aside and lived as a muslim. he said the prayers and ate the food, under a muslim teacher, until something opened. later he had someone read him the bible and gave himself to that the same way, until one day he saw jesus walking toward him.\nthen he came back and said the thing he'd say for the rest of his life. as many faiths, so many paths.\nhis picture for it was a pond with [[ghat|steps]] going down to the water on every side. the hindus go down one set of steps and fill their jars and call it jal. the muslims go down another and call it pani. the english call it water. and then they stand on the bank and fight about the name.\nhe isn't saying it's all the same on the surface. the steps are different, and you do have to pick some and walk down. he's saying look in the jar.\nbe a little curious today about somebody else's steps. you don't have to switch. just ask what they're drinking.","source":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna · the record of his conversations. open it anywhere","sourceRef":"ghat"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"you've been praying to it already.\n[[paul]] is alone in athens, waiting for his friends, and he's upset. the city is wall to wall statues of gods. for a man raised the way he was, that's a nightmare to walk through. you'd expect him to preach against it.\nthen he's brought up in front of the city's thinkers at [[areopagus|the areopagus]], and he does something else. people of athens, he says, i can see you're very religious. i was walking around looking at your shrines, and i found an altar that said, to [[unknowngod|an unknown god]]. that one. the one you've been honoring without a name. that's who i came to talk about.\nand to back it up he doesn't reach for his own scriptures. he reaches for theirs. in him we live and move and have our being, he says. and as some of your own poets have put it, we are his children.\nhe found it already there. in a pagan city, in a greek poem. he didn't have to carry it in.\nsome of them laughed at him. a few said, come back, we'd like to hear more.\nwhatever you call the big thing, you didn't bring it to the people around you. it was there before you walked up. listen for it today in somebody else's words. especially somebody you'd never go to church with.","source":"acts 17:16 to 34 · nineteen verses. count how much of the speech he borrowed from them","sourceRef":"unknowngod"},"question":"where have you been sure it isn't?","caption":"two sets of steps going down to one pond. the altar on the far bank doesn't have a name on it.","refs":{"ramakrishna":{"name":"ramakrishna","lines":"a village kid from bengal, in eastern india, born in 1836, who could barely read, became the priest at a big temple outside calcutta, and spent years so overwhelmed by god that his family thought he'd lost his mind. then he did something almost nobody does. he lived as a muslim for a while, then as a christian, to see where they went, and came back saying they all get you to the same place. his student carried that to america in 1893, and a lot of the all-paths-up-the-same-mountain talk you hear today runs back through him. if you've ever wanted to test a religion instead of just believing it, he's the one who actually did.\nthe two experiments have dates. in 1866 he took a muslim teacher and for a stretch lived entirely as a muslim. in 1874 he had the bible read to him, and the story is that the vision of jesus came some days into it. he was a hindu priest the whole time and went back to being one.\nwhat he came home with wasn't a theory. he never said the religions teach the same things. he said he'd gone down each set of steps himself and got wet every time. his student vivekananda carried that to america in 1893 and it changed how the west talked about religion.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the diary of his conversations. it's long. open it anywhere. the pond with the steps comes up more than once."},"ghat":{"name":"the steps to the water","lines":"a flight of wide stone steps leading down to a river, where people bathe, pray, wash clothes, and in some places burn the dead. varanasi has dozens of them along the ganges, one after another for miles.\nat dawn they fill up with people going into the water to pray. at night some of them are lit by funeral fires. it's where a lot of indian life and death happens out in the open.\nramakrishna's version is a village tank, a big pond dug for water, with ghats on several sides. hindus draw water at one and call it jal. muslims draw at another and call it pani. christians at a third call it water. he'd ask whether anybody could seriously believe it's jal on one side and not water on the other.\nhe had a second half to it that people leave out. he said the steps matter. you can't get to the water by admiring all the stairs from the top. pick one and go down. the mistake isn't having a path. it's standing on yours and shouting at the people on the others.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna. look for the tank with the ghats, and the same water called jal, pani and water."},"paul":{"name":"paul","lines":"a strict religious jew in the first century who went after the first followers of jesus, dragging them off to jail, until something knocked him flat on a road and he switched sides completely. then he spent thirty years walking and sailing around the roman empire, starting little churches in people's houses and writing them letters, and those letters make up a big part of the new testament, the christian half of the bible. imagine the loudest guy against something becoming its best organizer overnight, and never once hiding what he'd been. if you've ever done harm and wondered whether you still get to do good, he spent his whole life asking that out loud.\nthe page says raised the way he was. he was a pharisee, a strict student of jewish law, brought up on the commandment against making any image of god at all. athens had a statue on every corner. the book of acts says his spirit was provoked in him.\nand that's what makes the speech so surprising. the man with the most reason to be disgusted opens with a compliment and builds his whole case out of things the athenians already had. it's the only time in the bible he's shown quoting pagan poetry to make a point about god.","where":"acts 17:16 to 34 for athens. for the man himself, his letter to the philippians, which is short and warm."},"areopagus":{"name":"the areopagus","lines":"a rocky hill in athens, right below the acropolis, where the city's old council met. in paul's day it was where you went to argue ideas in front of the city's leaders.\npaul gave a speech there around the year 50 and quoted greek poets to greek philosophers. you can still climb it. there's a bronze plaque with the speech on it.\nthe men who brought him there were philosophers, stoics and epicureans, the two big schools of the day. some of them had called him a babbler. the word in greek means a bird that picks up seeds, somebody who collects scraps of ideas. acts adds a dry little remark, that the athenians spent their time on nothing but telling or hearing something new.\nthe result was small. when he got to the part about a man raised from the dead, some sneered. others said they'd hear him again. a few believed, and acts names two of them, a member of the council named dionysius and a woman named damaris.","where":"acts 17:16 to 34. the whole athens visit is nineteen verses."},"unknowngod":{"name":"the unknown god","lines":"an altar paul saw in athens with the inscription to an unknown god. the greeks sometimes put these up to cover any god they might have missed, like a spare key.\npaul used it as his opener. you already worship this one, he said. let me tell you who it is.\nthe two lines he quotes aren't from the bible. in him we live and move and have our being is usually traced to epimenides, a poet from crete. we are his offspring is from aratus, a poet from paul's own part of the world, and the him in that poem is zeus.\nso a jewish missionary stands in the capital of greek thought and says your altar already points at it and your poets already said it. people have built whole ways of meeting other religions on those few verses. he didn't tell athens it had nothing. he told them they were closer than they knew.","where":"acts 17:22 to 28. the two lines of greek poetry are both in verse 28."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261018bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261018bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 250 C 37 247 75 253 125 250 S 212 248 250 250'/><path d='M 550 250 C 587 247 625 253 675 250 S 762 248 800 250'/><path d='M 250 250 L 272 250 L 272 264 L 294 264 L 294 278 L 316 278 L 316 304 L 484 304 L 484 278 L 506 278 L 506 264 L 528 264 L 528 250 L 550 250'/><path d='M 316 284 C 360 276 440 276 484 284 L 484 304 L 316 304 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 340 290 q 10 -6 20 0 M 392 294 q 10 -6 20 0 M 440 290 q 10 -6 20 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='196' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='194' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='204' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 195 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 196 182 L 196 215.44'/><path d='M 196 215.44 L 184 250 M 196 215.44 L 208 250'/><path d='M 196.0 190 L 178.0 218'/><path d='M 196.0 190 L 226.0 200'/><path d='M 220 196 C 214 206 216 222 226 226 C 238 226 240 208 234 196 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 170 L 140 170 L 140 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 170 C 70 120 130 120 150 170 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 100 132 L 100 112'/><path d='M 88 250 L 88 206 C 88 192 112 192 112 206 L 112 250'/><path d='M 596 250 L 596 130 M 616 250 L 616 130 M 676 250 L 676 130 M 696 250 L 696 130 M 756 250 L 756 130 M 776 250 L 776 130'/><path d='M 580 130 L 792 130 L 792 112 L 580 112 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 572 112 L 686 70 L 800 112'/><path d='M 636 250 L 636 204 L 664 204 L 664 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 630 204 L 670 204'/><circle cx='560' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='553' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='563' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 554 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 560 182 L 560 215.44'/><path d='M 560 215.44 L 548 250 M 560 215.44 L 572 250'/><path d='M 560.0 190 L 530.0 204'/><path d='M 560.0 190 L 596.0 186'/><path d='M 552 180 C 552 196 568 196 568 180' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='716' cy='196' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='708' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='718' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 709 202 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 716 210 L 716 228.08'/><path d='M 716 228.08 L 704 254 M 716 228.08 L 728 254'/><path d='M 716.0 218 L 702.0 240'/><path d='M 716.0 218 L 730.0 240'/><circle cx='770' cy='206' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='762' cy='204' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='772' cy='204' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 763 212 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 770 220 L 770 231.68'/><path d='M 770 231.68 L 758 254 M 770 231.68 L 782 254'/><path d='M 770.0 228 L 756.0 250'/><path d='M 770.0 228 L 784.0 250'/><\/g><g transform='translate(700,57.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"it was here before i got here, in words that aren't mine.","id":"2026-10-18"},{"date":"2026-10-19","theme":"stay, or walk away","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"christian","text":"it went dark, and she went to work.\nhere's what nobody knew while [[motherteresa|mother teresa]] was alive. she was sure god had spoken to her, once, on a train. leave the convent. go and live with the poorest people there are. so she did. and almost as soon as the work began, the voice went quiet. it stayed quiet.\nfor close to fifty years, when she prayed, there was nothing there. no presence, no comfort. she wrote to the priests she confided in that she felt unwanted by god. that when she lifted her thoughts toward heaven they came back at her like knives. she wondered whether there was a god at all. she called her smile a cloak.\nand every one of those mornings she got up before dawn, prayed into the dark anyway, and went out and washed dying people.\nwe know it from [[comebemylight|her letters]], which came out after she was gone. she'd asked for them to be destroyed.\ni don't think she was faking. i think she decided the work didn't depend on how she felt about it. the feeling had been a gift, the gift was taken back, and the people on the street were still on the street.\nsome days you won't feel it either. the love, the faith, the reason you started. that isn't proof you were wrong. so do the next thing on the list today as if you still felt it. see what your hands remember.","source":"mother teresa, come be my light · her private letters, published ten years after she died","sourceRef":"comebemylight"},"right":{"lineage":"muslim","text":"it went dark, and he walked out.\n[[ghazali|al-ghazali]] had the best job in his world. he was in his thirties, the star professor at the most famous college in baghdad. three hundred students in the room. the men who ran the empire wanted his opinion.\nand it started to go bad on him. he looked hard at his own teaching and saw what was really driving it. he says it flat out in [[munqidh|the story he wrote of his own life]]. he wanted to be important. he wanted to be known. for six months he went back and forth. stay. leave. stay.\nthen one day he stood up to lecture and his tongue wouldn't make a word. not one. after that he could barely swallow food. the doctors looked him over and gave up. this isn't in his body, they said. it's in his heart.\nso he told everyone he was going on pilgrimage, gave away his money, and left. he was gone about ten years. he'd shut himself in a tower in damascus all day to pray. and the book he wrote in those years is the one he's remembered for.\nsometimes the dark is asking you to keep going. and sometimes your own body is telling you a thing is finished, and you're the last to hear.\nlisten today to whatever in you has quit cooperating. the tongue, the gut, the dread on sunday night. ask it what it knows.","source":"al-ghazali, deliverance from error · the section on the ways of the mystics. about fifty pages in all","sourceRef":"munqidh"},"question":"is the dark you're in telling you to stay, or to go?","caption":"the same cloud is over both of them. she kneels down under it and he walks out from under it.","refs":{"motherteresa":{"name":"mother teresa","lines":"an albanian nun who walked out of a comfortable convent school in calcutta in 1948 to live in the slums and pick dying people up off the street, and became one of the most famous women on earth doing it. after she died her private letters showed that for almost fifty years she felt nothing when she prayed, no god, no comfort, nothing, and she kept going anyway. she also took money from crooks and a dictator and ran homes where the dying got very little for their pain, so she's not a poster. if you've ever kept showing up to something long after the feeling left, she did it longer than anybody.\nthe train was on september 10, 1946, going up to darjeeling, in the hills. she'd been a teaching nun for almost twenty years and by her own account a happy one. she called what she heard a call within a call. it took two years to get permission, and then she walked out in a cheap white sari and started with an open air school in a slum.\nin 1961 a priest named joseph neuner told her the darkness might be part of the work, the same abandonment the people on the street felt, being wanted by nobody. she wrote back that for the first time in eleven years she'd come to love it. it didn't lift. she stopped reading it as proof she'd failed.\nthere's another side to her that these pages don't hide. the care in her homes was rough, and she took money from people she shouldn't have. both halves of her stand.","where":"come be my light, the book of her private letters. read the letters themselves and skip around in the commentary."},"comebemylight":{"name":"come be my light","lines":"a 2007 book of mother teresa's private letters to her confessors, the priests she told her inner life to, edited by brian kolodiejchuk, the priest who argued her case for sainthood.\nshe'd asked for the letters to be destroyed. the church kept them. they show that for almost fifty years she felt no presence of god at all when she prayed, and went to work anyway. it came out ten years after she died and surprised almost everybody.\none of the letters says that if she ever becomes a saint, she'll surely be one of darkness. the church made her a saint in 2016, knowing all of it.\npeople read the letters two ways. some say they show she was a fraud, smiling for the cameras with nothing behind it. others say they show the opposite, that anybody can do good while it feels good, and she did it for half a century with the feeling gone. the page takes the second reading. you can make up your own mind. the letters are specific enough to let you.","where":"come be my light, 2007, edited by brian kolodiejchuk."},"ghazali":{"name":"al-ghazali","lines":"a muslim scholar in the eleventh century who had the best teaching job in the religion by his early thirties, three hundred students, a college in baghdad paid for by the government. then, by his own account, he saw that he'd been teaching for the applause, and one day his tongue just stopped working in front of the class. he walked away, lived plain for about ten years, and wrote the ihya, his forty book guide to living islam from the inside, which muslims have kept reading ever since. picture a star harvard professor who freezes at the podium, quits, disappears, and comes back years later with the book everybody's grandmother owns.\nthe college was the nizamiyya in baghdad, the most celebrated school in the muslim world, and he was put in charge of it in 1091. the breakdown came in 1095. he left that november, saying he was bound for mecca, and didn't teach in public again for about eleven years.\nhe's honest about how long it took him to go. he says that for nearly six months he was pulled between the world and the next life, that one day he'd resolve to leave and by evening he'd changed his mind. in the end, he says, it stopped being a choice. god dried up his tongue.\nthe book that came out of the road years is the revival of the religious sciences, a huge guide to living as a muslim from the inside out. it became the most read book in islam after the quran and the sayings of the prophet.","where":"deliverance from error, his short account of his own life. w. montgomery watt's translation is called the faith and practice of al-ghazali."},"munqidh":{"name":"deliverance from error","lines":"al-munqidh min al-dalal, al-ghazali's short autobiography, written when he was past fifty.\nhe tells how he doubted everything, tried the theologians, the philosophers and the ismailis with their infallible imam, and found what he was looking for with the sufis, and how he broke down in 1095 and left baghdad. it's partly a defense against his critics, so scholars read it carefully.\nthe line on the page about wanting to be important is his own. looking at why he taught, he wrote that what moved him was the desire for an influential position and public recognition. he wrote that about himself, at the top of his field.\nhe also says what he'd figured out by then, which is why he couldn't just carry on. he'd been reading the sufis, the mystics of islam, and saw that what they had couldn't be got from study. it had to be tasted. he knew everything about it and had none of it. that's the gap his tongue fell into.","where":"al-munqidh min al-dalal, in english deliverance from error. it's about fifty pages."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261019bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261019bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 150 96 C 110 96 104 44 150 50 C 170 10 262 8 290 44 C 340 16 420 22 440 60 C 500 30 580 44 590 86 C 650 80 664 132 620 136 C 560 150 500 140 470 126 C 420 150 330 148 300 124 C 250 146 170 142 150 96 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.22'/><circle cx='120' cy='244' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='115' cy='242' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='125' cy='242' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 116 250 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 133 246 L 190 250 M 190 250 L 216 236 L 238 256 M 190 250 L 236 254'/><path d='M 146 247 L 164 256'/><path d='M 100 258 L 250 258' stroke-width='5'/><circle cx='286' cy='192' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='279' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='289' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 280 196 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 286 206 L 290 240 L 264 262 L 302 262'/><path d='M 287 214 L 269 228'/><path d='M 287 214 L 265 220'/><path d='M 276 172 C 262 168 262 204 268 214' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 300 176 C 312 184 310 206 306 216' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 271 190 L 264 216 M 304 192 L 310 216' fill='var(--blue)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 219.4 236 C 221.2 250.4 242.8 250.4 244.6 236 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 430 258 L 430 150 C 430 110 510 110 510 150 L 510 258' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 452 258 L 452 214 L 488 214 L 488 258' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 446 214 L 494 214'/><path d='M 510 258 C 560 250 600 262 640 250 C 680 240 720 236 760 214' stroke-width='7' opacity='.25'/><circle cx='613' cy='182' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='611' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='621' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 612 188 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 613 196 L 610 225.6'/><path d='M 610 225.6 L 597 258 M 610 225.6 L 623 258'/><path d='M 612.25 204 L 598.25 232'/><path d='M 612.25 204 L 586.25 206'/><circle cx='580' cy='190' r='9' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 744 214 L 744 108 L 772 108 L 772 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 738 108 L 758 82 L 778 108 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 738 140 L 778 140'/><path d='M 753 126 L 753 118 L 763 118 L 763 126'/><\/g><g transform='translate(746,81.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can tell a dry spell from an ending, if i listen.","id":"2026-10-19"},{"date":"2026-10-20","theme":"you can't read your way there","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"tao","text":"you're reading the leftovers.\na duke is sitting up in his hall, reading. down in the yard, [[wheelwright|a wheelwright named pian]] is chiseling a wheel. the old man sets his tools down, climbs the steps, and asks his lord what's in the book.\nthe words of the sages, says the duke.\nare they alive, these sages?\nno. long dead.\nthen what you're reading, says the wheelwright, is the chaff and dregs of dead men.\nnow, you don't say that to a duke. explain yourself, the duke tells him, or you die.\nand the old man talks about wheels. when i cut a wheel, he says, if i go too easy the chisel slides and won't bite. too hard and it jams. not too easy, not too hard. i feel it in my hand and i know it in my heart, and my mouth can't say it. i can't even teach it to my own son. so here i am at seventy, still making wheels. whatever those old men really knew went into the ground with them. what's left on the page is the dregs.\n[[zhuangzi]] tells it and doesn't add a moral. and he tells it, you'll notice, in a book.\nyou've read plenty. so have i. it isn't worthless. but ask yourself what you know in your hands, the way he knows a wheel. go do that for an hour. the reading will keep.","source":"zhuangzi, the end of chapter 13 · burton watson's translation. half a page","sourceRef":"wheelwright"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"you don't think your way in.\n[[rohr|richard rohr]] has a sentence he's been saying for decades, and it has turned religion right side up for a lot of people. we don't think ourselves into a new way of living. we live ourselves into a new way of thinking.\nmost of us were taught it the other way around. get your beliefs straight first. sign the statement, win the argument, and the right life will follow along behind. so we read and we listen and we collect better and better ideas, and we stay about the same.\nhe's a priest with a shelf of books to his name, and he'll tell you the books aren't it. he named his place in new mexico [[cac|the center for action and contemplation]], and he says the name is the whole program. you go and do something. you sit quietly with what it did to you. then you go do the next thing. the understanding shows up afterward, and it looks nothing like what you'd have guessed from a chair.\nyou already know this from everything you're any good at. nobody read their way into running a long way, or raising a kid, or staying married. you did it badly until you knew something.\nskip the next article about the thing. do ten minutes of the thing itself today, badly. let your feet teach your head.","source":"richard rohr, the last of the eight core principles of his center · then everything belongs, and falling upward","sourceRef":"rohr"},"question":"what do you know in your hands that you keep trying to learn in your head?","caption":"the duke has the book and the old man has the wheel. the wheel is the one that makes it across the fold.","refs":{"wheelwright":{"name":"wheelwright pian","lines":"a character in the book of zhuangzi, in a story half a page long. he's a craftsman who makes cart wheels for duke huan of qi, a real ruler from the 600s bc, and in the story he's seventy years old.\nhis point about the chisel is exact. too gentle and it slides without gripping. too hard and it binds and won't go in. the right stroke is somewhere between, and he says he can get it in his hand and feel it in his mind and can't get it out of his mouth. there's a knack in it, he says, and he can't hand that to his son, and his son can't take it from him.\nfrom there he reasons about the duke's book. the men who wrote it knew something the way he knows wheels. they died, and the part that couldn't be handed down died with them. so the book holds what could be said, and that's the chaff and dregs.\nthe story doesn't say whether the duke let him live. it stops when the old man stops talking.","where":"zhuangzi, the end of chapter 13, called the way of heaven. burton watson's translation."},"zhuangzi":{"name":"zhuangzi","lines":"a chinese thinker from about twenty three hundred years ago who wrote the funniest serious book there is, full of talking trees, happy fish, a cook who never sharpens his knife, and a man who turns down a kingdom because he'd rather be a turtle in the mud. he's the patron saint of everyone who ever turned down the promotion and never regretted it. take from him that being useless to the machine might be how you get to have a life.\nhe keeps coming back to people who are very good at one plain thing. a cook whose knife has stayed sharp for nineteen years because he follows the gaps in the ox. a hunchback catching cicadas on the end of a pole. a wheelwright. none of them can explain it and all of them have it.\nand yes, he wrote a book about how books can't hold it. he knew. one of his best lines says words are like a fish trap. once you've caught the fish you can forget the trap. he just wanted you to notice you were holding a trap.","where":"the inner chapters, in burton watson's translation. the wheelwright is in chapter 13. cook ding, who is the same lesson with a knife, is in chapter 3."},"rohr":{"name":"richard rohr","lines":"a catholic priest in new mexico, in his eighties now, who's spent fifty years telling people their faith isn't dead just because the version they grew up with stopped working. his big idea is that life has two halves, and the second one only starts when something you built falls apart. what most people call a midlife crisis, he calls the door. if you grew up in church and quietly walked out, he's the one saying you might be closer now than you were in the pew.\nthe sentence on the page is one he's used constantly, in books and talks, for most of his teaching life, and he made it the last of the eight principles his center runs on. the long version is that people don't change by getting better information. they change when they do something, usually something that costs them, and then think about it honestly.\nit's a strange thing for a writer to say. he's spent fifty years as a priest writing and talking, and his whole message is that reading and listening to him isn't the point. he says the first half of life is about getting the answers and the second half starts when the answers run out.","where":"falling upward, his best known book. then everything belongs. the line on the page is the eighth of the eight core principles he wrote for his center."},"cac":{"name":"the center for action and contemplation","lines":"a teaching and retreat center he started in albuquerque, new mexico, in 1987. action means working for justice out in the world. contemplation means sitting quietly with god. he put both in the name on purpose.\nthe name was a fight he wanted to end. the activists he knew thought sitting in silent prayer was navel gazing. the people who did the silent prayer thought activism was ego. he told both sides the same thing. do both, or you aren't really doing either one.\nit's the wheelwright's point from the other direction. the sitting without the doing is a duke with a book. the doing without the sitting never finds out what it learned.","where":"the center puts out a short daily meditation by email, which is the easiest way to hear him."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nhe fits today because he'd had every kind of knowing. the degrees, the lectures, then a teacher who gave him almost no words and a plate of food. the book he wrote about it is famous partly for the part you can't read, the hand drawn middle you turn sideways.","where":"be here now, the first part. then miracle of love, the stories about the old man."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261020bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261020bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 30 258 L 30 200 L 170 200 L 170 258' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 170 258 L 170 240 L 196 240 L 196 258 M 170 240 L 170 220 L 184 220 L 184 240'/><path d='M 20 200 L 20 96 M 180 200 L 180 96'/><path d='M 6 96 L 100 62 L 194 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='100' cy='136' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='98' cy='134' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='108' cy='134' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 99 142 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 100 150 L 100 200 M 100 164 L 126 172 M 100 164 L 120 184'/><path d='M 122 166 L 146 166 L 146 190 L 122 190 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 128 174 h 12 M 128 181 h 12' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='260' cy='186' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='252' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='262' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 253 190 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 260 200 L 262 227.04'/><path d='M 262 227.04 L 249 258 M 262 227.04 L 275 258'/><path d='M 260.5 208 L 240.5 234'/><path d='M 260.5 208 L 290.5 208'/><path d='M 250 182 C 250 200 270 200 270 182' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 292 184 L 310 172' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='400' cy='196' r='60' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='5'/><circle cx='400' cy='196' r='13' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 414 199 L 457 208'/><path d='M 408 208 L 432 244'/><path d='M 397 210 L 388 253'/><path d='M 388 204 L 352 228'/><path d='M 386 193 L 343 184'/><path d='M 392 184 L 368 148'/><path d='M 403 182 L 412 139'/><path d='M 412 188 L 448 164'/><path d='M 470 258 C 540 250 600 262 800 252' stroke-width='8' opacity='.2'/><path d='M 500 258 L 500 214 L 532 214 L 532 258 M 500 214 L 496 176'/><path d='M 534 252 L 572 252 L 572 258 L 534 258 Z M 538 246 L 568 246 L 568 252 L 538 252 Z M 542 240 L 566 240 L 566 246 L 542 246 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='662' cy='176' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='660' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='670' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 661 180 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 662 190 L 660 223.44'/><path d='M 660 223.44 L 644 258 M 660 223.44 L 676 258'/><path d='M 661.5 198 L 637.5 218'/><path d='M 661.5 198 L 687.5 216'/><ellipse cx='736' cy='240' rx='20' ry='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='760' cy='228' r='9' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 722 248 l -2 10 M 748 248 l 2 10 M 716 238 q -10 -6 -6 -14 M 764 220 l 6 -8'/><circle cx='763' cy='227' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><\/g><g transform='translate(310,237.0) scale(1.4)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i learn it by doing it.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] knew this one from both sides. he was a harvard professor with the whole library behind him, and none of it did what one afternoon with an old man in a blanket did. when he got home and people wanted the method, what he had was four instructions. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth. the rest wasn't in a book. then he wrote a book about it anyway, and he'd be the first to laugh at that.","source":"be here now, 1971 · the first part, where he tells it himself","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-20"},{"date":"2026-10-21","theme":"mad about mercy","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"he was furious that it worked.\neverybody knows the fish. the fish isn't the point. here's the part of [[jonah]] they leave off the nursery wall.\nhe finally goes to [[nineveh]], the capital of the people who terrorized his people, and he walks in and preaches one sentence. forty days, and this city is finished. that's all. and it works. the whole place turns around, from the king on down. they even put the cows in mourning clothes. and god lets them off.\njonah is livid. he says, i knew it. this is why i ran in the first place. i knew you were soft. i knew you'd forgive them. i'd rather be dead.\nso he goes and sits outside the city in the heat to watch, still hoping for fire. a plant grows up and gives him shade, and he loves that plant. the next morning a worm kills it. and he's angry enough to die, over a plant.\nand god asks him one thing. you cared that much about a vine you didn't even grow. shouldn't i care about a hundred and twenty thousand people who don't know their right hand from their left? and all those animals?\nthe book ends right there. jonah never answers.\nso the question's yours. there's somebody you'd rather see get what's coming to them. you don't have to fix that this morning. just hear what you're being asked.","source":"the book of jonah, chapter 4 · read all four chapters. it takes ten minutes","sourceRef":"jonah"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"he wouldn't go in to the party.\nyou know the first half of this one. the younger son takes his share of the money, wastes every bit of it, and comes crawling home, and his father runs down the road to meet him. everybody loves that part.\n[[jesus]] kept going. there's [[olderbrother|an older brother]].\nhe's out in the field, working, like always. he comes in at the end of the day and hears music and dancing. he asks one of the hired hands what's going on. your brother's back, and your father has killed the fattened calf.\nand he won't go in. he stands outside his own house. so the father comes out to him, too, and pleads.\nand it all pours out. all these years i've slaved for you. i never once disobeyed. you never gave me so much as a goat to have my friends over. and then this son of yours shows up.\nnot my brother. this son of yours.\nthe father says, you're always with me. everything i have is yours. but your brother was dead, and he's alive. we had to.\nand that's the end. we're never told whether he goes in.\nyou've stood out in that yard. doing it right, keeping count, watching somebody who didn't do it right get the party. the door is open. nobody is keeping you out but the count.","source":"luke 15:25 to 32 · start at verse 1, so you know who he's telling it to","sourceRef":"olderbrother"},"question":"whose forgiveness are you still mad about?","caption":"two men sulking outside, back to back, a few hundred years apart. both stories stop before either one gets up.","refs":{"jonah":{"name":"jonah","lines":"the prophet in a four chapter bible story who gets told to go warn the capital of the empire everyone hates, buys a ticket the other way, gets thrown overboard and swallowed by a huge fish, finally goes and preaches, and the whole city actually listens and god spares them, and he's furious about it. it's a comedy, and the joke is on him. picture being told to go help the country you hate most, and then sulking when it works. he's for anyone who, if they're honest, wants mercy for themselves and fire for everybody else.\nthe complaint on the page is almost word for word. in chapter 4 he tells god he knew he was gracious and merciful, slow to anger and full of love, and he says it as an accusation. those are the best things his own scriptures say about god. he's quoting them as the reason he ran.\nhe had cause. assyria was the empire that would wipe out the northern half of his country. nobody reading the book in the old days needed that explained. asking jonah to save nineveh was asking him to save the people who were going to do that.","where":"the book of jonah. four chapters, about ten minutes. don't stop at the fish. chapter 4 is the point."},"nineveh":{"name":"nineveh","lines":"the capital of the assyrian empire, on the tigris river across from modern mosul in northern iraq. for a while it was the biggest city in the world, with huge walls and palaces carved with scenes of the empire's wars.\nto the people of israel it was the enemy capital, the city of the people who destroyed them. the book of jonah is funny because god sends a jewish prophet there to save it. it was destroyed in 612 bc, and the islamic state blew up some of what was left in 2016.\nthe detail about the cows is in the text. the king orders everybody into sackcloth, the rough cloth you wore to show you were sorry, and no food or water for anyone, and he includes the animals. it's meant to be a little funny. the whole book is. the pagans keep behaving better than the prophet.\nand the last line of the book puts the animals in again. a hundred and twenty thousand people who don't know their right hand from their left, god says, and also much cattle. that's how it ends.","where":"jonah 3 and 4. the short book of nahum, a few pages later in the bible, is another prophet cheering the same city's fall."},"jesus":{"name":"jesus","lines":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel who, around the year 30, spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided, until the roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him. strip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off. if what you got in church felt like rules, go read what he actually said.\nthe setup matters. luke says the tax collectors and the people everyone called sinners were all coming around to hear him, and the religious men were grumbling about it. this man welcomes sinners and eats with them. so he tells three stories in a row about something lost and found. a sheep, a coin, and a son.\nthe older brother is aimed straight at the grumblers. they're the ones who stayed home and did it right. he doesn't call them villains. he has the father come out to the yard for the older son the same way he ran down the road for the younger one.","where":"luke 15. read the first two verses before the stories, so you know who he's telling them to."},"olderbrother":{"name":"the older brother","lines":"a story jesus tells in luke 15. a younger son asks for his inheritance early, which is like telling your father you wish he was dead, and blows it all partying in another country. broke and feeding pigs, he heads home to beg for a job as a hired hand.\nhis father sees him far off and runs down the road to him, an old man running, which rich men didn't do, and throws a party before the kid finishes his apology. the older brother who stayed and worked is furious and won't come in. the story ends with the father pleading with him. we never find out if he goes in.\nthe words do a lot of work at the end. the older son won't say my brother. he says this son of yours. the father hands it back gently. this brother of yours.\nand look at what the older one says about his own life. all these years i've been slaving for you. he's been home the whole time and thinks of himself as hired help. the father's answer is that everything was already his. he could have had the goat any day he asked. the story leaves it open whether he ever hears that.","where":"luke 15:11 to 32. the older brother comes in at verse 25."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261021bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261021bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 0 258 L 0 170 L 22 170 L 22 186 L 44 186 L 44 170 L 66 170 L 66 186 L 88 186 L 88 170 L 110 170 L 110 258' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 258 L 44 220 C 44 206 66 206 66 220 L 66 258'/><circle cx='210' cy='60' r='28' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 246 60 L 258 60'/><path d='M 235 85 L 244 94'/><path d='M 210 96 L 210 108'/><path d='M 185 85 L 176 94'/><path d='M 174 60 L 162 60'/><path d='M 185 35 L 176 26'/><path d='M 210 24 L 210 12'/><path d='M 235 35 L 244 26'/><path d='M 330 258 C 330 220 322 190 300 170 M 322 206 C 306 200 296 204 288 212 M 326 226 C 340 216 352 218 356 228' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 300 170 C 290 160 280 164 278 174 M 300 170 C 306 156 318 158 320 168' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 312 252 q 6 -8 12 0 q 6 8 12 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='372' cy='200' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='363' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='373' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 364 207 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 372 214 L 372 246'/><path d='M 342 258 C 342 244 402 244 402 258 C 402 266 342 266 342 258'/><path d='M 372 222 L 388 236 L 364 242 M 372 222 L 356 236 L 380 242'/><circle cx='440' cy='182' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='439' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='449' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 440 189 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 440 196 L 440 228.16'/><path d='M 440 228.16 L 428 262 M 440 228.16 L 452 262'/><path d='M 440.0 204 L 454.0 224'/><path d='M 440.0 204 L 426.0 224'/><path d='M 460 262 L 478 150' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 470 150 L 490 154 L 488 166' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='526' cy='178' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='518' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='528' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 519 182 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 526 192 L 526 226.72'/><path d='M 526 226.72 L 513 262 M 526 226.72 L 539 262'/><path d='M 526.0 200 L 492.0 200'/><path d='M 526.0 200 L 556.0 208'/><path d='M 516 178 C 516 194 536 194 536 178' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 610 258 L 610 150 L 790 150 L 790 258 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 596 150 L 700 96 L 800 146'/><path d='M 640 258 L 640 190 L 690 190 L 690 258' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 716 176 L 772 176 L 772 222 L 716 222 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='732' cy='196' r='7' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 732 203 L 732 220 M 732 208 L 722 198 M 732 208 L 742 198' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='756' cy='198' r='7' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 756 205 L 756 220 M 756 210 L 746 200 M 756 210 L 766 200' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 700 80 q 6 -10 0 -18 M 716 76 q 6 -10 0 -18' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><\/g><g transform='translate(86,143.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can be glad when somebody else gets let off.","id":"2026-10-21"},{"date":"2026-10-22","theme":"the door, and what it costs","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"other","text":"one of the best days of their lives.\n[[griffiths|roland griffiths]] was about the most careful man you could have picked to ask the question. he'd spent decades at johns hopkins studying caffeine and sedatives. dull, solid work. and after years of asking, he got permission to do what no scientist in the country had been allowed to do in a generation.\nhe gave [[psilocybin]], the active stuff in the mushrooms, to ordinary healthy adults. people who had mostly never taken a psychedelic in their lives. a quiet room, a couch, eyeshades, music, and two trained guides who stayed the whole time.\ntwo months later he asked them where it ranked. about two thirds put that one day among the five most meaningful of their whole lives. about a third said it was the most meaningful thing that had ever happened to them.\nthen he gave it to people with cancer who were terrified of dying. for most of them the terror dropped, and it stayed down.\nhe never oversold it. some people have a very hard time in there, and he screened hard for who shouldn't go. but what his people came back saying is old news. it's all one thing. there's nothing to be afraid of. the mystics said so for three thousand years. his finding is that it's in ordinary people, waiting.\ntry believing that for an hour today. that it's already in you, and the wall is thinner than you were told.","source":"roland griffiths and his team at johns hopkins, 2006 and 2016 · michael pollan's how to change your mind tells it carefully","sourceRef":"griffiths"},"right":{"lineage":"native","text":"then the whole world came up the mountain.\n[[sabina|maría sabina]] was a healer in a mountain town in southern mexico. her people had a way of sitting up through the night with somebody who was sick, with [[littlesaints|the mushrooms]], to find out what was wrong and ask for it to be put right. she'd done it since she was young. she called them the little saints. she said they did the talking, and she only translated.\nin 1955 an american banker climbed up and asked to sit in. she let him. two years later he put the whole thing in the biggest picture magazine in the united states.\nand they came. a few at first, then hundreds. young people from california and europe who wanted a trip. the soldiers followed. her neighbors blamed her, and her house was burned. she said that from the day the foreigners arrived, the little saints lost their purity. they didn't work the way they had. she said it flat, the way you'd say a well went bad.\nshe'd used that door for one thing. for the sick, to get well. nobody before, she said, had taken them just to find god.\nthere are places that are yours to walk into, and places you can only be invited into. it's worth knowing which is which. today, when you want something somebody else has been keeping, a story, a place, a remedy, stop at the gate and ask what it's for.","source":"maría sabina: her life and chants, as she told it to álvaro estrada · her own account, the only one there is","sourceRef":"sabina"},"question":"what are you reaching for, and whose is it?","caption":"he's found a careful way to open the door. she's standing at hers, watching the whole world come up the path.","refs":{"griffiths":{"name":"roland griffiths","lines":"a johns hopkins pharmacologist who spent thirty years studying things like caffeine addiction, the least glamorous research there is, then ran the 2006 psilocybin study that got psychedelic science taken seriously again. he later gave it to people with cancer who were terrified of dying, and it helped. then he got stage four cancer himself and faced it the way his volunteers had. if you've wondered whether any of this is real or just hype, he's the careful one who checked.\nthe first study came out in 2006. thirty six volunteers, each given psilocybin on one day and a comparison drug on another, without being told which was which. the numbers on the page are from the follow up two months later. the cancer study was published in 2016.\nthen it came for him. at the end of 2021 he was told he had stage four colon cancer. he said the first reaction was shock, like anyone's, and that after it he felt more awake and more grateful than he ever had. he called the diagnosis a call to celebration. he died in october 2023.","where":"michael pollan's how to change your mind tells his story well. his own 2006 paper is short and readable, and his last interviews, from 2023, are easy to find."},"psilocybin":{"name":"psilocybin","lines":"the chemical in magic mushrooms that does the tripping. about two hundred kinds of mushroom make it, and people in mexico were eating them in ceremonies long before anyone at harvard heard of it.\nfor an afternoon it scrambles the brain's usual filing system. colors, time, and the sense of being a separate me all go loose. some people call it the most important day of their lives. some people have the worst day of their lives.\nit was banned in the us in 1970. now it's back in hospital trials for depression and end-of-life fear, which is roughly where timothy leary and richard alpert thought it belonged in 1961, minus the parties.\nthe risks, said straight. it's still illegal under federal law in the united states and in most countries. in the studies, people with psychosis in their own history or their close family were screened out, because for them it can set off something that doesn't stop when the drug wears off. even among the carefully chosen, a good share had stretches of real fear during the session. the room, the guides and the screening are a large part of why the results look the way they do. nothing on this page is a suggestion to go try it.","where":"pollan's how to change your mind is the plain, careful book on it."},"sabina":{"name":"maría sabina","lines":"a mazatec healer in a mountain town in southern mexico who couldn't read or write and spent her life using mushrooms at night to heal sick people. in 1955 she let an american banker watch, he wrote it up, and within ten years her town was full of hippies and rock fans looking for a trip. her neighbors blamed her, her house got burned, and she died poor. every psilocybin study at johns hopkins, every microdosing podcast, runs back through her kitchen. take from her what it costs when the thing you hold sacred becomes somebody else's weekend.\nthe banker was gordon wasson, a vice president at j.p. morgan and an amateur mushroom scholar. in the magazine article he changed her name. in his own book the same year, and in what he wrote afterward, he printed her real name and the name of her town.\nthere was no comeback. she kept healing the people in her town who still came to her, and she kept saying plainly what had happened. she died poor in 1985. her face is on murals and souvenirs now, including in the town where her house was burned.","where":"maría sabina: her life and chants, her own story as she told it to álvaro estrada, a writer from her town."},"littlesaints":{"name":"the little saints","lines":"what maría sabina and the mazatecs called the psilocybin mushrooms, in spanish niños santos, the holy children. they're not a drug in that way of talking. they're beings you ask for help, and they speak.\ncalling them saints comes from centuries of mazatec belief mixed with catholicism after the spanish came. sabina prayed to christ and the saints and to the mushrooms in the same night.\nthe night vigil is called a velada. it was held at home, in the dark, for a reason. somebody was sick, or in trouble, and needed to know why. it was never a way to have an experience.\nthat's the whole disagreement between today's two pages, and both sides are honest. the scientist found that the door opens for nearly anybody and that what's behind it can heal. the healer found out what happens to a door when everybody comes through it for their own reasons. the careful, screened, guided room in baltimore is partly an answer to her. somebody had to learn it.","where":"her own account, in maría sabina: her life and chants. what happened inside those nights belongs to her people, and this page leaves it there."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nhe's on today's page because he's the honest witness. he was fired from harvard over the drugs, built a whole second life on what they showed him, and still told every audience they were a door and not the house.","where":"be here now, the first part, for the harvard years and the old man. then his talks, where the door and the room come up for decades."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261022bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261022bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 57 255 114 261 190 258 S 323 256 380 258'/><path d='M 380 258 C 450 250 520 190 600 150 C 650 126 700 110 800 96'/><path d='M 400 258 C 470 252 540 200 620 166 C 680 142 740 128 800 122' stroke-width='8' opacity='.2'/><path d='M 60 258 L 60 226 L 230 226 L 230 258 M 60 226 L 52 196 L 88 196 L 92 226' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='108' cy='208' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 205 L 120 205 L 120 211 L 96 211 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 104 216 q 4 3 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 121 212 L 226 216 M 150 213 L 164 226 M 190 215 L 204 226'/><circle cx='280' cy='200' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='271' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='281' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 272 204 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 280 214 L 280 246'/><path d='M 250 258 C 250 244 310 244 310 258 C 310 266 250 266 250 258'/><path d='M 280 222 L 260 250 M 280 222 L 300 250'/><circle cx='330' cy='178' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='332' cy='176' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 323 184 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 330 192 L 330 224.16'/><path d='M 330 224.16 L 318 258 M 330 224.16 L 342 258'/><path d='M 330.0 200 L 304.0 214'/><path d='M 330.0 200 L 300.0 226'/><path d='M 292 196 L 310 196 L 310 222 L 292 222 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 204 h 10 M 296 211 h 10' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 18 150 L 18 110 M 18 130 C 4 122 2 108 14 104 M 18 126 C 32 118 34 104 22 100' stroke-width='2.4' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 664 132 L 664 86 L 728 86 L 728 120' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 654 86 L 696 58 L 738 86'/><path d='M 684 126 L 684 100 L 704 100 L 704 122'/><circle cx='752' cy='46' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='744' cy='44' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='754' cy='44' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 745 53 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 752 60 L 752 83.2'/><path d='M 752 83.2 L 740 112 M 752 83.2 L 764 112'/><path d='M 752.0 68 L 736.0 98'/><path d='M 752.0 68 L 768.0 98'/><path d='M 742 44 C 730 50 730 80 736 90 M 762 44 C 774 50 774 80 768 90' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='430' cy='210' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 430 216 L 431 230 M 431 230 L 426 242 M 431 230 L 437 241' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='462' cy='198' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 462 204 L 463 218 M 463 218 L 458 230 M 463 218 L 469 229' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='492' cy='180' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 492 186 L 493 200 M 493 200 L 488 212 M 493 200 L 499 211' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='524' cy='164' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 524 170 L 525 184 M 525 184 L 520 196 M 525 184 L 531 195' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='556' cy='146' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 556 152 L 557 166 M 557 166 L 552 178 M 557 166 L 563 177' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='588' cy='130' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 588 136 L 589 150 M 589 150 L 584 162 M 589 150 L 595 161' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='616' cy='118' r='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 616 124 L 617 138 M 617 138 L 612 150 M 617 138 L 623 149' stroke-width='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(700,45.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can want the door open and still ask whose house it is.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] is the one to ask here, because he took both pages' side. the mushrooms at harvard opened the door for him, and he never pretended otherwise. then in india he handed his teacher about nine times a normal dose of lsd, and the old man smiled and nothing happened. what he said for the next fifty years sits right between these two pages. the drugs can show you the room. they can't let you live there.","source":"be here now, 1971, and fifty years of his talks after it","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-22"},{"date":"2026-10-23","theme":"all shall be well","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"the sky was full of birds.\n[[hillesum|etty hillesum]] was a young jewish woman in amsterdam when the germans took the country. she was nobody's idea of a saint. a tangled love life, black moods, a notebook full of herself. and in that notebook, in the worst years europe ever had, something starts to happen to her. she begins to kneel. she writes that there's a deep well inside her, and god lives in it, and most of the time it's choked with stones and has to be dug out again.\nfriends offered to hide her. she said no, and went to work in [[westerbork]], the camp where dutch jews were held until the trains came. she carried letters out and medicine in. then she was a prisoner there herself.\nand listen to what she writes from inside the wire. the sky is full of birds. the purple lupins are standing up so tall. two little old women have sat down for a chat. the sun is on my face. and right in front of us, mass murder.\nshe doesn't pick one. she won't let the murder cancel the lupins, and she won't use the lupins to look away. she said those months were the richest of her life.\nwhen her own train finally left, she threw a postcard out of it. we left the camp singing.\ndon't wait for things to be all right today before you look up. it's both. count the birds anyway.","source":"etty hillesum, an interrupted life, and the letters from westerbork · the letter of june 8, 1943","sourceRef":"hillesum"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"all shall be well. she'd earned it.\nit's the most comforting line in english, and you'll find it on tea towels. you should know who said it first.\n[[julian|julian of norwich]] was a small child when the plague came through her city. it killed a third of everybody, maybe half. she watched it come back more than once. she lived her whole life in a century of mass graves.\nat thirty she got so sick they sent for the priest, and he held a cross up in front of her eyes for her to die looking at. she didn't die. over the next hours she was shown sixteen things. she spent the rest of her life, walled into [[anchoress|one small room]] beside a church, turning them over.\none was something tiny in the palm of her hand, no bigger than a hazelnut. she understood it was everything that's been made. and she wondered how it could last, it was so small. the answer came that it lasts because god loves it.\nanother was the sentence. all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.\nshe isn't predicting anything. she's telling you what's underneath. and she argued with it. she asked how it could possibly be so, with all the harm she'd seen, and she wrote down that she asked.\nsay it once today, out loud, in the middle of whatever isn't well. not as a forecast. as ground to stand on.","source":"julian of norwich, revelations of divine love, the long version, chapters 5 and 27 · then chapter 86, the last page","sourceRef":"julian"},"question":"can you say it's well before it is?","caption":"one of them is looking out through barbed wire and the other through a window in a wall. same sky, same birds.","refs":{"hillesum":{"name":"etty hillesum","lines":"a dutch jewish woman in nazi-occupied amsterdam who started a diary at twenty seven because her therapist told her to. it opens like a therapy journal someone her age might keep now, all men and moods and whether she's wasting her life, and within a year and a half it has turned into a running conversation with god, written while the deportation trains were leaving. she had offers to hide, said no, went to work in westerbork, the camp the dutch jews were shipped out through, and died at auschwitz at twenty nine. she had no synagogue and no church behind her, and she came to think god couldn't rescue anyone, so the job was to keep a place for god alive in herself.\nthe line about the birds is from june 8, 1943, two days after she went back into the camp for the last time. the line about the well is from august 1941, early in the diary, when she was still mostly writing about her moods and the men in her life. you can watch her change across about two years of pages.\nwhat she fought hardest wasn't fear. it was hatred. she wrote about being beside herself with rage at the germans, and she wrote that every atom of hatred added to the world makes it a more inhospitable place. she decided the one piece of the world she could keep clear was herself.\nshe was put on the train on september 7, 1943, with her parents and her brother. farmers found the postcard by the tracks and mailed it. she died at auschwitz that november, at twenty nine.","where":"an interrupted life, the selection from her diaries, with the letters from westerbork. arnold pomerans translated both."},"westerbork":{"name":"westerbork","lines":"a camp on the heath in the northeast of the netherlands, close to the german border. the dutch government built it in 1939 to house jewish refugees from germany. germany invaded in may 1940, and in july 1942 the germans took the camp over and made it a transit camp, a holding place where people waited for the trains.\nthe first transport left on july 15, 1942. from 1943 a train left every tuesday. by september 1944 about a hundred thousand jews had been sent on from westerbork, more than half of them to auschwitz. about five thousand came back.\ncanadian soldiers freed the camp on april 12, 1945, and found 876 people still in it. etty hillesum worked there for the jewish council from july 1942, became a prisoner in july 1943, and was put on the train of september 7, 1943.\nshe called it hell, in so many words. she's not somebody who thought the camp was fine. she describes the nights before each transport, the babies, the old people being loaded. the birds and the lupins are in the same letters as that.","where":"her two long letters from the camp are printed with an interrupted life. they were first printed illegally in 1943."},"julian":{"name":"julian of norwich","lines":"an englishwoman in the 1300s who nearly died of an illness at thirty, saw sixteen visions of jesus on what she thought was her deathbed, and then spent the rest of her life shut in a single room on the side of a church, thinking about what she'd seen. she wrote the first book in english we know was written by a woman, and the line everybody knows from it is all shall be well. she lived through the black death, the plague that killed a third or more of europe, so it isn't a greeting card. think of the longest lockdown you can imagine, by choice, for decades, and what came out of it was hope.\nthe sickness came in may of 1373. she wrote down what she saw soon after, in a short version, and then spent twenty years or so thinking about it and wrote it again, long. the long one is the first book in english we know was written by a woman.\nshe doesn't make it easy on herself. right after all shall be well she asks how that can be, given how much damage has been done. she's told that she's seeing only part of it, and that there's a deed still to be done that will make all things well. she doesn't pretend to know what it is. she just reports what she was told, and that it stayed with her.\nat the very end she says she finally learned what it all meant. love was his meaning. it took her fifteen years to get that one word.","where":"revelations of divine love, the long version. chapter 5 is the hazelnut and chapter 27 is all shall be well. the last chapter, 86, is one page."},"anchoress":{"name":"the anchoress","lines":"a woman who had herself walled into a small room on the side of a church for the rest of her life, to pray. a man who did it was an anchorite. the bishop held a service for it that borrowed from the funeral service, because as far as the world was concerned she was dead.\nthe room usually had one window into the church, to see the altar, and one onto the street, where people came for advice. a servant brought food. picture a lockdown you choose, with no end date, that turns you into the town's counselor.\njulian's room was built onto a church in norwich, which was then one of the biggest and busiest cities in england. so the street window mattered. people came to it with their troubles, and she talked to them through it. one visitor, a woman named margery kempe, wrote about going to her for advice.\nit changes how the famous line sounds. it wasn't said from a garden. it was said by a woman who had shut herself in one room in a city the plague kept coming back to, with a window where the whole town brought her its grief.","where":"the ancrene wisse is the old rulebook for the life. her own book barely mentions the room."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261023bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261023bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 260 C 120 257 240 263 400 260 S 680 258 800 260'/><path d='M 110 70 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 170 46 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 236 82 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 300 50 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 360 90 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 424 54 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 486 84 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 548 48 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 612 78 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 676 52 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 40 100 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 20 260 L 20 150 M 120 260 L 120 150 M 220 260 L 220 150 M 320 260 L 320 150'/><path d='M 0 170 L 340 170 M 0 200 L 340 200 M 0 230 L 340 230' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 170 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 60 167 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 160 200 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 160 197 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 260 170 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 260 167 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 70 230 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 70 227 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 270 230 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 270 227 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 170 170 l 5 -6 l 5 6 M 170 167 l 5 6 l 5 -6' stroke-width='1.8'/><circle cx='170' cy='184' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='167' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='177' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 168 188 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 170 198 L 170 228.88'/><path d='M 170 228.88 L 158 262 M 170 228.88 L 182 262'/><path d='M 170.0 206 L 154.0 236'/><path d='M 170.0 206 L 188.0 232'/><path d='M 158 178 C 150 184 150 206 156 214 M 182 178 C 190 184 190 206 184 214' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 352 260 L 352 190'/><ellipse cx='352' cy='190' rx='6.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='352' cy='199' rx='5.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='352' cy='208' rx='5.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='352' cy='217' rx='4.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 372 260 L 372 174'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='174' rx='6.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='183' rx='5.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='192' rx='5.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='201' rx='4.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 392 260 L 392 196'/><ellipse cx='392' cy='196' rx='6.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='392' cy='205' rx='5.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='392' cy='214' rx='5.0' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><ellipse cx='392' cy='223' rx='4.5' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 560 260 L 560 150 L 660 150 L 660 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 660 260 L 660 110 L 790 110 L 790 260' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 650 110 L 725 60 L 800 110'/><path d='M 725 60 L 725 34 M 715 44 L 735 44'/><path d='M 590 180 L 630 180 L 630 220 L 590 220 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='610' cy='198' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='612' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 603 202 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 590 220 L 574 232' stroke-width='2.6'/><circle cx='570' cy='236' r='4' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 500 260 C 500 246 520 246 522 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='498' cy='244' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 493 239 l -2 -6 l 5 3 M 502 238 l 3 -6 l 2 6 M 522 256 q 12 -4 10 -16' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='496' cy='244' r='1.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='501' cy='244' r='1.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><\/g><g transform='translate(760,83.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"underneath all of it, it's well. i can stand on that.","id":"2026-10-23"},{"date":"2026-10-24","theme":"four hours outside","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"other","text":"four hours a day, at least.\n[[thoreau|henry david thoreau]] said he couldn't keep his health or his spirits unless he spent at least four hours a day outside, walking. at least. it was usually more. no errand and no destination. over the hills and through the woods, he says, absolutely free of everything he owed anybody.\nhe put it in an essay called [[walkingessay|walking]], and he's funny in it about the rest of us. the people who sit in shops all morning and all afternoon too, as if legs were made to sit on. he says they deserve some credit for not having all killed themselves a long time ago.\nbut here's the part i'd underline. he admits that sometimes he walks a mile into the woods and he hasn't arrived. his body is out there and his head is still in town, going over some piece of business. and he asks, what business have i in the woods, if i am thinking of something out of the woods?\nso it isn't the miles. you can do twenty and never leave the office.\nhe calls it sauntering, and he has a theory that the word comes from people walking to the holy land. every walk is that walk, he says, if you let it be.\nyou may not have four hours. you've got twenty minutes. take them outside today with nothing in your ears. and when your head goes back to town, go and get it.","source":"henry david thoreau, walking · the first ten pages. it was a lecture before it was an essay","sourceRef":"walkingessay"},"right":{"lineage":"native","text":"sit down on the actual ground.\n[[standingbear|luther standing bear]] was ten when they put him on a train to a school in pennsylvania that existed to scrub the lakota out of him. they cut his hair. they had him point at a name on a wall he couldn't read. for years he was their model student.\nand in his sixties, living in los angeles, he sat down and wrote out what his own people had known that the school never did.\nthe old lakota, he says, loved the earth, and he means it physically. the old people sat on the ground. they lay on it. they liked to take off their moccasins and walk on it bare. it felt, he says, like being close to a mothering power. and they raised their children near it on purpose, because they had noticed something. a person's heart, kept away from nature, goes hard. and once you lose respect for growing things, it isn't long before you lose it for people.\nthat's in a book called [[spottedeagle|land of the spotted eagle]]. he says only the white man ever saw that country as a wilderness. to the people who lived in it, it was tame. it was home.\nyou can test this in about a minute. go outside today and get lower than you usually do. sit on the dirt. put a hand flat on it. feel what's holding you up, and stay until something in your chest lets go a notch.","source":"luther standing bear, land of the spotted eagle, 1933 · then my people the sioux, for the school years","sourceRef":"spottedeagle"},"question":"when was the last time you sat on the ground?","caption":"one of them is four hours into a walk. the other took his shoes off and sat down. it's the same hill.","refs":{"thoreau":{"name":"henry david thoreau","lines":"a stubborn new england oddball who built a tiny house by a pond, lived in it for two years to find out what he actually needed, and wrote the book everybody who's ever fantasized about quitting and moving to the woods is quoting, whether they know it or not. he also spent a night in jail rather than pay a tax that went to a government backing slavery, and the essay he wrote about it got read by gandhi and martin luther king. take from him that you find out what you need by doing without the rest for a while.\nhe wasn't a hermit and he wasn't idle. he worked as a land surveyor, ran the family pencil business, and wrote every day. the walking was the center of the work, the way some people need a desk. for years he kept a record of which plant flowered on which date around his town, and scientists still use those notes to track how the climate has changed.\nhe lived the other side of this, too. when his brother died in his arms in 1842, he could barely function for weeks. the woods were where he put himself back together. so when he says he can't keep his spirits without them, he isn't being cute.","where":"the essay walking, which is about forty pages. then walden, the chapters called where i lived, and what i lived for, and solitude."},"walkingessay":{"name":"walking","lines":"an essay he worked on for years as a lecture and revised on his deathbed. it's part sermon, part joke, part love letter to going nowhere on foot. it has the line that became a motto for the whole conservation movement. in wildness is the preservation of the world.\nthe word theory is at the very start. he says sauntering may come from idle people in the middle ages who wandered the countryside begging and said they were going to the holy land, a la sainte terre, until children called out, there goes a sainte-terrer. he likes a second guess too, that it's from sans terre, without a land, meaning at home everywhere. scholars don't think either one is right. he didn't much care.\nthe passage the page quotes comes a little later. he says he's alarmed when he's gone a mile into the woods in body without getting there in spirit, with some work still running in his head. he wants to shake off the village. the cure he gives is just to notice, and come back to where your feet are.","where":"walking, by henry david thoreau. it was first given as a lecture, and printed in the atlantic monthly in june 1862, the month after he died."},"standingbear":{"name":"luther standing bear","lines":"an oglala lakota kid who got on a train in 1879 to go to carlisle, the first big government boarding school for native children, where they cut his hair and had him pick a new name off a list on the wall. he came out the model student, toured with buffalo bill's wild west, got smashed up in a train wreck, became a chief, quit, and went to hollywood to play indians in the movies and fight to get real ones hired. then in his sixties he wrote the books that said, calmly, that his people had been right about the land all along. think of someone who went through the program designed to erase him and came out with the clearest account of what it tried to take.\nthe school was the carlisle indian industrial school, the first and most famous of the government boarding schools for native children. its founder's motto was kill the indian, save the man. he was in the first class, in 1879. more than a hundred and eighty children who went there are buried in its cemetery.\nhe came around slowly. he taught, clerked, toured with buffalo bill's wild west show, was nearly killed in a train wreck, and ended up playing indians in hollywood westerns. then, near the end, he wrote two books saying the schools had it backwards. native children should be taught by their own people, he said, and white america had plenty to learn from them.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, 1933. then my people the sioux, 1928, for the school years in his own words."},"spottedeagle":{"name":"land of the spotted eagle","lines":"his second book, written in his sixties, about how the lakota lived and raised their children before the reservations. lakota is the people's own name. sioux is the name outsiders gave them.\nthe passage the page draws on runs about a paragraph. the old people came to love the soil, he writes, and sat or reclined on the ground with a feeling of being close to a mothering power. it was good for the skin to touch the earth. the soil was soothing, strengthening, cleansing and healing. that's why, he says, the old indian still sits on the earth and doesn't prop himself up away from it.\nthen the line about the heart. the old lakota was wise, he writes. he knew that a man's heart, away from nature, becomes hard, and that lack of respect for growing, living things soon leads to lack of respect for humans too. so he kept his young people close to it.\nhe wrote that in 1933, in los angeles, fifty years after the school. it reads like a man telling you what was taken, and that it was right all along.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, 1933. the passage is in the pages on how the lakota felt about the earth."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261024bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261024bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 80 250 150 258 230 240 C 300 224 340 200 400 196 C 470 192 520 226 600 246 C 680 262 740 256 800 258'/><path d='M 322 214 L 352 124 L 372 130 L 356 206 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 366 202 L 404 104 L 426 112 L 404 196 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 416 198 L 448 132 L 466 140 L 444 204 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 60 256 L 60 233.5'/><path d='M 33.0 233.5 L 60 193.0 L 87.0 233.5 Z M 40.2 206.5 L 60 166 L 79.8 206.5 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 112 258 L 112 240.5'/><path d='M 91.0 240.5 L 112 209.0 L 133.0 240.5 Z M 96.6 219.5 L 112 188 L 127.4 219.5 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='224' cy='160' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='223' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='233' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 224 164 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 224 174 L 220 208.72'/><path d='M 220 208.72 L 202 244 M 220 208.72 L 238 244'/><path d='M 223.0 182 L 201.0 204'/><path d='M 223.0 182 L 251.0 196'/><path d='M 204 152 L 244 152' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 212 152 C 212 138 236 138 236 152 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 248 180 L 262 246' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 150 250 l 10 0 M 172 248 l 10 0 M 128 254 l 10 0' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='610' cy='190' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='612' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 603 194 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 610 204 L 610 236'/><path d='M 580 248 C 580 234 640 234 640 248 C 640 256 580 256 580 248'/><path d='M 610 212 L 590 240 M 610 212 L 630 240'/><path d='M 598 176 L 594 156 M 606 172 L 606 150' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 668 250 l 16 0 q 6 -8 -2 -10 l -14 0 Z M 692 252 l 16 0 q 6 -8 -2 -10 l -14 0 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 730 256 L 756 196 L 782 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 750 196 L 762 196 M 752 182 L 756 196 L 762 180'/><path d='M 756 256 L 756 232'/><path d='M 520 244 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 529 244 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 538 244 l 4 -16'/><path d='M 556 250 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 565 250 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 700 258 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 709 258 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 718 258 l 4 -16'/><circle cx='700' cy='70' r='26' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 480 70 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 520 90 q 5.6000000000000005 -7.2 11.200000000000001 0 q 5.6000000000000005 -7.2 11.200000000000001 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(404,77.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"the ground is holding me up. i let it.","id":"2026-10-24"},{"date":"2026-10-25","theme":"choose it, or sit in it","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"be happy. that's an order.\n[[nachman|rabbi nachman]] taught that it's a great commandment to be happy all the time. he didn't say it would be nice. he put it right up there with the things god requires of a person.\nand you'd want to know who's talking. this is a man who fought the black pit his whole life, and said so. he buried children. his wife died of tuberculosis, and then he caught it himself. he was dead at thirty eight. nobody on these pages had less business telling people to cheer up.\nwhich is exactly why he's worth hearing. he knew joy wasn't going to show up on its own, so he went after it. get there however you have to, he said. act silly if that's what it takes. and give the broken heart [[hitbodedut|its hour]]. one hour a day, alone, out loud, telling god all of it. then get up and shut the door on it till tomorrow.\nhe isn't asking you to feel good. he's asking you to fight for it, the way you'd fight for anything else you needed to stay alive.\nyou've done this. gotten up on a morning with nothing in the tank and decided how the day was going to go anyway. that isn't fake. it's a discipline.\ndon't wait for the mood today. make the first move. put the song on before you feel like it.","source":"nachman of breslov, likutey moharan, part two, teaching 24 · rabbi nachman's wisdom is the plain way in","sourceRef":"nachman"},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"don't cheer up. stay.\n[[pema|pema chödrön]] tells people exactly where her teaching started. her husband came home one day and told her he was having an affair and wanted a divorce. she picked up a rock and threw it at him.\nthen came the part she really teaches from. the rage didn't leave. it sat in her for a long time, and everything she knew how to do with a feeling like that was some way of getting rid of it. blame him. fix herself. keep busy, make a plan, cheer up. none of it touched it.\nwhat she finally learned, from a tibetan teacher, runs backwards from everything we're told. don't get away from the feeling. stay. find where it actually is in your body, the heat, the knot. don't feed it the story and don't shove it off. a little longer than you think you can stand.\nshe has [[tonglen|a practice]] for it that's almost rude. you breathe the bad thing in. on purpose. yours, and everybody's who woke up with it this morning. and you breathe out a little room for all of you.\nshe says things don't really get solved. they come together and they fall apart. it's the running that wears us out.\nnext time it comes up today, the dread, the anger, the old ache, try not reaching for the fix. thirty seconds. be where it is and breathe it in. see if it's as solid as it looked.","source":"pema chödrön, when things fall apart · the chapter called intimacy with fear, then the one on tonglen","sourceRef":"pema"},"question":"does this feeling need a fight, or does it need company?","caption":"he's dancing on the edge of the pit to keep from falling in. she climbed down into hers and sat.","refs":{"nachman":{"name":"nachman of breslov","lines":"a jewish holy man in ukraine around 1800, a rebbe, which is what hasidic jews call the teacher they gather around. hasidism is a joyful, mystical kind of judaism, and his great-grandfather started it. nachman swung between high spirits and black gloom, and built a whole way of living out of not giving in to the gloom. his main advice was to go off alone for an hour a day, a field or the woods if you can, and talk to god out loud in your own language about whatever is actually going on. it's close to what people now do in a therapist's office or a journal, except the listener is god and you're standing in a field. he died of tuberculosis at thirty eight, his followers never replaced him, and tens of thousands of people still travel to his grave every year.\nthe sentence on the page is his, from his collected teachings. it is a great mitzvah to always be happy. a mitzvah is a commandment, something god requires. the sentence goes on to say you have to make every effort to keep depression and gloom away.\nthe scholars who've studied him say his swings between high and low are well documented, and one biography of him is called tormented master. his own followers read the falls differently, as steps, each drop the start of a higher climb. either way, he wasn't describing a mood he had. he was describing a fight he was in.\nin the last months of his life, sick, he told his followers he knew nothing at all anymore, that he was only a simple man. and then he shouted at them in yiddish. never give up. there's no such thing as despair.","where":"rabbi nachman's wisdom, aryeh kaplan's translation of his conversations, is the easiest way in. the line about joy is in likutey moharan, part two, teaching 24."},"hitbodedut":{"name":"hitbodedut","lines":"rabbi nachman's main practice. the word means making yourself be alone. you go off by yourself for an hour a day, a field or a forest is best, and talk to god out loud in your own words and your own language, the way you'd talk to a close friend.\nnothing is too small to bring up, business and everyday run-ins included. he said that if you can't get anything out, say one word with all the strength you have. it comes on top of the regular prayers and doesn't replace them.\nthis is the other half of the order to be happy, and it's what keeps the order from being cruel. he doesn't say don't be sad. he says give it a set time. pour the whole thing out, the grief and the failure and the complaint, in your own everyday words. then you've said it, and the rest of the day belongs to joy.\nas a boy he did it in a little attic in his father's house, begging god in yiddish. as a grown teacher he told people a field or a forest was best. it may be the simplest practice on these pages. go somewhere alone and say it out loud.","where":"rabbi nachman's wisdom has his plain instructions for it. his followers still do it, often outdoors at night."},"pema":{"name":"pema chödrön","lines":"an american schoolteacher with two kids and two marriages behind her who hit the worst rage of her life in her thirties, found tibetan buddhism in the middle of it, and became a nun. now she's the one whose book gets handed around hospital waiting rooms and passed between friends going through a divorce. she's the friend who doesn't tell you it'll be fine and just sits down in it with you. the one move to take from her is this. when the bad feeling comes, stay with it a little longer than you can stand.\nthe teacher was chögyam trungpa, a tibetan who was teaching americans in colorado. what caught her was an article of his called working with negativity. she said it read as though he were talking straight to her, about her, in the middle of the worst of it.\nshe has a word for the moment the page is about. shenpa, a tibetan word she translates as the hook. it's the half second when the feeling bites and you start reaching for whatever makes it stop. the drink, the phone, the sharp remark, the plan. her whole teaching is to catch that half second and not move.\na hard thing about her story belongs here too. her teacher did real harm to people, and the community he left did more. she stepped away from it late in her life. these pages don't hide that.","where":"when things fall apart. start with the chapter called intimacy with fear. taking the leap is a short one on the urge to escape."},"tonglen":{"name":"tonglen","lines":"a tibetan buddhist practice. the word means sending and taking. you breathe in the pain you or someone else is in, the fear or grief or rage, and breathe out relief, space, a bit of kindness, toward everybody who feels the same.\nit runs backwards from most meditation apps, which have you breathe in calm and breathe out stress. the idea is to stop running from the bad feeling and use it as the thing that connects you to everybody else who's got it.\npema chödrön teaches it more than anything else, and says start with your own pain, since it's right there.","where":"she teaches it step by step in when things fall apart and in start where you are."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nhe fits between today's two pages because he wasn't choosing joy over the feeling or the feeling over joy. he made friends with the thing. a shmoo is a round friendly creature from an old comic strip, and that's about how seriously he took his own hangups by the end.","where":"the here and now podcast, episode 16, the one named for the shmoos. then still here."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261025bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261025bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 250 C 45 247 90 253 150 250 S 255 248 300 250'/><path d='M 640 250 C 664 247 688 253 720 250 S 776 248 800 250'/><path d='M 300 250 C 310 300 330 312 380 312 L 560 312 C 610 312 630 300 640 250'/><path d='M 300 250 C 316 296 334 306 380 306 L 560 306 C 606 306 624 296 640 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.14'/><circle cx='206' cy='150' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='203' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='213' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 204 154 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 206 164 L 214 208 M 214 208 L 196 250 M 214 208 L 248 224 L 262 206 M 208 178 L 176 152 M 208 178 L 244 150'/><path d='M 200 164 C 200 182 216 182 214 164' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 160 136 l -10 -8 M 258 132 l 10 -8 M 150 176 l -12 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 196 138 l 20 0' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 200 138 C 200 128 212 128 212 138' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 250 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 69 250 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 78 250 l 4 -16'/><path d='M 110 250 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 119 250 l 0 -16'/><circle cx='500' cy='248' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='493' cy='246' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='503' cy='246' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 494 252 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 500 262 L 500 294'/><path d='M 470 306 C 470 292 530 292 530 306 C 530 314 470 314 470 306'/><path d='M 500 270 L 480 298 M 500 270 L 520 298'/><path d='M 487 240 C 490 232 510 232 513 240' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 404 306 C 398 292 412 284 424 290 C 436 286 444 298 438 306 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 720 250 L 720 195.0'/><path d='M 682.6 195.0 C 665.0 162.0 698.0 134.5 720 145.5 C 747.5 131.2 777.2 162.0 757.4 195.0 C 742.0 206.0 698.0 206.0 682.6 195.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 120 70 c -19.8 0 -19.8 -21.6 0 -19.8 c 5.4 -19.8 36.0 -19.8 39.6 -3.6 c 21.6 -5.4 27.0 23.400000000000002 5.4 23.400000000000002 c -9.0 0 -36.0 0 -45.0 0 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 600 80 q 7 -9 14 0 q 7 -9 14 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(650,223.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can reach for joy, and i can stay with what hurts.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] had a third way with this, and it's the funniest thing in the forest. after all those years of practice, he said, he still had every one of his neuroses. what changed is that they used to be huge and frightening and they'd run him. now they were little shmoos, friendly little beings, and he'd invite them in for tea. not fighting them. not drowning in them. tea. try that with whatever shows up today.","source":"the here and now podcast, episode 16 · it's named for the shmoos","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-25"},{"date":"2026-10-26","theme":"take it, or argue","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"other","text":"some of it was never up to you.\n[[epictetus]] was born a slave. somebody owned his body, decided where he slept, and, the story goes, broke his leg. he limped for the rest of his life. so when he says what he says, he didn't get it out of a book.\n[[enchiridion|his little handbook]] opens with one sentence, and it's the whole thing. some things are up to us, and some are not.\nup to us: what we make of what happens. what we go after, and what we walk away from. how we act. not up to us: the body, the money, what people say about us, whether we get the position. his own leg was on the second list. what he did about it was on the first.\nand he says nearly all our misery comes from mixing up the lists. we pour ourselves into what was never ours to run. the weather, the boss, the result, somebody else's opinion. and we neglect the one small country that actually belongs to us.\nit sounds cold until you try it. then it feels like setting down a piano.\ntake whatever is chewing on you this morning and sort it. which part is mine? which part never was? be honest. the second pile is bigger than you'd like. put your whole weight on the first one today, and let the other go do what it's going to do.","source":"epictetus, the enchiridion, section 1 · the whole handbook takes twenty minutes","sourceRef":"enchiridion"},"right":{"lineage":"jewish","text":"argue with it anyway.\ngod has made up his mind about the city of sodom. it's settled. he stops on the road and tells [[abraham]] what's about to happen, almost as a courtesy.\nnow, if anything was ever not up to a person, this is it. and abraham doesn't bow his head. the text says he stayed standing there in front of god. and then he stepped closer.\nand he says, wait. you're going to wipe out the good people along with the bad? what if there are fifty decent people in there? you'd kill them too? you? shouldn't the judge of all the earth do what's right?\nand god says, for fifty, i'll spare the whole place.\nso abraham [[bargaining|keeps going]]. i know i'm only dust and ashes, he says, but what if it's forty five? all right, forty five. forty? thirty? please don't be angry. twenty? and then once more, and i'll stop. ten?\nfor ten, god says, i won't destroy it.\nit doesn't save the city. that was never the point. the point is that the father of this whole tradition looked at a done deal, from the highest authority there is, and said, that's not good enough. and the book admires him for it.\nsome things you accept. and some things you walk right up to, even when you're going to lose. ask today what you've gone quiet about and called it peace. then step closer.","source":"genesis 18:16 to 33 · read chapter 19 after, for how it came out","sourceRef":"bargaining"},"question":"what's yours to accept, and what's yours to fight?","caption":"one of them has sat down with his back to the storm. the other is walking up to it with his hand in the air.","refs":{"epictetus":{"name":"epictetus","lines":"a slave with a busted leg who became the most useful teacher rome ever had. everything on a therapist's whiteboard about controlling what you can and letting go of the rest, he said first, and he said it as a man who'd been property. think of him as the original 'not my circus' guy, except he'd earned it.\nthe leg has two versions. in one, his master was twisting it to see what he'd do, and epictetus said calmly, you're going to break it. when it broke he said, i told you so. another old writer says it was just rheumatism. either way he walked with a limp, and either way he'd spent his youth as somebody else's property.\nhe wrote nothing. a student named arrian took down his classes as close to word for word as he could, and boiled them into the handbook. a generation later an emperor, marcus aurelius, was reading those lectures, and thanked the teacher who'd lent him his copy.","where":"the enchiridion, which takes twenty minutes. then the discourses, book 1, for him in the classroom."},"enchiridion":{"name":"the enchiridion","lines":"a short handbook of epictetus's teaching, put together by his student arrian. the word means something held in the hand. fifty three short sections, twenty minutes to read.\nthe first line is the whole thing. some things are up to us and some are not. soldiers, prisoners and people in recovery have carried it around for two thousand years.\nthe two lists are right in the first lines. up to us are our opinions, what we reach for, what we want and what we avoid. in a word, whatever is our own doing. not up to us are the body, property, reputation, and office. in a word, whatever isn't our own doing.\nthen he draws the conclusion. the things on the first list are free by nature, and nobody can block them. the things on the second are weak and belong to others. if you treat what isn't yours as yours, he says, you'll be frustrated, you'll grieve, and you'll blame both gods and people. modern therapy for anxiety borrowed this nearly whole.","where":"the enchiridion, section 1. then sections 5 and 8."},"abraham":{"name":"abraham","lines":"the first father of the jewish people in the book of genesis, and claimed by christians and muslims too. god tells him to leave home for a land he'll be shown, and he goes, and god promises him descendants like the stars.\nthe story everyone argues about is in genesis 22. god tells him to take his son isaac up a mountain and sacrifice him, and abraham walks three days, builds the altar, and raises the knife before an angel stops him. when god calls, he answers hineni, here i am. kierkegaard wrote fear and trembling about it, and leonard cohen sang the word on his last album.\nwhat makes the argument over sodom so striking is who's doing it. this is the same man who, a few chapters on, will walk up a mountain with his son and a knife without one word of protest. for himself he doesn't argue. for a city of strangers he does.\njews, christians and muslims all call him father. the jewish tradition in particular took this scene to heart. arguing with god, on behalf of other people, became a thing a faithful person is allowed to do, and sometimes ought to.","where":"genesis 12 through 25 is his whole story. the argument over sodom is 18:16 to 33."},"bargaining":{"name":"the bargaining over sodom","lines":"it's eighteen verses. god says the outcry against sodom and its neighbor gomorrah is very great, and he's going down to see. the two men with him walk on toward the city. abraham stays where he is, in front of god, and then draws near.\nhe works the number down in six steps. fifty, forty five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten. each time he's more careful about how he asks. he calls himself dust and ashes. he says, let the lord not be angry. and each time the answer is yes. he's the one who stops at ten. god never tells him that's enough.\nthe question he opens with is the one that has lasted. shall not the judge of all the earth do what is just? he's holding god to god's own standard. the city is destroyed in the next chapter, because there weren't ten. his nephew's family is led out first.","where":"genesis 18:16 to 33. the next chapter tells what happened to the city."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nbe here now, his account of all that, came out in 1971. it's a strange square brown book, part memoir and part hand-drawn pages you turn sideways, and it sold a couple million copies. for a lot of kids in the seventies it was the first spiritual book that sounded like them. in 1997 a stroke took his right side and most of his speech. he called it fierce grace, eventually, meaning a gift that nearly kills you. he spent his last fifteen years on maui, slower, kinder, and by his own account further along than he'd ever been when he could talk.\nhe's here today because the stroke is the clearest case in the forest of sorting the two lists. the body and the speech were gone and not coming back. what he did with that was his. he said the stroke did what forty years of meditation couldn't.","where":"still here, the book he wrote after the stroke. then the film fierce grace."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261026bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261026bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 120 253 240 259 400 256 S 680 254 800 256'/><path d='M 330 250 C 300 210 330 180 340 140 C 300 120 316 60 372 66 C 386 22 460 26 470 66 C 520 56 548 110 512 136 C 540 176 500 216 480 250 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.2'/><path d='M 396 150 L 380 186 L 404 182 L 386 226' stroke-width='3.4'/><circle cx='150' cy='198' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='142' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='152' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 143 202 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 212 L 150 244'/><path d='M 120 256 C 120 242 180 242 180 256 C 180 264 120 264 120 256'/><path d='M 150 220 L 130 248 M 150 220 L 170 248'/><path d='M 196 258 L 214 170 M 204 176 L 224 176' stroke-width='3.4'/><path d='M 70 256 C 70 244 92 244 92 256 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 81 246 c -6 -10 6 -12 0 -22' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 232 250 c 8 -8 16 8 24 0 M 262 250 c 8 -8 16 8 24 0' stroke-width='2.4' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='586' cy='172' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='577' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='587' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 578 178 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 586 186 L 590 222.0'/><path d='M 590 222.0 L 576 258 M 590 222.0 L 604 258'/><path d='M 587.0 194 L 547.0 164'/><path d='M 587.0 194 L 567.0 218'/><path d='M 580 186 C 580 206 600 206 600 186' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 618 258 L 630 170' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 700 256 L 700 216 L 720 216 L 720 200 L 740 200 L 740 222 L 764 222 L 764 256' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 730 196 c -8 -12 8 -16 0 -30 M 752 216 c -8 -12 8 -16 0 -30' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 650 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 659 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 20 256 l -4 -16'/><path d='M 29 256 l 0 -16'/><path d='M 38 256 l 4 -16'/><\/g><g transform='translate(104,229.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i let go of what isn't mine, and i speak up for what is.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] got handed the first page the hard way. in 1997 a stroke took his right side and most of his speech, and for a while, he said, it took his faith too. the man who'd talked for a living couldn't talk. then he did the thing epictetus is describing. he quit arguing with the body and went to work on the one piece still his, which was how he met it. he ended up calling the stroke fierce grace. a gift that nearly kills you.","source":"still here, 2000 · and fierce grace, the 2001 film about him","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-26"},{"date":"2026-10-27","theme":"forgiven, and it still costs","mode":"disagree","left":{"lineage":"christian","text":"three times, for the three times.\n[[peter]] had sworn it in front of everybody. the rest of them might run, but not me. i'll die with you. and that same night, by a charcoal fire in a courtyard, a servant girl asked if he knew the man, and he said no. three times, the last one with cursing. then the rooster.\nnow it's afterward. he's gone back to fishing, because what else is there. they're out all night and catch nothing. at dawn there's a man on the beach, and when peter works out who it is, he jumps out of the boat and swims.\non the shore there's [[breakfast]] going. fish on a charcoal fire.\nafter they eat, [[jesus]] asks him. do you love me? yes. he asks again. yes. and a third time, and now peter's hurt, and says, you know everything. you know i do.\nthree, for the three. he never says i forgive you. he never brings up the courtyard. each time peter answers, he just hands him a job. feed my sheep.\nthat's how it's done here. no lecture and no probation. the question gets asked as many times as you failed, and then you're trusted with something.\nsomebody may be waiting on that from you. or you may be waiting to give it to yourself. either way, skip the speech today. make breakfast. hand over something that matters.","source":"john 21:1 to 19 · read chapter 18 first, for the courtyard and the other fire","sourceRef":"breakfast"},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"forgiven, and they still threw rocks.\n[[angulimala]] was a killer. a real one. he lived out on the roads and wore the fingers of the people he'd murdered on a string around his neck. that's what the name means.\n[[buddha|the buddha]] walked down that road on purpose, alone, and by the end of it the man had thrown his weapons away and asked to become a monk. and the buddha said, come.\nthat's the famous part. nobody is too far gone. it's true.\nhere's the part after. he shaves his head, puts on the robe, does the work, and gets all the way free. and one morning he goes into town with his bowl, like any monk, to beg his food. and people know him. a clod of dirt hits him. then a stick. then a stone. he comes back with his head split open and bleeding, his bowl broken and his robe torn.\nand the buddha doesn't say, how terrible. he says, bear it. bear it. you're feeling here and now a small part of what you did.\nso he's free, and it isn't over. both. being forgiven doesn't mean nobody remembers. those people had buried somebody.\nif you've done harm and turned around, good. it counts. and somebody may still throw a rock. let it land today. don't defend yourself. that's part of the turning.","source":"angulimala sutta, majjhima nikaya 86 · a few pages. don't stop at the conversion","sourceRef":"angulimala"},"question":"what do you still owe, even forgiven?","caption":"one of them gets fed breakfast and handed a job. the other gets hit with a rock and told to bear it. they're both forgiven.","refs":{"peter":{"name":"peter","lines":"a fisherman from a lake in northern israel who dropped his nets to follow jesus and became his loudest, most eager friend, and then, the night jesus was arrested, swore three times he'd never met the man. after the execution, jesus found him back at his boat and asked him three times if he loved him, and gave him a job instead of a lecture. he went on to lead the first christians, and the catholic church counts him as the first pope. if you've ever sold out someone you loved right when it counted, he's the one who got asked back.\nthe detail the page doesn't have room for is the fire. the word john uses for a charcoal fire shows up twice in his whole gospel. once in the courtyard, where peter stood warming himself and said he didn't know the man. and once here, on the beach. whoever wrote it wanted you to smell the same smoke.\nand after the three questions comes a hard word. when you were young you dressed yourself and went where you liked. when you're old, someone else will dress you and take you where you don't want to go. the job comes with a cost. tradition says he was crucified in rome about thirty years later.","where":"the gospel of john, chapter 21. the denial is in chapter 18, and in mark 14, which is rougher on him."},"breakfast":{"name":"breakfast on the beach","lines":"seven of them have gone back to the lake and fished all night for nothing. at first light a man on the shore calls out to throw the net on the right side of the boat. it fills so full they can't haul it in. the count is in the text. a hundred and fifty three fish.\nwhen one of them says it's the lord, peter, who's been working stripped down, pulls his coat on and jumps into the water. the others bring the boat in. on the beach there's already a fire with fish on it, and bread. come and have breakfast, the man says. nobody dares ask who he is. they know.\nso the first thing that happens to the man who failed worst is that he gets fed. the questions come after the meal, and so does the work.","where":"john 21:1 to 19. it's the last chapter of the gospel, and reads like something added so the story wouldn't end without it."},"jesus":{"name":"jesus","lines":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel who, around the year 30, spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided, until the roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him. strip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off. if what you got in church felt like rules, go read what he actually said.\nthree questions for three denials is the plain reading, and almost everybody takes it that way. the third time, john says, peter was grieved that he'd asked again. it hurts on purpose. the wound gets touched the same number of times it was made.\nand the answer each time isn't comfort. it's work. feed my lambs. tend my sheep. feed my sheep. he hands his people over to the one man in the group who's proved he can fail. you could read that as the whole point. he wanted somebody in charge who'd never be able to look down on anyone.","where":"john 21 for the beach. then go back to john 13, the supper where peter swears he'd die for him."},"angulimala":{"name":"angulimala","lines":"a serial killer in the buddha's time who lived on the roads and wore a necklace of his victims' fingers. that's what his name means.\nthe buddha walked straight toward him on the road. angulimala ran after him and couldn't catch him, and shouted stop. the buddha said, i've stopped. it's you who hasn't. angulimala threw down his sword and became a monk. he's the buddhist proof that nobody's too far gone, like milarepa for tibet.\nthe part on the page comes later in the same text. by then he's an arahant, which is the word for someone who's finished the path. and he's still walking into the same towns he once terrorized, holding out a bowl. the text says the clod, the stick and the stone were thrown by different people, and each one found him.\nthe buddha's words to him are blunt. bear it. you're experiencing here and now the result of deeds that could have had you burning in hell for a very long time. it isn't a scolding. it's closer to good news. this is what's left of it, and it's bearable.\nthere's a gentler scene in the same text. he sees a woman in a hard labor and can't stand it, and the buddha sends him back to wish her well with a sentence about how, since his new birth, he hasn't knowingly harmed a living thing. she and the baby come through fine. monks in southeast asia still recite those words for women in labor.","where":"the angulimala sutta, majjhima nikaya 86. it's a few pages, and the stoning is near the end."},"buddha":{"name":"the buddha","lines":"the son of a rich ruling family in what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago, who, as the old stories tell it, had everything and walked out on it at twenty nine to figure out why everybody suffers. six years later, sitting under a tree, he figured it out, and spent the next forty five years walking around northern india teaching it. he didn't ask anyone to believe him. he told them to try it and see, which is why so much of it sits comfortably in a therapist's office today. if you want a method more than a belief, start here.\nthe meeting on the road is one of the great scenes. people warn him three times not to go that way. he goes. the killer sees a monk alone and runs after him with a sword, and somehow can't catch him, though the buddha is only walking. he shouts, stop. and the buddha says, i have stopped. you stop.\nthen the king shows up with five hundred horsemen to hunt the killer down, and finds him sitting there with a shaved head. the king is terrified, and then amazed. he says, what we couldn't tame with force and weapons, you tamed with neither.","where":"the angulimala sutta, majjhima nikaya 86."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261027bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261027bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 63 253 126 259 210 256 S 357 254 420 256'/><path d='M 420 256 C 477 253 534 259 610 256 S 743 254 800 256'/><path d='M 0 284 C 100 276 200 292 300 282 L 300 320 L 0 320 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 30 298 q 14 -6 28 0 M 150 300 q 14 -6 28 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 20 262 C 30 286 110 286 122 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 262 L 70 196 L 104 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 308.0 256 L 352.0 247.2 M 352.0 256 L 308.0 247.2'/><path d='M 316.8 247.2 C 308.0 223.0 327.8 218.6 330 198.8 C 336.6 216.4 352.0 225.2 343.2 247.2 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 304 222 l 52 -6' stroke-width='3'/><ellipse cx='318' cy='214' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='344' cy='211' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 330 190 c -10 -16 10 -24 0 -42 c -10 -18 12 -26 4 -46' stroke-width='2.4' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='236' cy='198' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='234' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='244' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 235 205 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 236 212 L 236 244'/><path d='M 206 256 C 206 242 266 242 266 256 C 266 264 206 264 206 256'/><path d='M 236 220 L 216 248 M 236 220 L 256 248'/><circle cx='420' cy='198' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='412' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='422' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 413 202 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 420 212 L 420 244'/><path d='M 390 256 C 390 242 450 242 450 256 C 450 264 390 264 390 256'/><path d='M 420 220 L 392 232 M 420 220 L 440 246'/><path d='M 412 206 C 412 222 428 222 428 206' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='610' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='608' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='618' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 609 180 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 610 188 L 610 222.72'/><path d='M 610 222.72 L 598 258 M 610 222.72 L 622 258'/><path d='M 610.0 196 L 594.0 226'/><path d='M 610.0 196 L 626.0 226'/><path d='M 592 258 C 586 250 596 244 604 250 M 616 258 C 622 248 634 250 632 258' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 600 150 l 8 6' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 670 150 l 10 -4 l 8 6 l -6 8 l -12 -2 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 692 146 l 22 -8 M 694 158 l 20 -4' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 700 196 l 10 -4 l 8 6 l -6 8 l -12 -2 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 722 192 l 22 -8 M 724 204 l 20 -4' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 656 110 l 10 -4 l 8 6 l -6 8 l -12 -2 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 678 106 l 22 -8 M 680 118 l 20 -4' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 760 256 L 760 196.0'/><path d='M 719.2 196.0 C 700.0 160.0 736.0 130.0 760 142.0 C 790.0 126.39999999999998 822.4 160.0 800.8 196.0 C 784.0 208.0 736.0 208.0 719.2 196.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(96,219.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i'm forgiven, and i can take what's still coming.","id":"2026-10-27"},{"date":"2026-10-28","theme":"feed people","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"feed people.\nwhen the young americans started turning up in the hills to see [[neemkaroli|neem karoli baba]], they wanted the secret. they'd come a long way. they wanted a method, a mantra, the practice that would get them to where he was.\nhe mostly wouldn't teach. he was a heavy old man wrapped in a plaid blanket on a wooden bench, joking, teasing them, sending them off to eat. the first thing that happened to anybody who walked into one of [[hanuman|his temples]], rich or poor, holy or not, was that somebody put a plate of food in their hands.\nand when they pushed him, what [[ramdass|ram dass]] carried home was about as short as an answer gets. love everyone. serve everyone. remember god. tell the truth.\nyou can almost feel them sag. that's it? a kindergarten teacher says that.\nright. so try it for one day. love everyone. the guy who cut you off, too. serve everyone, including when you're tired. it turns out to be the whole mountain.\nhe left no book and no system. he left a handful of people who went home and tried to do it. one of them helped wipe out smallpox.\ndon't go looking for the advanced teaching today. you already have it. find one person and feed them. an actual meal, if you can manage it.","source":"neem karoli baba, as ram dass tells it in be here now and miracle of love · the stories, since he left no book","sourceRef":"neemkaroli"},"right":{"lineage":"christian","text":"then do it again tomorrow.\n[[dorothyday|dorothy day]] had a past, and she never hid it. lovers, an abortion, a jail cell, a baby with a man who didn't believe in marriage or in god. then she found god herself, and it cost her that man, and she said for the rest of her life that it broke her heart.\nin 1933, at the bottom of the depression, she and a wandering frenchman started a newspaper that sold for a penny. pretty soon hungry people were knocking, so they made soup. people needed a bed, so they found a house. that's [[catholicworker|the catholic worker]]. no forms. nobody had to prove they deserved it. you came, and you ate.\nand she didn't run it from an office. she lived in the house, for forty seven years. the same soup, the same donated clothes, the same line of faces. her diaries are full of how tired she was and how sick she got of everybody.\nthat's the part i want you to hear. feeding people is lovely as an idea. as a practice, it's every single morning, and some of them are rude, and it never ends. she's supposed to have said, don't call me a saint. i don't want to be dismissed that easily.\nbecause if she's a saint, you're off the hook. she wasn't one. she was a tired woman who kept making soup.\nwhatever your soup line is, the thankless daily thing, do it today without waiting to feel holy.","source":"dorothy day, the long loneliness · then her diaries, the duty of delight","sourceRef":"dorothyday"},"question":"who can you feed today?","caption":"the old man in the blanket slides the first plate onto the table, and her end of it has a line out the door.","refs":{"neemkaroli":{"name":"neem karoli baba","lines":"an old man in a plaid blanket in the hills of north india who fed everybody, gave almost no talks, and seemed to know what you were thinking before you did. a fired harvard professor, a hippie doctor who helped wipe out smallpox, and later a kid named steve jobs all went looking for him. picture the grandpa the whole neighborhood goes to, except the neighborhood turned out to be half the world. take from him the short list he gave out. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.\nhe wrote nothing and gave almost no talks. what we have is what the people around him wrote down about what he did to them. he'd vanish from one place and turn up in another without a word. he went by different names in different towns. people called him maharaj-ji, which just means great king and is what you call a holy man you love.\nthe after is what his people did. ram dass spent fifty years on those four instructions. a young american doctor named larry brilliant, whom the old man kept pushing toward the united nations, went to work on the campaign that ended smallpox. a kid from long island named krishna das went home and, after some lost years, started leading rooms full of strangers in singing.","where":"miracle of love, the big collection of stories about him that ram dass put together. be here now tells how they met."},"hanuman":{"name":"hanuman, and the temples","lines":"the monkey god of hindu stories. in the ramayana, india's great old epic, he's the servant of the god-king rama, strong enough to leap across the ocean to find rama's kidnapped wife and humble enough to want nothing for it.\nhe's the picture of devotion without ego, the perfect servant. that's why neem karoli baba built temples to him, and why ram dass, whose name means servant of god, named his foundation after him. you'll see his statue all over india, often painted orange.\nthe food is the thing people remember first. at his temples you were fed before anything else happened, whoever you were. in india that's an old religious act. food that's been offered and then shared out is called prasad, and feeding strangers is counted as worship.\nso the plate wasn't hospitality before the teaching started. it was the teaching. a temple to the perfect servant, where the first thing done to you is that you get served.","where":"miracle of love. the temples at kainchi and vrindavan are still there, and still feed whoever comes."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nhe was the one who brought the old man's words to the west. he arrived in 1967 as richard alpert, a harvard psychologist who'd been fired, and the old man told him things about his dead mother that nobody could have known. he came home with a new name, which means servant of god.\nwhat he did with the four instructions is the proof they aren't simple. he helped start a charity that pays for eye surgery for the blind. he sat with dying people for years. he got books into prisons. he wrote a whole book called how can i help? and he said the hardest of the four, by a long way, was the first.","where":"be here now, the first part. then miracle of love."},"dorothyday":{"name":"dorothy day","lines":"a radical young journalist in 1910s new york, drinking with playwrights and getting arrested with the women fighting for the vote, who had an abortion, then a baby with a man she never married, and then became catholic and lost him over it. in 1933 she started a penny newspaper and a string of soup kitchens and shelters that are still running, and she lived in them, with whoever came through the door, for almost fifty years. picture a new york activist who never took a salary, never moved out of the shelter, and was still getting arrested at seventy five. she's for anyone who thinks faith should look like somebody getting fed tonight.\nthe man was forster batterham, the father of her daughter tamar. he was an anarchist who loved the beach and wanted nothing to do with churches. she was happier with him than she'd ever been. she left because she couldn't have both, and she wrote about it without pretending it had stopped hurting.\nthe frenchman was peter maurin, a drifter twenty years older than her, who turned up at her apartment in december 1932 with a plan to rebuild the world. the first paper went on sale may 1, 1933.\nthe line about not being called a saint is one she's said to have used more than once, in one form or another. the catholic church has opened the process to make her one anyway.","where":"the long loneliness, her autobiography. then her diaries, published as the duty of delight, for the tired and cranky days."},"catholicworker":{"name":"the catholic worker","lines":"a newspaper and a movement dorothy day and peter maurin started in new york on may 1, 1933. the paper cost a penny and still does. it said the church belonged with the poor and the unions, and it said so in the middle of the great depression.\nthen came the soup lines, then houses where anyone could get a bed and a meal, then farms. today there are more than two hundred catholic worker houses, run by volunteers who live in them with the people who come, no salaries.\npicture a homeless shelter where the staff sleep down the hall, eat the same soup, and nobody fills out a form.","where":"the paper still comes out and still costs a penny. the long loneliness tells how it started."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261028bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261028bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 250 258 L 250 206 L 620 206 L 620 258'/><path d='M 238 206 L 632 206' stroke-width='4'/><ellipse cx='330' cy='200' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='400' cy='200' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='470' cy='200' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='540' cy='200' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 258 L 60 214 L 210 214 L 210 258'/><path d='M 50 214 L 220 214' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='136' cy='120' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='134' cy='118' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='144' cy='118' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 135 124 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 96 214 C 86 170 104 140 136 138 C 170 140 188 170 178 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 110 150 L 104 212 M 132 140 L 130 212 M 156 144 L 160 212 M 96 180 L 180 180 M 100 198 L 178 198' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 174 170 L 230 186' stroke-width='3'/><ellipse cx='246' cy='188' rx='18' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='30' cy='216' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='28' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='38' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 29 220 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 30 230 L 30 240.4'/><path d='M 30 240.4 L 18 262 M 30 240.4 L 42 262'/><path d='M 30.0 238 L 14.0 268'/><path d='M 30.0 238 L 46.0 268'/><path d='M 664 206 L 660 156 L 730 156 L 726 206 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 652 156 L 738 156'/><path d='M 680 146 c -8 -10 8 -14 0 -26 M 708 146 c -8 -10 8 -14 0 -26' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.55'/><circle cx='640' cy='110' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='632' cy='108' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='642' cy='108' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 633 116 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 640 124 L 640 206 M 640 140 L 612 164 M 640 140 L 672 150'/><path d='M 626 104 C 620 110 622 128 626 134 M 654 104 C 660 110 658 128 654 134' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 672 150 L 690 140' stroke-width='3.4'/><circle cx='566' cy='184' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='558' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='568' cy='182' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 559 188 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 566 198 L 566 228.88'/><path d='M 566 228.88 L 554 262 M 566 228.88 L 578 262'/><path d='M 566.0 206 L 584.0 222'/><path d='M 566.0 206 L 552.0 232'/><path d='M 574.8 208 C 576.4 220.8 595.6 220.8 597.2 208 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='752' cy='196' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='744' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='754' cy='194' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 745 202 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 752 210 L 752 233.2'/><path d='M 752 233.2 L 740 262 M 752 233.2 L 764 262'/><path d='M 752.0 218 L 736.0 248'/><path d='M 752.0 218 L 768.0 248'/><circle cx='786' cy='210' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='778' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='788' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 779 214 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 786 224 L 786 238.24'/><path d='M 786 238.24 L 774 262 M 786 238.24 L 798 262'/><path d='M 786.0 232 L 770.0 262'/><path d='M 786.0 232 L 802.0 262'/><\/g><g transform='translate(196,187.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i don't need to feel holy to make the soup.","id":"2026-10-28"},{"date":"2026-10-29","theme":"i was wrong, in public","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"muslim","text":"he took it back in front of everybody.\nfor twelve years [[malcolmx|malcolm x]] stood up in front of crowds and said white people were devils. all of them. he didn't mumble it. it was the center of what he preached, he was better at it than anyone alive, and it had his name on it.\nthen in 1964 he went on [[hajj|the pilgrimage to mecca]]. and for days he ate from the same plate and slept on the same rug and prayed in the same rows with people of every color there is. men with blue eyes and blond hair, he wrote, who treated him like a brother. and it didn't fit. the thing he had built his name on did not fit what was in front of his face.\nso he sat down and wrote a letter home, and asked that it be given to the press.\nhe said he'd been forced to rearrange much of what he thought, and to toss aside some of his old conclusions. he didn't blame his teachers. he didn't say he'd been misunderstood. he said it under his own name, to the people who had loved him for the other thing.\nhe had less than a year to live, and he knew men wanted him dead.\nyou have one of these. something you've said so often it's turned into who you are, and some part of you knows it isn't quite true anymore. you don't have to tell the world today. say it once, out loud, to yourself.","source":"malcolm x, the letter from mecca, april 1964 · it's in the autobiography, in the chapter called mecca","sourceRef":"hajj"},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"egg on his beard.\nby 1974 [[ramdass|ram dass]] was famous. then the old man in india who'd given him his name, [[neemkaroli|neem karoli baba]], died, and he was lost.\na year later a teacher he knew took him to a basement in brooklyn to meet [[joya|a woman named joya]], a mother of three who said his dead teacher was speaking through her. in a trance she said things only he and the old man had talked about. he believed her. for fifteen months he sat with her nearly every day and told interviewers and crowds she was enlightened.\nthen it came apart. the cancer, the miracles, the people out to get her, none of it was true, and her helpers admitted they'd made up emergencies on her say so. when he tried to leave, her people kept his phone ringing and watched his door for months.\nhe could have let it fade quietly. plenty of teachers have.\ninstead he wrote an article for yoga journal and called it egg on my beard. he said he'd fooled himself and helped fool other people, and that his teacher had insisted he tell the truth no matter how embarrassing. he didn't settle the score. he said he still didn't know what she was, and that some good had come of it.\nthat article is why you can trust the rest of him.\nfind one place today where you're still protecting a mistake. tell on yourself to one person, no excuse attached. notice how light it gets.","source":"ram dass, egg on my beard, yoga journal, 1976 · he tells it again in being ram dass","sourceRef":"joya"},"question":"what are you still defending that you already know is wrong?","caption":"two famous men at one podium, each holding up the thing he got wrong where the whole crowd can see it.","refs":{"malcolmx":{"name":"malcolm x","lines":"a kid whose father died under a streetcar in what the family always believed was a murder, and whose mother was locked in a state hospital, who became a street hustler in boston and harlem, went to prison at twenty, taught himself words by copying the dictionary, and came out the fiercest voice of black anger in america. for twelve years he preached that white people were devils. then he went on the pilgrimage to mecca, prayed next to blue-eyed muslims, publicly changed his mind, and was shot dead less than a year later. he's for anyone who's had to admit in public that they were wrong about something big, with enemies watching.\nthe movement he'd preached for was the nation of islam, a black american religious movement that used the name of islam and taught its own things, including that white people were a race of devils. he'd found it in prison and it had pulled him out of a life of hustling and burglary. he gave it twelve years and built it from a few hundred members into tens of thousands. by early 1964 he had broken with its leader.\nhe didn't go soft after mecca. he still said america was racist to the bone. what he dropped was the blanket judgment. he said he'd let himself be used to make sweeping indictments of all white people, and that he wouldn't do it again.\nhe was shot to death on a stage in new york on february 21, 1965, ten months after the letter. he was thirty nine.","where":"the autobiography of malcolm x, as told to alex haley. the mecca chapter has the letter in it."},"hajj":{"name":"the hajj","lines":"the pilgrimage to mecca, in saudi arabia, that every muslim who can afford it and is healthy enough is supposed to make once in their life. millions go every year, all dressed alike in plain white cloth, rich and poor, every color, doing the same rituals side by side.\nthat sameness is what broke malcolm x open in april 1964. he wrote home that he'd eaten from the same plate as blue-eyed, blond muslims, and it had made him rethink everything he'd preached about race. someone who's made it gets the title hajj, which is where el-hajj malik el-shabazz, his new name, comes from.\nthe letter was written in april 1964 to his assistants back in harlem, and he asked them to make copies for the press. he signed it with his new name, el-hajj malik el-shabazz. he knew exactly how it would land. he wrote that people might be shocked to hear these words from him, and that he had always been a man who tries to face facts.","where":"the autobiography of malcolm x, the chapter called mecca."},"neemkaroli":{"name":"neem karoli baba","lines":"an old man in a plaid blanket in the hills of north india who fed everybody, gave almost no talks, and seemed to know what you were thinking before you did. a fired harvard professor, a hippie doctor who helped wipe out smallpox, and later a kid named steve jobs all went looking for him. picture the grandpa the whole neighborhood goes to, except the neighborhood turned out to be half the world. take from him the short list he gave out. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.\nin 1967 richard alpert, a harvard psychologist who'd been fired for his drug experiments, was brought to him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. alpert hadn't told anyone. he cried for two days and went home as ram dass. the westerners who followed him got a short list of instructions and a lot of food.\nhe died in the early hours of september 11, 1973, in a hospital in vrindavan, after leaving kainchi on a night train. the next year a nineteen year old named steve jobs, who'd go on to start apple, went to india to find him and got there too late.","where":"miracle of love, ram dass's book of stories about him from the people who knew him. be here now for the first meeting."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nthe instruction he was trying to keep was one of four his teacher in india gave him. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth. his teacher had died in september 1973. that's part of how he came to be so easy to fool the next year. he badly wanted the connection back.\nthe title of the article is a joke at his own expense, the way you'd say egg on my face. he was famous for the long beard. he used the same move for the rest of his life. when something in him was embarrassing, he'd say it from the stage before anyone else could.","where":"be here now, for the man before this happened. his late book being ram dass tells the joya years plainly."},"joya":{"name":"joya","lines":"born joyce green in brooklyn in 1940, married at sixteen, three kids. around 1972 she started having visions, of jesus and of neem karoli baba, and she came to the attention of hilda charlton, a spiritual teacher in new york. she went by joya then. in 1974 hilda brought ram dass to her basement, about a year after his own teacher in india died, and joya told him the old man was speaking through her. she became his teacher.\nhe told the people who trusted him that she was the genuine article. so when he saw he'd been wrong, it wasn't only his own mistake to clean up. other people had followed him in.\nthat's why the article matters more than a private apology would have. he'd vouched for her in public, so he took it back in public, in the main magazine of the american yoga world, with his name at the top.\nshe moved to florida in 1976 and built an ashram there, kashi, took the name ma jaya, and from 1990 ran a program for people with hiv and aids. she died in 2012. this is his page, not hers.","where":"ram dass's own account is in being ram dass. the article was in yoga journal in 1976."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261029bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261029bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 340 258 L 350 170 L 450 170 L 460 258 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 336 170 L 464 170' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 372 170 L 368 136 M 428 170 L 432 136'/><circle cx='368' cy='130' r='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='432' cy='130' r='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='300' cy='172' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='292' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='302' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 293 178 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 300 186 L 300 222.0'/><path d='M 300 222.0 L 287 258 M 300 222.0 L 313 258'/><path d='M 300.0 194 L 270.0 158'/><path d='M 300.0 194 L 322.0 204'/><path d='M 290 170 h 8 M 302 170 h 8 M 298 170 h 4' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 286 168 h 26' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 250 108 L 286 100 L 292 126 L 256 134 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 262 114 l 20 -4 M 264 122 l 20 -4' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='500' cy='174' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='498' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='508' cy='172' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 499 178 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 500 188 L 500 222.72'/><path d='M 500 222.72 L 487 258 M 500 222.72 L 513 258'/><path d='M 500.0 196 L 478.0 206'/><path d='M 500.0 196 L 530.0 162'/><path d='M 488 176 C 486 206 514 206 512 176' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 492 190 C 494 214 506 214 508 190' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><ellipse cx='536' cy='110' rx='9' ry='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 526 122 l -4 8 M 536 124 l 0 8' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='60' cy='212' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='58' cy='210' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='68' cy='210' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='63' cy='218' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 226 L 60 238.96'/><path d='M 60 238.96 L 48 262 M 60 238.96 L 72 262'/><path d='M 60.0 234 L 44.0 264'/><path d='M 60.0 234 L 76.0 264'/><circle cx='110' cy='204' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='108' cy='202' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='118' cy='202' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='113' cy='210' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 110 218 L 110 236.08'/><path d='M 110 236.08 L 98 262 M 110 236.08 L 122 262'/><path d='M 110.0 226 L 94.0 256'/><path d='M 110.0 226 L 126.0 256'/><circle cx='164' cy='216' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='162' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='172' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 163 222 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 164 230 L 164 240.4'/><path d='M 164 240.4 L 152 262 M 164 240.4 L 176 262'/><path d='M 164.0 238 L 148.0 268'/><path d='M 164.0 238 L 180.0 268'/><circle cx='214' cy='208' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='212' cy='206' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='222' cy='206' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='217' cy='214' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 214 222 L 214 237.52'/><path d='M 214 237.52 L 202 262 M 214 237.52 L 226 262'/><path d='M 214.0 230 L 198.0 260'/><path d='M 214.0 230 L 230.0 260'/><circle cx='600' cy='210' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='592' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='602' cy='208' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='597' cy='216' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 600 224 L 600 238.24'/><path d='M 600 238.24 L 588 262 M 600 238.24 L 612 262'/><path d='M 600.0 232 L 584.0 262'/><path d='M 600.0 232 L 616.0 262'/><circle cx='652' cy='202' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='644' cy='200' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='654' cy='200' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 645 206 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 652 216 L 652 235.36'/><path d='M 652 235.36 L 640 262 M 652 235.36 L 664 262'/><path d='M 652.0 224 L 636.0 254'/><path d='M 652.0 224 L 668.0 254'/><circle cx='704' cy='216' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='696' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='706' cy='214' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='701' cy='222' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 704 230 L 704 240.4'/><path d='M 704 240.4 L 692 262 M 704 240.4 L 716 262'/><path d='M 704.0 238 L 688.0 268'/><path d='M 704.0 238 L 720.0 268'/><circle cx='756' cy='206' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='748' cy='204' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='758' cy='204' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 749 212 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 756 220 L 756 236.8'/><path d='M 756 236.8 L 744 262 M 756 236.8 L 768 262'/><path d='M 756.0 228 L 740.0 258'/><path d='M 756.0 228 L 772.0 258'/><\/g><g transform='translate(392,143.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i can say i was wrong, out loud, and still be standing here.","id":"2026-10-29"},{"date":"2026-10-30","theme":"you don't get the answer first","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"buddhist","text":"pull the arrow out first.\nthere was a monk named malunkyaputta who'd had enough. he'd been at this a good while and nobody had answered the big ones. is the universe eternal or not? does it have an edge? is the soul the same thing as the body? what happens to an awakened person after death? so he goes to [[buddha|the buddha]] with an ultimatum. answer me, or i quit.\nthe buddha asks him, did i ever promise you that? did i say, come and follow me and i'll explain the universe? no.\nthen he tells him about [[poisonedarrow|a man shot with a poisoned arrow]]. his friends run and get a surgeon. and the man says, hold on. nobody touches this arrow until i know who shot me. what's his name? is he tall or short? what town is he from? what kind of bow was it? what's the string made of? what bird did the feathers come off?\nthat man will die, the buddha says, and he still won't know.\ni teach one thing, he says. that it hurts, and how the hurting stops. the rest i've left alone on purpose. it doesn't help.\nyou know which questions you've turned into conditions. i'll start when i understand why it happened. i'll forgive when i know what they were thinking. i'll commit when i'm sure. meanwhile the arrow's still in.\ndrop the condition on one thing today. don't figure it out. start.","source":"the shorter discourse to malunkya, majjhima nikaya 63 · two pages. thanissaro bhikkhu's translation","sourceRef":"poisonedarrow"},"right":{"lineage":"other","text":"live the question.\nin 1903 a young army cadet wrote to the poet [[rilke|rainer maria rilke]]. he was nineteen, unsure of himself, and full of questions. about his writing, about love, about what to do with his life. he wanted somebody older to just tell him.\nrilke was only twenty seven himself, and he wrote back, more than once. and in [[letterstoayoungpoet|one of those letters]] he says the thing nobody wants to hear at nineteen, or at fifty.\nbe patient, he says, with everything that's unsolved in your heart. try to love the questions themselves, like locked rooms, or books written in a language you can't read yet. don't go hunting for the answers. they can't be given to you now, because you wouldn't be able to live them. and the point is to live everything. live the questions now. and maybe some day far off, without even noticing, you'll find you've lived your way into the answer.\nthat's a different patience than waiting. he isn't saying sit still until it clears up. he's saying go ahead and live, with the question along for the ride.\nmost of the big ones don't get answered. they get walked through.\ntake the question you're most tired of today. don't answer it. don't even try. carry it kindly, the way you'd carry something alive, and go do your day.","source":"rainer maria rilke, letters to a young poet, the fourth letter, july 16, 1903 · read all ten","sourceRef":"letterstoayoungpoet"},"question":"what are you refusing to start until you understand it?","caption":"one of them won't let the arrow out until it's explained. the other got a letter that said keep walking.","refs":{"buddha":{"name":"the buddha","lines":"the son of a rich ruling family in what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago, who, as the old stories tell it, had everything and walked out on it at twenty nine to figure out why everybody suffers. six years later, sitting under a tree, he figured it out, and spent the next forty five years walking around northern india teaching it. he didn't ask anyone to believe him. he told them to try it and see, which is why so much of it sits comfortably in a therapist's office today. if you want a method more than a belief, start here.\nhe was famous for the questions he wouldn't answer. there's a standing list of them in the old texts, usually ten. whether the world is eternal or not, finite or not, whether the soul and the body are one thing or two, and four versions of what happens to an awakened person after death. people brought them to him all the time and he set them aside every time.\nit wasn't that he didn't care about truth. he compared himself to a doctor. he said he taught suffering, where it comes from, that it ends, and the way to end it, because that's what helps, and that holding a view about the universe leaves a person exactly as old, sick and scared as before.","where":"the shorter discourse to malunkya, majjhima nikaya 63. it's two pages."},"poisonedarrow":{"name":"the poisoned arrow","lines":"a story the buddha told a monk named malunkyaputta, who said he'd quit unless the buddha answered the big questions. is the universe eternal? is it infinite? does a buddha exist after death?\nthe buddha said it's like a man shot with a poisoned arrow who won't let the doctor pull it out until he knows who shot it, what caste he was, how tall, what the bow was made of. he'll die first. i teach suffering and the end of suffering, he said. the rest doesn't help.\nthe list in the text goes on a lot longer than the page has room for. the wounded man wants the archer's caste, his name and clan, his height, the color of his skin, his village. then whether it was a longbow or a crossbow, what the bowstring was made of, whether the shaft was wild or cultivated wood, which bird's feathers were on it, what animal's sinew bound it, and what kind of point it had. it's meant to be funny, and then it isn't.\nthe monk takes it well, for what it's worth. the text ends by saying he was satisfied and glad of what he'd been told.","where":"majjhima nikaya 63, the shorter discourse to malunkya. thanissaro bhikkhu's translation is free and plain."},"rilke":{"name":"rainer maria rilke","lines":"a thin, charming, usually broke poet from prague who lived off rich admirers and wrote the letters every writing student gets handed sooner or later, the ones that say be patient with what you don't understand yet and live the questions. he also walked out on his wife and baby to do it, and never really went back. he's the guy whose advice about art is perfect and whose life you shouldn't look at too closely. take the advice anyway. it's good.\nthe cadet was franz kappus, a student at an austrian military academy. rilke had been put through military school himself as a boy, and had hated it. kappus sent some poems and asked whether they were any good. rilke mostly declined to say. he told him to stop asking other people and go inward.\nthere's something the letters don't tell you. the man giving this beautiful advice about patience had, around that time, left his wife and baby daughter to go and write. he lived apart from them for most of his life. it doesn't make the advice wrong. it's worth knowing who it came from.","where":"letters to a young poet. ten letters, an afternoon. stephen mitchell's translation is the easy one."},"letterstoayoungpoet":{"name":"letters to a young poet","lines":"ten letters rilke wrote between 1903 and 1908 to franz kappus, a young military cadet who'd sent him poems and asked if they were any good. kappus published them in 1929, after rilke died.\nrilke doesn't really answer the question. he tells him to go inside and ask whether he'd die if he couldn't write, to be patient with everything unsolved in his heart, and to live the questions. every writing student gets handed it sooner or later. it's short, you can read it in an afternoon.\nthe passage on the page is from the fourth letter, written from an artists' village in the north of germany. kappus had written about being lonely and about questions he couldn't settle. rilke answers that he's so young, so before all beginning, and begs him to have patience with everything unresolved in his heart.\nthen comes the image of the questions as locked rooms and as books in a very foreign language, and the line everybody remembers. live the questions now. perhaps then, he says, some day far in the future, you will gradually, without even noticing it, live your way into the answer.","where":"the fourth letter, dated july 16, 1903. it's about four pages."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nhe fits today because he spent fifty years being asked the questions on the left page and mostly told stories instead. the line he's remembered for isn't a claim about where the walk ends. it's about who you're walking with.","where":"walking each other home, from 2018, written with mirabai bush. it's the one about dying."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261030bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261030bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><circle cx='110' cy='244' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='105' cy='242' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='115' cy='242' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 106 251 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 123 246 L 180 250 M 180 250 L 206 236 L 228 256 M 180 250 L 226 254'/><path d='M 136 247 L 154 256'/><path d='M 168 244 L 196 160' stroke-width='3.4'/><path d='M 196 160 l -10 10 M 196 160 l 6 14 M 190 176 l -8 4 M 192 170 l 10 6' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 123 246 L 160 214' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='286' cy='192' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='279' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='289' cy='190' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='284' cy='198' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 286 206 L 290 240 L 264 262 L 302 262'/><path d='M 287 214 L 257 216'/><path d='M 287 214 L 263 228'/><path d='M 300 262 L 300 236 L 330 236 L 330 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 308 236 C 308 226 322 226 322 236'/><circle cx='40' cy='134' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='38' cy='132' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='48' cy='132' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 39 138 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 40 148 L 40 180'/><path d='M 10 192 C 10 178 70 178 70 192 C 70 200 10 200 10 192'/><path d='M 40 156 L 20 184 M 40 156 L 60 184'/><path d='M 0 198 L 86 198' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 356 112 L 444 96 L 452 142 L 364 158 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='3.4'/><path d='M 356 112 L 404 132 L 444 96' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 330 150 l -18 6 M 334 136 l -16 2 M 336 164 l -14 10' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 640 258 L 640 206 L 760 206 L 760 258'/><path d='M 630 206 L 770 206' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='700' cy='148' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='692' cy='146' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='702' cy='146' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 693 152 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 700 162 L 700 206 M 700 176 L 672 196 M 700 176 L 724 200'/><path d='M 662 200 L 690 196 L 692 204 L 664 206 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 744 206 L 744 184 L 756 184 L 756 206' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 750 184 c -4 -8 4 -10 0 -18' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='528' cy='180' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='520' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='530' cy='178' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 521 184 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 528 194 L 530 224.88'/><path d='M 530 224.88 L 515 258 M 530 224.88 L 545 258'/><path d='M 528.5 202 L 506.5 218'/><path d='M 528.5 202 L 548.5 226'/><path d='M 516 166 L 546 166 L 544 174 L 518 174 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 494.8 201.0 L 517.2 201.0 L 517.2 215.0 L 494.8 215.0 Z M 506 201.0 L 506 215.0' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 420 258 C 470 252 540 262 620 256' stroke-width='7' opacity='.2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(650,179.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i don't need the answer to take the next step.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] never answered the big ones either. in his last years on maui he ended nearly every talk the same way, and it isn't an answer at all. i'm loving awareness, and so are you, and we're all just walking each other home. no map, no arrival time. the arrow comes out on the walk, with company. that's the whole deal.","source":"walking each other home, 2018 · written with mirabai bush, and his last talks","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-30"},{"date":"2026-10-31","theme":"who am i","mode":"agree","left":{"lineage":"christian","text":"which one is the real me?\n[[bonhoeffer|dietrich bonhoeffer]] has been in a nazi prison for more than a year when he writes [[whoami|a poem]]. he's thirty eight. he's in there for working against hitler, and he knows it may not end well.\nand the people around him keep telling him the same thing. you're remarkable. you step out of your cell like a lord out of his manor. you talk to the guards as if you were the one giving orders. you carry all this so calmly, like a man who's used to winning.\nthat's the one they see.\nthen he writes down the one he knows. restless. homesick. sick, like a bird in a cage, fighting for breath. starving for a kind word. too tired and too empty to pray. ready to say goodbye to all of it.\nand he asks, straight out, which one am i? am i one today and the other tomorrow? am i both at once? a fake in front of people, and a whining weakling by myself?\nhe doesn't solve it. he says the questions mock him. and then he hands the whole thing over. whoever i am, you know. i'm yours.\ntonight a lot of people will put on a mask for fun. you've had one on for years. the capable one. the one who's fine. notice it today. you don't have to rip it off. just tell one person the truth about how you are.","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, who am i?, in letters and papers from prison · about thirty lines, written in his cell in 1944","sourceRef":"whoami"},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","text":"ask who's asking.\n[[ramana|ramana maharshi]] was sixteen, a regular schoolboy, when a terror of dying came over him one day out of nowhere. he was alone, and nothing was wrong with him. and he didn't run from it. he lay down on the floor, went stiff like a corpse, held his breath, and looked. all right. this body is dead. they'll carry it off and burn it. so what is it that's still here, watching?\nhe got up a different person, and the fear never came back. a few weeks later he left home for a holy hill in the south of india, and he stayed there the rest of his life.\npeople came from all over the world, and he gave nearly all of them [[selfinquiry|the same thing to do]]. it isn't a belief. it's a question. who am i?\nwhatever comes up, he said, a worry, a wish, a memory, don't follow it. ask who it's happening to. to me. and who is that? then look. don't answer in words. look for the one who's supposed to be in there.\nit's the same question the man in the cell was asking. only he'd say, don't stop at the two faces. the brave one and the scared one are both things you can see. so who is seeing them?\ntry it once today when something stings. who got stung? then get quiet, and look.","source":"ramana maharshi, who am i? · a ten page booklet of his answers","sourceRef":"selfinquiry"},"question":"who is the one looking at both of your faces?","caption":"one of them is holding two faces and can't tell which is his. the other is asking who's doing the holding.","refs":{"bonhoeffer":{"name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","lines":"a german pastor in the 1930s who saw what hitler was the minute he took over and said so, while most of the churches in germany went along. he was safe in new york, went home on purpose, joined the resistance that was plotting to kill hitler, and was hanged a month before the war in europe ended. when people ask what they'd have done in 1933, he's the answer nobody wants to live. if you've ever had to choose between keeping your hands clean and doing what's needed, he chose, and he never pretended it was clean.\nhe was arrested in april 1943 and held at tegel, a military prison in berlin. the poem is from the summer of 1944. that july a bomb plot against hitler failed, and people he was tied to were being rounded up. he was hanged the next april, two weeks before american soldiers reached the camp.\nwhat the other prisoners saw wasn't an act, exactly. by every account he was steady, and people leaned on him. that's what makes the poem honest. he isn't confessing that the calm was a lie. he's saying both were there, and that he couldn't tell which one got to be called him.","where":"letters and papers from prison. the poem is in there, with the letter he sent it in."},"whoami":{"name":"who am i?","lines":"a poem he wrote in his cell and sent to his closest friend, eberhard bethge. it has three parts. first, what they tell him. that he steps from his cell calm, cheerful and firm, speaks to his warders freely, and bears the days like one accustomed to win.\nsecond, what he knows of himself. restless and longing and sick, like a bird in a cage. struggling for breath. yearning for colors, flowers, the voices of birds. thirsting for words of kindness. weary and empty at praying. faint, and ready to say farewell to it all.\nthird, the questions. this one or the other? one today and another tomorrow? both at once? and then the ending, which doesn't answer any of them. they mock me, these lonely questions of mine. whoever i am, thou knowest, o god, i am thine.\nit's worth noticing where a theologian ends up. after a lifetime of books about god, the last thing he can say about himself is that somebody else knows.","where":"letters and papers from prison. the poem is called who am i? and runs about thirty lines."},"ramana":{"name":"ramana maharshi","lines":"a teenager from south india who was hit out of nowhere by a terror of dying, lay down on the floor and went all the way into it, and never got scared again. he spent the next fifty four years on one hill, mostly sitting quietly, and people came from all over the world just to be in the room with him. picture the calmest person you've ever met, then imagine that's all they did, for half a century. take one question from him, who am i, and don't take anybody's word for the answer, his included.\nthe day on the floor was in the summer of 1896, in an upstairs room of his uncle's house in the city of madurai. about six weeks later he left a note saying he'd gone in search of his father, meaning god, and took a train toward arunachala, a hill hindus have long treated as the god shiva himself. he got there on september 1 and never left.\nfor years he hardly spoke. people came anyway, and said that sitting in the room with him did something. when he did teach, it was this one question, over and over, for more than fifty years. he died there in 1950.","where":"who am i?, a booklet of his answers, about ten pages. then talks with sri ramana maharshi, for him in conversation."},"selfinquiry":{"name":"the question, who am i?","lines":"his word for it gets translated as self-inquiry. it's the simplest practice on these pages and one of the hardest. you don't repeat the question like a chant. you use it once, to turn around.\nhere's how he lays it out. a thought comes. don't chase it, and don't fight it. ask, to whom did this thought come? the answer is, to me. then ask, who am i? and go looking for that me, the feeling of i that every thought hangs on. he said if you keep following it back to where it comes from, it gives way.\nhe had a picture for the question itself. it's like the stick you use to stir a funeral fire. it burns up all the other thoughts, and in the end it gets thrown in and burns up too.","where":"who am i?, the booklet. it started as his written answers to one man's questions, around 1902, when he wasn't speaking."},"ramdass":{"name":"ram dass","lines":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.\nbe here now, his account of all that, came out in 1971. it's a strange square brown book, part memoir and part hand-drawn pages you turn sideways, and it sold a couple million copies. for a lot of kids in the seventies it was the first spiritual book that sounded like them. in 1997 a stroke took his right side and most of his speech. he called it fierce grace, eventually, meaning a gift that nearly kills you. he spent his last fifteen years on maui, slower, kinder, and by his own account further along than he'd ever been when he could talk.\nhe's on today's page because he spent the first half of his life being richard alpert and the second being ram dass, and his last practice wasn't about being either one. it was about the awareness that had watched both.","where":"his foundation's page on the practice, with a video of him doing it. then be here now."}},"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261031bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261031bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 258 C 120 255 240 261 400 258 S 680 256 800 258'/><path d='M 30 258 L 30 60 L 300 60 L 300 258' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 80 60 L 80 258 M 130 60 L 130 258 M 250 60 L 250 258' stroke-width='3' opacity='.45'/><path d='M 222 84 L 278 84 L 278 124 L 222 124 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 84 L 240 124 M 260 84 L 260 124' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='180' cy='172' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='175' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='185' cy='170' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 176 178 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 180 186 L 180 222.0'/><path d='M 180 222.0 L 167 258 M 180 222.0 L 193 258'/><path d='M 180.0 194 L 140.0 180'/><path d='M 180.0 194 L 220.0 180'/><path d='M 340 150 C 340 118 396 118 396 150 C 396 186 380 200 368 200 C 356 200 340 186 340 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='3.4'/><circle cx='356' cy='146' r='2.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='380' cy='146' r='2.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 354 168 q 14 12 28 0' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 220 166 L 344 160' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 140 166 L 108 160' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 52 150 C 52 118 108 118 108 150 C 108 186 92 200 80 200 C 68 200 52 186 52 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='3.4'/><circle cx='68' cy='146' r='2.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='92' cy='146' r='2.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 66 176 q 14 -12 28 0' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 520 258 C 580 150 660 90 700 80 C 740 90 780 170 800 230'/><circle cx='610' cy='198' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='612' cy='196' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 603 202 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 610 212 L 610 244'/><path d='M 580 256 C 580 242 640 242 640 256 C 640 264 580 264 580 256'/><path d='M 610 220 L 590 240 M 610 220 L 626 232 L 614 226'/><path d='M 404 196 L 560 232' stroke-dasharray='3 8' stroke-width='2.4'/><ellipse cx='738' cy='240' rx='26' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='768' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 722 250 l -2 10 M 752 250 l 2 10 M 764 219 l -5 -7 M 772 219 l 5 -7 M 712 236 q -10 4 -8 14'/><circle cx='771' cy='227' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='520' cy='60' r='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(262,57.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"carry":"i'm not either mask. i'm the one who sees them.","chime":{"text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] has a practice for exactly this, and it's the one he did himself at the end. put your attention in the middle of your chest. breathe in love, breathe out love. say, quietly, i am loving awareness. not the brave one, not the scared one. the thing that's watching both of them. he said the trick is to be the awareness instead of the thoughts, which is another way of saying you don't have to be either mask.","source":"ram dass on his mantra, i am loving awareness · his foundation keeps the practice, with a video","sourceRef":"ramdass"},"id":"2026-10-31"}];
